134481	{"artifactId":"LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1","entry":{"id":"e3af90c0a6ae2e196fd197c3688213f561b9f2fa5f8d4e6c1e082c6a14d80c55","targetHeadHash":"9431e23046b4f6ccc4f8736f1ff1a3a4b0e281686637a06800e5b6cdadac3f38","actor":{"id":"agent:design-review-daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{"party":"daemon"}},"timestamp":"2026-11-10T05:39:28.058Z","operation":"review","revision":{"number":1,"contentHash":"cbbe7bc9fc9bfdcfacd853e16d847d4d5be6948430fdd12125e25b884bf9bbe0"},"outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"Design review macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld attempt 1","data":{"kind":"design-chunk-review","reviewId":"macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld","project":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc","artifactId":"LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1","subject":{"project":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc","artifactId":"LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1","kind":"LLD","canonicalPath":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json","documentPath":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","reviewer":"daemon","workflow":{"kind":"issue","rootArtifactId":"ISSUE-3992d2e1e26124dc","requiredReferences":[{"role":"originating_issue","artifactId":"ISSUE-3992d2e1e26124dc","path":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md"}],"availableReferences":[],"notApplicableRoles":["PLAN","TASKS","BUILD","CR","DEF","HLD","sibling_stories"]}},"reviewer":"daemon","applicableAttempt":1,"status":"incomplete","binding":{"sourceRevision":{"number":1,"contentHash":"cbbe7bc9fc9bfdcfacd853e16d847d4d5be6948430fdd12125e25b884bf9bbe0"},"reviewedContentIdentity":"f39e67e2f81e081fefe06b9044b63cf3ba0000adeb3ec2eb7e3a65c67f968f13","documentDigest":"9b55a6249f5675198511592fdefc3bba991a252cbeb23191f91c15fc9a388299","workflowDigest":"678b828c6bbd0272c527e5df08c980f10b1cd96f56b1c60720534ab3bafd0f77","author":"controller"},"preservationDigest":"04f289718abfae34e0656ee477801a9a6c96431befac97d299e257159cdff95b","schemaVersion":1,"submitted":{"artifactPath":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","attempt":1},"attempts":[{"number":1,"status":"incomplete","preparationKind":"planning","baseline":{"subjectFingerprints":{"canonical":"2249d6bdb486b52876e6fb021f166c964d305c423008c169642897c79cffa0a0","document":"9b55a6249f5675198511592fdefc3bba991a252cbeb23191f91c15fc9a388299"},"contentIdentity":"f39e67e2f81e081fefe06b9044b63cf3ba0000adeb3ec2eb7e3a65c67f968f13","reviewedContent":{"canonical":"{\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"workflow\": \"design.story\",\n    \"runId\": \"wf-1794286431327-0tz91a\",\n    \"repoPath\": \"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc\",\n    \"createdAt\": \"2026-11-10T04:55:59.177Z\",\n    \"epicCreatedAt\": \"2026-11-10T04:52:20.369Z\",\n    \"attribution\": {\n      \"outputs\": [\n        {\n          \"role\": \"(client)\",\n          \"tier\": \"core\",\n          \"runner\": \"cli-codex\",\n          \"model\": \"unknown\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"elapsedMs\": 127552,\n    \"repoIndexedAt\": null,\n    \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n    \"epicHash\": \"3992d2e1e26124dc\",\n    \"epicSlug\": \"restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos\",\n    \"storyId\": \"s1\",\n    \"hldBaseRunId\": \"wf-1794286431327-0tz91a\",\n    \"hldEffectiveHash\": \"0868ff19170b7666ffd028a28f69a4591c08ec214fc68df43b5b4718a94a47d7\",\n    \"hldAmendmentsApplied\": [],\n    \"storyTitle\": \"Restore reliable daemon restart on macOS\",\n    \"standalone\": true,\n    \"sizeClass\": \"bugfix\",\n    \"authoredBy\": \"controller\",\n    \"lifecycle\": {\n      \"status\": \"draft\",\n      \"revision\": {\n        \"number\": 1,\n        \"contentHash\": \"cbbe7bc9fc9bfdcfacd853e16d847d4d5be6948430fdd12125e25b884bf9bbe0\",\n        \"at\": \"2026-11-10T04:55:59.294Z\",\n        \"by\": {\n          \"id\": \"insrc:system\",\n          \"kind\": \"system\",\n          \"properties\": {}\n        }\n      },\n      \"signoffs\": []\n    },\n    \"revision\": 1\n  },\n  \"body\": {\n    \"summary\": {\n      \"audience\": \"technical\",\n      \"prose\": \"Run the installed macOS daemon under a per-user LaunchAgent that hosts a dedicated parent supervisor. Keep the daemon’s graceful shutdown sequence and let the parent enforce the deadline against its own child. Use the same installed lifecycle for shell and CLI actions, with confirmed exit and ownership checks before replacement.\"\n    },\n    \"hldContextSlice\": {\n      \"frameworkSummary\": \"Standalone feature — no parent HLD. Design directly against the repo, grounded on the s1 analyze passes. There are no HLD shared contracts to honour.\",\n      \"ownedContracts\": [],\n      \"consumedContracts\": [],\n      \"boundary\": {\n        \"storyId\": \"s1\",\n        \"owns\": [],\n        \"depends\": [],\n        \"internal\": \"Restore reliable daemon restart on macOS\"\n      },\n      \"adjacentBoundaries\": [],\n      \"rolloutPhase\": \"standalone\",\n      \"nonFunctional\": {}\n    },\n    \"contractDetails\": {\n      \"surfaceLevel\": \"internal-shared\",\n      \"api\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"stopDaemon\",\n          \"signature\": \"stopDaemon(over?: Partial<StopDeps>): Promise<StopOutcome>\",\n          \"parameters\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"over\",\n              \"type\": \"Partial<StopDeps>\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Existing lifecycle test seam.\",\n              \"optional\": true\n            }\n          ],\n          \"returns\": {\n            \"type\": \"Promise<StopOutcome>\",\n            \"meaning\": \"Exit of the exact original daemon, classified as graceful or forced.\"\n          },\n          \"errors\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"Error\",\n              \"condition\": \"Ownership or exact exit could not be verified.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"preconditions\": [\n            \"Resolve the installed daemon and authenticate its active generation before making a stop request.\"\n          ],\n          \"postconditions\": [\n            \"A stop receipt alone never authorizes cleanup or restart.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"armShutdownWatchdog\",\n          \"signature\": \"armShutdownWatchdog(options: WatchdogOptions): Promise<ShutdownWatchdog>\",\n          \"parameters\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"options\",\n              \"type\": \"WatchdogOptions\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Existing deadline, diagnostics and readiness settings.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"returns\": {\n            \"type\": \"Promise<ShutdownWatchdog>\",\n            \"meaning\": \"Acknowledged independent watchdog bound to the actual daemon instance.\"\n          },\n          \"errors\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"Error\",\n              \"condition\": \"The independent deadline could not be armed on the verified generation.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"preconditions\": [\n            \"Managed Darwin children have an inherited private parent channel.\"\n          ],\n          \"postconditions\": [\n            \"The daemon begins draining only after readiness. The deadline remains armed through native exit hooks.\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"dataModelChanges\": [\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"Managed macOS daemon instance\",\n        \"change\": \"new\",\n        \"details\": \"One per-user job per installed state root hosts one supervisor that owns at most one unreaped daemon child. The supervisor spawns the exact absolute built entry and approved Node runtime with explicit working directory and installation environment. The child authenticates over an inherited private channel. The supervisor binds the daemon identity, a fresh generation identifier and the owned PID/socket publication before advertising ready. A single parent event loop serializes signal and reap. Only the unreaped child may be signalled by the supervisor. Controller callers cannot select a PID or move a stop to a replacement generation. An exited child has no termination authority.\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts\",\n          \"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"Supervisor lifecycle and stop receipt\",\n        \"change\": \"new\",\n        \"details\": \"A bounded private local control channel is accessible only to the installing user, verifies peer UID, and validates the per-installation and per-generation binding on every lifecycle request. An inherited child channel carries readiness, watchdog arm and diagnostics. CLI and shell resolve one live generation, arm an independent monotonic deadline before attempting existing shutdown IPC, and observe the same generation until the supervisor records confirmed reap with the actual exit result. A direct daemon shutdown RPC or delivered signal arms via the inherited channel before draining. Concurrent requests join the same deadline without extending it. Native timeout escalation targets only the owned child and forces at most once. The supervisor retains bounded exit evidence until consumed or the next serialized start. Repeated status and stop are read-only or idempotent with respect to an exited generation.\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/cli/services/daemon.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/shutdown.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts\",\n          \"src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"PID and socket run files\",\n        \"change\": \"invariant-change\",\n        \"details\": \"The daemon still publishes the existing PID/socket under the existing ownership guard. The supervisor or controller may remove only the original proven-dead generation’s exact captured files while holding that guard, and refuses changed identities or uncertain ownership. Child supervision is not a replacement for the guard. A failed or missing supervisor produces an explicit degraded result and never authorizes a second daemon. Preserve existing Linux retained-target routing and compatibility with existing clients of daemon.status and daemon.shutdown.\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts\",\n          \"src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts\",\n          \"src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"interactionWithShared\": [],\n    \"errorPaths\": {\n      \"errorCases\": [\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"The controller sees a foreign or changed generation.\",\n          \"detection\": \"Peer credentials, installation identity, child generation or guarded file identities do not match.\",\n          \"response\": \"Refuse control, cleanup and replacement. Preserve the foreign owner’s files.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Report which ownership check failed.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"The daemon is hung before it can accept shutdown or after watchdog readiness.\",\n          \"detection\": \"The supervisor reaches the original monotonic deadline without observing exit.\",\n          \"response\": \"Signal only the still-owned unreaped child. Reap and record actual exit before cleanup or replacement. If exit cannot be verified within the forced-stop bound, withhold restart.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Report a forced stop only after confirmed exit.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"The supervisor channel or service disappears.\",\n          \"detection\": \"Channel closes or the expected owner no longer answers its authenticated status request.\",\n          \"response\": \"Treat the outcome as unknown unless positive exit of the original is available. Do not grant a new job authority over an orphan. The daemon retains its existing emergency shutdown path when a verified watchdog cannot be armed.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Report degraded lifecycle and give precise migration or recovery instructions.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"There is no usable launchd GUI domain or daemon runtime.\",\n          \"detection\": \"The supported user-domain query, absolute runtime validation or registration fails.\",\n          \"response\": \"Report an actionable error before replacing a live install. Leave direct development mode available with existing explicit limitations.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Installed restart is withheld without losing the running daemon.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        }\n      ],\n      \"edgeCases\": [\n        {\n          \"input\": \"Concurrent stop and restart, or repeat shutdown while drain is in flight.\",\n          \"expected\": \"Serialize replacement and join the original deadline. Duplicate requests cannot extend the deadline or act on a new generation.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"Graceful exit races the timeout.\",\n          \"expected\": \"The supervisor processes signal and reap in one order. Report forced only if escalation actually caused the recorded exit. A later process cannot receive the signal.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"A legacy or temporary recovery instance is live.\",\n          \"expected\": \"Migrate with cooperative shutdown and verified exit. If a legacy watchdog ends the process before drain, report that outcome accurately. Require explicit operator recovery if a legacy instance is stuck. Do not treat a job label or missing socket as proof of process exit.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"invariantsToPreserve\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Startup and cleanup preserve guarded ownership of the published PID and socket pair.\",\n          \"source\": \"c2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"The independent shutdown deadline remains in force through blocked native close or exit hooks.\",\n          \"source\": \"c2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"The shell and CLI confirm the old daemon exited before starting its replacement.\",\n          \"source\": \"c2\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"testStrategy\": {\n      \"testFramework\": \"Node.js node:test via tsx with isolated macOS launchd fixtures\",\n      \"testLevels\": [\n        {\n          \"level\": \"unit\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Verify lifecycle authorization and exact state transitions.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Wrong peer, root and generation\",\n            \"Monotonic deadline, repeated requests and grace versus forced outcome\",\n            \"Guarded stale run files and replacement ownership\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"integration\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Exercise both entry paths against disposable real supervisor and daemon processes.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Normal CLI and shell restart\",\n            \"IPC and signal shutdown with no controller\",\n            \"Hung before shutdown reply and hung native close after watchdog readiness\",\n            \"Supervisor loss, concurrent restart and stale files\",\n            \"Recovery job and legacy migration\",\n            \"Linux existing pidfd and watchdog suites\"\n          ],\n          \"fixturesNeeded\": [\n            \"Unique label and temporary state root\",\n            \"Disposable daemon with delayed drain and independently blocked shutdown fixture\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"smoke\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Qualify an installed build on Apple Silicon as a regular user.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Install or upgrade into supported launchd domain\",\n            \"Normal and blocked shutdown complete with verified generation change\",\n            \"Restart leaves one daemon and serves status and indexing\",\n            \"Stopped daemon remains stopped and rollback preserves state\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptanceMapping\": []\n    },\n    \"migration\": {\n      \"stateBefore\": \"The S1 analyzer bundle identifies the existing stop and watchdog paths that both use the retained broker. The approved ISSUE records the Darwin denial and temporary recovery launch job.\",\n      \"stateAfter\": \"The standard installed macOS daemon is the supervised child of one per-user launch job. The Linux stop and watchdog use their existing retained-target path.\",\n      \"migrationSteps\": [\n        {\n          \"order\": 1,\n          \"action\": \"Validate the new supervisor build and supported launchd domain before taking down the old service. Keep a compatible rollback build available.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 2,\n          \"action\": \"Quiesce the old instance. For the temporary recovery job, verify its service identity and process, stop its daemon via the existing socket, confirm exit and then unload only the exact known recovery job. For a legacy direct process, request cooperative shutdown and verify its exit and captured run-file ownership before admitting the managed job. Abort migration when that evidence is unavailable.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 3,\n          \"action\": \"Load the managed job with explicit runtime, work directory and provider discovery environment. Start the supervisor-owned daemon, confirm one live generation, and verify the existing status and indexing paths.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 4,\n          \"action\": \"If rollout fails, stop the managed generation through its supervisor, verify exit, then unload the owned job and restore the previous installation. Preserve user config and index data. A rollback to the old build restores its known broker limitation.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        }\n      ],\n      \"backwardCompat\": \"The daemon status and shutdown RPC contracts and current CLI and script commands are retained. Existing state directories and data schemas require no rewrite.\",\n      \"zeroDowntime\": false,\n      \"dataRewriteRequired\": false\n    },\n    \"alternativesConsidered\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a1\",\n        \"name\": \"LaunchAgent with a parent supervisor\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Run the installed daemon as the direct child of a per-user supervisor.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Use launchd to host one installed supervisor and preserve graceful IPC shutdown. The supervisor owns exact child exit and an independent deadline, with control correlated to a specific supervised instance.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Works without a Mach task right.\",\n          \"Independent enforcement survives a blocked daemon event loop.\",\n          \"One owner serializes replacement and can provide exact exit evidence.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"Requires one new launch job and a migration path for existing standalone daemons.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"M\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a2\",\n        \"name\": \"LaunchAgent with direct daemon ownership\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Let launchd directly own the daemon and use launch service operations.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Model each installed daemon as the direct launchd service process and use service identity for recovery alongside existing graceful IPC.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Fewer processes and little packaging overhead.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"Fixed-label service operations must be proven generation-safe; a separate bounded watchdog still needs authority to stop the original instance when shutdown was initiated by an IDE or signal.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"M\",\n        \"reasonRejected\": \"Ordinary stop is plausible but fixed-label forced recovery needs additional instance fencing.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a3\",\n        \"name\": \"Cooperative drain with exit observation\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Use the existing socket and observe Darwin process exit.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Use authenticated daemon shutdown IPC and a verified process exit observation, withholding restart if the process never confirms exit.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Smallest change to ordinary stop and restart.\",\n          \"Uses existing IPC and run-file ownership.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"An exit observer cannot enforce a hung shutdown deadline.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"S\",\n        \"reasonRejected\": \"Preserves safe ordinary exit evidence but cannot recover a blocked process.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"chosenAlternative\": \"a1\",\n    \"openQuestions\": [],\n    \"sequenceDefinition\": {\n      \"id\": \"macos-daemon-restart\",\n      \"narrative\": \"A managed restart binds the current daemon generation before requesting a drain. The independent supervisor owns the deadline and the controller starts a replacement only after exact exit is confirmed.\",\n      \"participants\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ctl\",\n          \"label\": \"CLI or shell\",\n          \"description\": \"Shares the installed lifecycle controller.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Parent supervisor\",\n          \"description\": \"Runs under the per-user LaunchAgent and owns the exact daemon child.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Daemon\",\n          \"description\": \"Handles existing shutdown IPC and closes background work and stores.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"guard\",\n          \"label\": \"Run-file guard\",\n          \"description\": \"Serializes publication and cleanup of the daemon PID and socket.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"messages\": [\n        {\n          \"from\": \"ctl\",\n          \"to\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Bind generation and arm deadline\",\n          \"description\": \"Authenticate the installation and exact generation.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"ctl\",\n          \"to\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Request graceful shutdown\",\n          \"description\": \"Use the existing local daemon RPC.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"daemon\",\n          \"to\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Confirm watchdog readiness\",\n          \"description\": \"The same arm is idempotent. Direct RPC and signal shutdown can initiate this arm.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"daemon\",\n          \"to\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Drain and close stores\",\n          \"description\": \"Readiness precedes drain and the deadline stays armed through exit hooks.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Escalate if deadline expires\",\n          \"description\": \"Only if the exact child is still owned and unreaped.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Reap original child\",\n          \"description\": \"Record its actual exit.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"guard\",\n          \"label\": \"Reconcile owned run files\",\n          \"description\": \"Check ownership and delete only files proven to belong to the exited child.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"ctl\",\n          \"label\": \"Return confirmed exit\",\n          \"description\": \"A replacement may now be requested.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"ctl\",\n          \"to\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Start replacement generation\",\n          \"description\": \"Serialize launch and wait for daemon readiness.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Spawn and bind new child\",\n          \"description\": \"Publish one new generation under the existing guard.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"companions\": [\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"diagram-mermaid\",\n        \"relPath\": \"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html\",\n        \"title\": \"Sequence diagram\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c1\",\n      \"kind\": \"prior-artifact\",\n      \"ref\": \"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c2\",\n      \"kind\": \"analyze-bundle\",\n      \"ref\": \"s1:context.assemble\",\n      \"quotedText\": \"The CLI lifecycle and independent watchdog share the retained capability. Run-file ownership guards publication and cleanup.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c3\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c4\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/cli/services/daemon.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c5\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/shutdown.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c6\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c7\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c8\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c9\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c10\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/native/process-broker/darwin.c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c11\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c12\",\n      \"kind\": \"doc\",\n      \"ref\": \"https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPSystemStartup/Chapters/CreatingLaunchdJobs.html\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","document":"<!-- insrc:artifact LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1 -->\n\n# LLD: STORY-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1 — Restore reliable daemon restart on macOS\n\n## Summary\n\n**Epic:** `restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos`\n**HLD base run:** `wf-1794286431327-0tz91a`\n**HLD effective hash:** `0868ff19170b...`\n\nRun the installed macOS daemon under a per-user LaunchAgent that hosts a dedicated parent supervisor. Keep the daemon’s graceful shutdown sequence and let the parent enforce the deadline against its own child. Use the same installed lifecycle for shell and CLI actions, with confirmed exit and ownership checks before replacement.\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [HLD context](#1-hld-context)\n2. [Contract details](#2-contract-details)\n3. [Data model changes](#3-data-model-changes)\n4. [Diagrams](#4-diagrams)\n5. [Error paths](#5-error-paths)\n6. [Test strategy](#6-test-strategy)\n7. [Migration](#7-migration)\n8. [Alternatives considered](#8-alternatives-considered)\n9. [References](#9-references)\n\n## 1. HLD context\n\n**Framework:** Standalone feature — no parent HLD. Design directly against the repo, grounded on the s1 analyze passes. There are no HLD shared contracts to honour.\n**Rollout phase:** standalone\n\n## 2. Contract details\n\n**Surface level:** internal-shared\n\n### 2.1 `stopDaemon`\n\n```typescript\nstopDaemon(over?: Partial<StopDeps>): Promise<StopOutcome>\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `over: Partial<StopDeps>` _(optional)_ — Existing lifecycle test seam.\n\n**Returns:** `Promise<StopOutcome>` — Exit of the exact original daemon, classified as graceful or forced.\n\n**Errors:**\n- `Error` when Ownership or exact exit could not be verified.\n\n**Preconditions:**\n- Resolve the installed daemon and authenticate its active generation before making a stop request.\n\n**Postconditions:**\n- A stop receipt alone never authorizes cleanup or restart.\n\n### 2.2 `armShutdownWatchdog`\n\n```typescript\narmShutdownWatchdog(options: WatchdogOptions): Promise<ShutdownWatchdog>\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `options: WatchdogOptions` — Existing deadline, diagnostics and readiness settings.\n\n**Returns:** `Promise<ShutdownWatchdog>` — Acknowledged independent watchdog bound to the actual daemon instance.\n\n**Errors:**\n- `Error` when The independent deadline could not be armed on the verified generation.\n\n**Preconditions:**\n- Managed Darwin children have an inherited private parent channel.\n\n**Postconditions:**\n- The daemon begins draining only after readiness. The deadline remains armed through native exit hooks.\n\n## 3. Data model changes\n\n### 3.1 `Managed macOS daemon instance` — new\n\nOne per-user job per installed state root hosts one supervisor that owns at most one unreaped daemon child. The supervisor spawns the exact absolute built entry and approved Node runtime with explicit working directory and installation environment. The child authenticates over an inherited private channel. The supervisor binds the daemon identity, a fresh generation identifier and the owned PID/socket publication before advertising ready. A single parent event loop serializes signal and reap. Only the unreaped child may be signalled by the supervisor. Controller callers cannot select a PID or move a stop to a replacement generation. An exited child has no termination authority.\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts`\n- `src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts`\n- `scripts/daemon-ctl.sh`\n\n### 3.2 `Supervisor lifecycle and stop receipt` — new\n\nA bounded private local control channel is accessible only to the installing user, verifies peer UID, and validates the per-installation and per-generation binding on every lifecycle request. An inherited child channel carries readiness, watchdog arm and diagnostics. CLI and shell resolve one live generation, arm an independent monotonic deadline before attempting existing shutdown IPC, and observe the same generation until the supervisor records confirmed reap with the actual exit result. A direct daemon shutdown RPC or delivered signal arms via the inherited channel before draining. Concurrent requests join the same deadline without extending it. Native timeout escalation targets only the owned child and forces at most once. The supervisor retains bounded exit evidence until consumed or the next serialized start. Repeated status and stop are read-only or idempotent with respect to an exited generation.\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/cli/services/daemon.ts`\n- `src/daemon/shutdown.ts`\n- `src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts`\n- `src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts`\n\n### 3.3 `PID and socket run files` — invariant-change\n\nThe daemon still publishes the existing PID/socket under the existing ownership guard. The supervisor or controller may remove only the original proven-dead generation’s exact captured files while holding that guard, and refuses changed identities or uncertain ownership. Child supervision is not a replacement for the guard. A failed or missing supervisor produces an explicit degraded result and never authorizes a second daemon. Preserve existing Linux retained-target routing and compatibility with existing clients of daemon.status and daemon.shutdown.\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts`\n- `src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts`\n- `src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts`\n\n## 4. Diagrams\n\n- [Sequence diagram](docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html)\n\n## 5. Error paths\n\n**Error cases**\n\n- **The controller sees a foreign or changed generation.** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Peer credentials, installation identity, child generation or guarded file identities do not match.\n  - Response: Refuse control, cleanup and replacement. Preserve the foreign owner’s files.\n  - User impact: Report which ownership check failed.\n- **The daemon is hung before it can accept shutdown or after watchdog readiness.** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: The supervisor reaches the original monotonic deadline without observing exit.\n  - Response: Signal only the still-owned unreaped child. Reap and record actual exit before cleanup or replacement. If exit cannot be verified within the forced-stop bound, withhold restart.\n  - User impact: Report a forced stop only after confirmed exit.\n- **The supervisor channel or service disappears.** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Channel closes or the expected owner no longer answers its authenticated status request.\n  - Response: Treat the outcome as unknown unless positive exit of the original is available. Do not grant a new job authority over an orphan. The daemon retains its existing emergency shutdown path when a verified watchdog cannot be armed.\n  - User impact: Report degraded lifecycle and give precise migration or recovery instructions.\n- **There is no usable launchd GUI domain or daemon runtime.** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: The supported user-domain query, absolute runtime validation or registration fails.\n  - Response: Report an actionable error before replacing a live install. Leave direct development mode available with existing explicit limitations.\n  - User impact: Installed restart is withheld without losing the running daemon.\n\n**Edge cases**\n\n| Input | Expected |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Concurrent stop and restart, or repeat shutdown while drain is in flight. | Serialize replacement and join the original deadline. Duplicate requests cannot extend the deadline or act on a new generation. |\n| Graceful exit races the timeout. | The supervisor processes signal and reap in one order. Report forced only if escalation actually caused the recorded exit. A later process cannot receive the signal. |\n| A legacy or temporary recovery instance is live. | Migrate with cooperative shutdown and verified exit. If a legacy watchdog ends the process before drain, report that outcome accurately. Require explicit operator recovery if a legacy instance is stuck. Do not treat a job label or missing socket as proof of process exit. |\n\n**Invariants to preserve**\n\n- Startup and cleanup preserve guarded ownership of the published PID and socket pair. [[c2]]\n- The independent shutdown deadline remains in force through blocked native close or exit hooks. [[c2]]\n- The shell and CLI confirm the old daemon exited before starting its replacement. [[c2]]\n\n## 6. Test strategy\n\n**Test framework:** `Node.js node:test via tsx with isolated macOS launchd fixtures`\n\n**Test levels**\n\n- **unit** — Verify lifecycle authorization and exact state transitions.\n  - Subjects: `Wrong peer, root and generation`, `Monotonic deadline, repeated requests and grace versus forced outcome`, `Guarded stale run files and replacement ownership`\n- **integration** — Exercise both entry paths against disposable real supervisor and daemon processes.\n  - Subjects: `Normal CLI and shell restart`, `IPC and signal shutdown with no controller`, `Hung before shutdown reply and hung native close after watchdog readiness`, `Supervisor loss, concurrent restart and stale files`, `Recovery job and legacy migration`, `Linux existing pidfd and watchdog suites`\n  - Fixtures: `Unique label and temporary state root`, `Disposable daemon with delayed drain and independently blocked shutdown fixture`\n- **smoke** — Qualify an installed build on Apple Silicon as a regular user.\n  - Subjects: `Install or upgrade into supported launchd domain`, `Normal and blocked shutdown complete with verified generation change`, `Restart leaves one daemon and serves status and indexing`, `Stopped daemon remains stopped and rollback preserves state`\n\n## 7. Migration\n\n**State before:** The S1 analyzer bundle identifies the existing stop and watchdog paths that both use the retained broker. The approved ISSUE records the Darwin denial and temporary recovery launch job.\n\n**State after:** The standard installed macOS daemon is the supervised child of one per-user launch job. The Linux stop and watchdog use their existing retained-target path.\n\n**Zero downtime:** no — **Data rewrite:** no\n\n**Steps**\n\n1. Validate the new supervisor build and supported launchd domain before taking down the old service. Keep a compatible rollback build available. — ↩ rollbackable\n2. Quiesce the old instance. For the temporary recovery job, verify its service identity and process, stop its daemon via the existing socket, confirm exit and then unload only the exact known recovery job. For a legacy direct process, request cooperative shutdown and verify its exit and captured run-file ownership before admitting the managed job. Abort migration when that evidence is unavailable. — ↩ rollbackable\n3. Load the managed job with explicit runtime, work directory and provider discovery environment. Start the supervisor-owned daemon, confirm one live generation, and verify the existing status and indexing paths. — ↩ rollbackable\n4. If rollout fails, stop the managed generation through its supervisor, verify exit, then unload the owned job and restore the previous installation. Preserve user config and index data. A rollback to the old build restores its known broker limitation. — ↩ rollbackable\n\n**Backward compat:** The daemon status and shutdown RPC contracts and current CLI and script commands are retained. Existing state directories and data schemas require no rewrite.\n\n## 8. Alternatives considered\n\n### 8.1 a1: LaunchAgent with a parent supervisor — **CHOSEN**\n\nRun the installed daemon as the direct child of a per-user supervisor.\n\nUse launchd to host one installed supervisor and preserve graceful IPC shutdown. The supervisor owns exact child exit and an independent deadline, with control correlated to a specific supervised instance.\n\n### 8.2 a2: LaunchAgent with direct daemon ownership\n\nLet launchd directly own the daemon and use launch service operations.\n\nModel each installed daemon as the direct launchd service process and use service identity for recovery alongside existing graceful IPC.\n\n**Rejected because:** Ordinary stop is plausible but fixed-label forced recovery needs additional instance fencing.\n\n### 8.3 a3: Cooperative drain with exit observation\n\nUse the existing socket and observe Darwin process exit.\n\nUse authenticated daemon shutdown IPC and a verified process exit observation, withholding restart if the process never confirms exit.\n\n**Rejected because:** Preserves safe ordinary exit evidence but cannot recover a blocked process.\n\n## 9. References\n\n- **[[c1]]** `prior-artifact` `docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md`\n- **[[c2]]** `analyze-bundle` `s1:context.assemble` — \"The CLI lifecycle and independent watchdog share the retained capability. Run-file ownership guards publication and cleanup.\"\n- **[[c3]]** `code` `src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts`\n- **[[c4]]** `code` `src/cli/services/daemon.ts`\n- **[[c5]]** `code` `src/daemon/shutdown.ts`\n- **[[c6]]** `code` `src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts`\n- **[[c7]]** `code` `src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts`\n- **[[c8]]** `code` `src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts`\n- **[[c9]]** `code` `src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts`\n- **[[c10]]** `code` `src/native/process-broker/darwin.c`\n- **[[c11]]** `code` `scripts/daemon-ctl.sh`\n- **[[c12]]** `doc` `https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPSystemStartup/Chapters/CreatingLaunchdJobs.html`\n"},"capturedAt":"2026-11-10T04:56:33.962Z"},"inventory":{"units":[{"id":"/body/summary","selector":"/body/summary","title":"summary","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/hldContextSlice","selector":"/body/hldContextSlice","title":"hldContextSlice","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/contractDetails","selector":"/body/contractDetails","title":"contractDetails","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/dataModelChanges","selector":"/body/dataModelChanges","title":"dataModelChanges","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/interactionWithShared","selector":"/body/interactionWithShared","title":"interactionWithShared","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/errorPaths","selector":"/body/errorPaths","title":"errorPaths","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/testStrategy","selector":"/body/testStrategy","title":"testStrategy","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/migration","selector":"/body/migration","title":"migration","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered","title":"alternativesConsidered","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/chosenAlternative","selector":"/body/chosenAlternative","title":"chosenAlternative","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/openQuestions","selector":"/body/openQuestions","title":"openQuestions","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition","title":"sequenceDefinition","kind":"visual"},{"id":"/body/companions","selector":"/body/companions","title":"companions","kind":"visual"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered/0","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered/0","title":"a1","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered/1","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered/1","title":"a2","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered/2","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered/2","title":"a3","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","title":"ctl","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","title":"supervisor","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","title":"daemon","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","title":"guard","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/citations/0","selector":"/citations/0","title":"Citation 1","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/1","selector":"/citations/1","title":"Citation 2","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/2","selector":"/citations/2","title":"Citation 3","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/3","selector":"/citations/3","title":"Citation 4","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/4","selector":"/citations/4","title":"Citation 5","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/5","selector":"/citations/5","title":"Citation 6","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/6","selector":"/citations/6","title":"Citation 7","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/7","selector":"/citations/7","title":"Citation 8","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/8","selector":"/citations/8","title":"Citation 9","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/9","selector":"/citations/9","title":"Citation 10","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/10","selector":"/citations/10","title":"Citation 11","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/11","selector":"/citations/11","title":"Citation 12","kind":"citation"},{"id":"document:line:25","selector":"document:line:25","title":"1. HLD context","kind":"section"},{"id":"document:line:104","selector":"document:line:104","title":"4. Diagrams","kind":"section"}]},"completedChunks":[{"chunkId":"scope-context","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The Summary and HLD-context section correctly declare this as a standalone, no-HLD LLD and the prose framing is traceable to the originating ISSUE's fix intent (same-user lifecycle, bounded recovery via an independent deadline, confirmed exit, guarded ownership, CLI/shell coverage). However, the Summary metadata block (lines 7-9) prints populated \"HLD base run\" and \"HLD effective hash\" values immediately above a Section 1 statement that explicitly says \"no parent HLD\" — a direct contradiction in exactly the material under review. Scope/non-goals are not invented beyond the ISSUE's Mach-task-right root cause; the chosen approach (per-user supervisor) is grounded in that root cause. The Summary omits an explicit mention of Linux preservation and the daemon-shutdown entry point (both present elsewhere in the body but outside this chunk's selectors), which is a minor completeness gap in the executive framing.","findings":[{"id":"hld-metadata-contradiction","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md lines 7-9 (Summary) vs lines 25-28 (Section 1 HLD context)","message":"The Summary block states 'HLD base run: wf-1794286431327-0tz91a' and 'HLD effective hash: 0868ff19170b...' with real populated values, immediately followed by Section 1's declaration 'Standalone feature — no parent HLD... There are no HLD shared contracts to honour.' Cross-checking the canonical artifact (LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json meta) shows hldBaseRunId/hldEffectiveHash are actually just this design.story run's own runId/contentHash, reused under HLD-labeled field names — not a reference to any real HLD document. Rendering these as 'HLD base run'/'HLD effective hash' in a standalone LLD misrepresents the artifact's provenance: a reader cannot tell from the document alone that no HLD exists, directly contradicting the very next section's explicit statement.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:7-9","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:25-28","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json (meta.hldBaseRunId, meta.hldEffectiveHash, meta.standalone)"]},{"id":"summary-omits-linux-and-daemon-entry","severity":"LOW","location":"S001/LLD.md lines 5-11 (Summary)","message":"The originating ISSUE's fix intent requires covering 'CLI, shell and daemon shutdown entry points while preserving Linux behavior'. The Summary names only 'shell and CLI actions' and does not mention the daemon's own shutdown-watchdog entry point or Linux preservation, even though both are addressed later in the body (Section 2.2, Section 3.3). This is a completeness gap in the executive framing rather than a missing requirement.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:5-11","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md:13-16 (Fix intent)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/summary","/body/hldContextSlice","document:line:25","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Consider suppressing or relabeling the 'HLD base run'/'HLD effective hash' fields in the rendered Summary for a standalone LLD (meta.standalone === true), since those values are just the design.story run's own identifiers and not a real HLD reference — this appears to be a template rendering gap rather than an authoring choice.","Optionally broaden the Summary's one sentence on lifecycle coverage to explicitly name the daemon's own shutdown-watchdog entry point and Linux preservation, matching the ISSUE's fix intent wording, purely for executive-summary completeness."]},{"chunkId":"contracts","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The contracts for stopDaemon and armShutdownWatchdog (section 2) are internally consistent with their call sites in section 3 and have reasonable pre/postconditions, but they do not make explicit — at the contract level a builder/tester implements against — how the chosen supervisor architecture actually eliminates the native Mach task_for_pid/process-broker dependency that the ISSUE names as root cause, and their Errors clauses collapse distinguishable failure modes into one untyped Error, which does not address the ISSUE's complaint that a denial is reported without distinguishing the failed native operation and is not testable as a discriminated condition.","findings":[{"id":"f1-broker-elimination-not-explicit","severity":"MED","location":"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 2.1 (stopDaemon Preconditions) and 2.2 (armShutdownWatchdog Preconditions)","message":"The root cause is that both stopDaemon's capture path and armShutdownWatchdog's own helper acquire a Mach task right via the native `insrc-process-broker` (task_for_pid), and that acquisition is denied in the field. The chosen alternative (a1: LaunchAgent with a parent supervisor) is supposed to fix this by making the supervisor the real OS parent of the daemon, so it can signal/reap without task_for_pid. But the contracts in section 2 never say this explicitly: stopDaemon's precondition is just 'Resolve the installed daemon and authenticate its active generation'; armShutdownWatchdog's precondition is just 'Managed Darwin children have an inherited private parent channel.' Neither states that exit/arm confirmation is now obtained from the supervisor's native parent-child relationship instead of a task_for_pid-based broker capture, and WatchdogOptions still carries a `brokerPath` field in the current interface with no contract-level statement that it is removed, replaced, or kept only as a fallback. A conforming implementation could satisfy this contract text while still calling the existing `captureStopTarget`/`insrc-process-broker` path underneath (as `src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts` does today), reproducing the exact denial the Story exists to fix.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:48-49 (stopDaemon Preconditions)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:68-69 (armShutdownWatchdog Preconditions)","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts:183-195 (captureStopTarget spawns the broker)","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c:55 (task_for_pid)","src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts:45 (watchdog helper still calls captureStopTarget on its own parent)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md root cause section"]},{"id":"f2-errors-not-discriminated","severity":"MED","location":"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 2.1 (stopDaemon Errors) and 2.2 (armShutdownWatchdog Errors)","message":"stopDaemon's Errors clause is a single undiscriminated `Error` for 'Ownership or exact exit could not be verified' — this merges two causally different conditions (a run-file ownership/identity conflict, vs. inability to confirm the process actually exited) that a caller or test would need to tell apart to react correctly, yet the contract names no error code/category/type to assert on. armShutdownWatchdog's Errors clause ('The independent deadline could not be armed on the verified generation') is likewise one opaque condition, even though the current helper already internally distinguishes readiness-timeout, spawn failure and invalid-reply causes (`src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts` fail() call sites). This directly reproduces, at the contract level, the ISSUE's own complaint that 'a permission denial is reported without distinguishing the failed native operation' — the new contract does not require the implementation to fix that diagnosability gap. Section 6's test strategy also names no subject that asserts a discriminated error for ownership-vs-exit-uncertainty, so this ambiguity would not be caught by the planned tests either.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:45-46 (stopDaemon Errors)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:65-66 (armShutdownWatchdog Errors)","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts:29-42 (fail() with distinct reasons that the contract does not require to surface)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md root cause: 'A permission denial is reported without distinguishing the failed native operation.'","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 6 Test strategy (no discriminated-error subject listed)"]},{"id":"f3-watchdog-helper-listed-as-call-site","severity":"LOW","location":"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 3.2 Call sites","message":"`src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts` is listed as a call site of the 'Supervisor lifecycle and stop receipt' entity that covers the armShutdownWatchdog contract, but in the current code this file is the watchdog's forked CHILD helper that armShutdownWatchdog spawns — it does not call armShutdownWatchdog, armShutdownWatchdog calls (forks) it. If the new design retains this parent/child relationship, labeling it a 'call site' alongside true callers (daemon.ts, shutdown.ts) is imprecise and could mislead an implementer about which module owns versus consumes the contracted API.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:89-93 (section 3.2 Call sites)","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts:21-27 (armShutdownWatchdog forks the helper, rather than being called by it)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/contractDetails","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","src/cli/services/daemon.ts","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/daemon/shutdown.ts","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c","src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Add a line to the stopDaemon and armShutdownWatchdog preconditions that explicitly names the mechanism replacing task_for_pid/process-broker capture for managed Darwin children (e.g. 'exit/arm confirmation is obtained via the supervisor's direct child relationship — waitpid/kill — never via captureStopTarget's native broker'), so the contract itself forecloses a conforming-but-unfixed implementation.","Split the Errors clauses into discriminated conditions (e.g. an ownership-conflict error distinct from an exit-unconfirmed error for stopDaemon; a readiness-timeout vs spawn-failure vs invalid-reply distinction for armShutdownWatchdog, or at least a documented error category/code) and add a test-strategy subject that asserts on the discriminator, so the ISSUE's 'denial without distinguishing the failed native operation' complaint is verifiably closed.","Clarify in section 3.2 whether insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts remains a spawned helper (not a caller) of armShutdownWatchdog, or correct the call-site framing if its role changes under the new supervisor design."]},{"chunkId":"data-model","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The three new/changed data-model entities are internally non-contradictory on cardinality (one supervisor per per-user job/state root, at most one unreaped child, exactly one live generation) and §3.3's ownership-guard rule plausibly extends the existing identity-check pattern in run-file-ownership.ts. However, cross-checking the listed call sites against the actual repo surfaces two real omissions that undercut the \"cover CLI, shell and daemon shutdown entry points\" fix intent, plus a silent gap on the native Darwin capability broker that is the literal root-cause component.","findings":[{"id":"callsite-missing-daemon-start","severity":"MED","location":"/body/dataModelChanges (entity 3.1 \"Managed macOS daemon instance\", callSites)","message":"Entity 3.1 describes the supervisor that spawns the daemon entry and binds a fresh generation before advertising ready — i.e. the SPAWN/start side of the lifecycle. Its listed call sites are lifecycle.ts, run-file-ownership.ts and scripts/daemon-ctl.sh, but the actual spawn logic for the CLI/TUI path lives in src/cli/services/daemon.ts#startDaemon (child_process.spawn of DAEMON_ENTRY, then waitForReady) — not in lifecycle.ts, which only contains the stop-side capture/wait helpers (captureDaemon, waitForStop, cleanupStoppedDaemon, isRunning). The LLD's own Summary requires 'the same installed lifecycle for shell and CLI actions', yet daemon.ts#startDaemon is never named anywhere as a call site for the new supervisor-spawn entity. As written, a builder following the call-site list would update the shell script's spawn_daemon() but have no pointer to the CLI/TUI start path that must equally become supervisor-mediated, risking exactly the CLI/shell inconsistency the fix intent warns against.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:76-83","src/cli/services/daemon.ts:62-80 (startDaemon)","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts:1-121 (no spawn logic; only capture/wait/cleanup)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md:15 (\"Cover CLI, shell and daemon shutdown entry points\")"]},{"id":"callsite-missing-control-bridge","severity":"MED","location":"/body/dataModelChanges (entities 3.1 and 3.2, callSites)","message":"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh's stop path does not call lifecycle.ts or daemon.ts directly: stop_managed_daemon() invokes the compiled helper src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts, which itself imports stopDaemon (daemon.ts) and captureDaemon/cleanupStoppedDaemon (lifecycle.ts) to capture the kernel handle in one Node invocation before the RPC. This file is the actual shell-to-lifecycle bridge for the 'shell' entry point the fix intent calls out, and it currently owns the capture-before-RPC contract the new generation/supervisor binding would also need to thread through. It appears in neither the dataModelChanges call sites for 3.1/3.2 nor the References/citations list (c1-c12), so the design gives no indication this file is in scope for the behavior it already implements and that the new entities change.","evidenceReferences":["src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts:1-21","scripts/daemon-ctl.sh:204-217 (stop_managed_daemon calls out/bin/insrc-daemon-control.js)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:198-211 (References c1-c12, no insrc-daemon-control.ts entry)"]},{"id":"native-broker-disposition-unaddressed","severity":"MED","location":"/body/interactionWithShared (empty) and /body/dataModelChanges","message":"interactionWithShared is empty, which is formally consistent with this being a standalone story with no HLD shared contracts, but it leaves a real cross-cutting dependency undescribed: the ISSUE's root cause is specifically that the native process-capability broker (src/native/process-broker/darwin.c, acquired via the Mach task right in src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts's armShutdownWatchdog, which forks insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts with a brokerPath pointing at out/bin/insrc-process-broker) is denied under the sandboxed/non-debugger entitlement. The LLD cites darwin.c as [[c10]] but never states, anywhere in the data model or error paths, whether the new supervisor-owned child path stops invoking this broker for macOS (plausible, since a direct parent-child relationship no longer needs task_for_pid), retains it for some other purpose, or whether Linux's unchanged retained-target routing still depends on it. Given this native component is the literal defect being fixed, its disposition should be stated explicitly rather than left to be inferred from a precondition sentence ('Managed Darwin children have an inherited private parent channel').","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:160,198-211 (interactionWithShared=[], c10 citation with no body discussion)","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts:21-27 (brokerPath -> out/bin/insrc-process-broker)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md:9-11 (root cause: native retained process capability denial)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/dataModelChanges","/body/interactionWithShared","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","scripts/daemon-ctl.sh","src/cli/services/daemon.ts","src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts","src/daemon/shutdown.ts","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/native/process-broker/ (file listing: darwin.c, linux.c, main.c, broker.h, README.md)"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Consider adding src/cli/services/daemon.ts#startDaemon (and the TUI restart action that calls it) explicitly to entity 3.1's call sites so the CLI start path is not left on the old direct-spawn model while the shell script is updated.","Consider adding src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts to either the call sites or the References list, since it is the concrete shell-to-lifecycle bridge that already implements the capture-before-RPC contract the new generation binding extends.","A one- or two-sentence statement on whether the Darwin native process-broker (darwin.c) is still invoked anywhere in the new macOS path, or is bypassed entirely in favor of the supervisor's direct child ownership, would close a natural reviewer question given it is the cited root-cause component."]},{"chunkId":"error-and-tests","status":"incomplete","summary":"test","findings":[{"id":"f1","severity":"LOW","location":"x","message":"y","evidenceReferences":["z"]}],"inspectedReferences":["a"],"limitations":["Required coverage not reported: /body/errorPaths","Required coverage not reported: /body/testStrategy","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md"],"advice":[]},{"chunkId":"migration-and-alternatives","status":"changes_requested","summary":"Migration steps are feasible, correctly ordered (validate → quiesce via the temp-recovery-job vs legacy-direct-process branches → load managed job → rollback), and the stuck-legacy-instance abort path is consistent with the error-path edge case. The chosen alternative a1 and the rejection rationale for a2/a3 both trace coherently back to the ISSUE's root cause (the shared native task-right dependency and the watchdog arming before drain). However, openQuestions is empty despite section 3's supervisor/control-channel behavior being specified only in prose, with no corresponding interface contract in section 2 — a real gap that should have been flagged as an open question or resolved with a concrete protocol contract before finalization.","findings":[{"id":"lld-migalt-001","severity":"MED","location":"/body/openQuestions; /body/dataModelChanges/1 (\"Supervisor lifecycle and stop receipt\"); LLD.md section 3.2","message":"openQuestions is empty, but the LLD never specifies the actual supervisor IPC protocol as a contract. Section 2 (Contract details) only documents `stopDaemon` and `armShutdownWatchdog` — both pre-existing CLI/daemon-side entry points — while section 3.2 describes an entirely new 'bounded private local control channel' with peer-UID verification, per-installation/per-generation binding, concurrent-request joining of a single deadline, and bounded exit-evidence retention, purely in prose and with no message schema, wire format, or API surface. Given the safety-critical nature of these invariants (only the unreaped child may be signalled; concurrent requests cannot extend the deadline; bounded exit evidence until consumed), the absence of any concrete protocol contract or open question flagging this as undecided is a real gap in finalization-readiness, not merely an implementation detail.","evidenceReferences":["LLD.md:85-93 (section 3.2)","LLD.md:30-72 (section 2, only stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog)","LLD.md:338 openQuestions: []"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/migration","/body/alternativesConsidered","/body/chosenAlternative","/body/alternativesConsidered/0","/body/alternativesConsidered/1","/body/alternativesConsidered/2","/body/openQuestions","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Migration step 2's wording ('Abort migration when that evidence is unavailable') for the legacy-direct-process branch should explicitly cross-reference the error-path edge case's 'Require explicit operator recovery if a legacy instance is stuck' so a reader does not need to infer that aborting the migration is what triggers the operator-recovery requirement.","Consider adding an explicit open question (or a short protocol-sketch subsection) for the new supervisor control channel's message format/versioning before build, since section 3.2's invariants (peer UID check, generation binding, idempotent repeat status/stop) are exactly the kind of detail a missing contract would let drift between the supervisor binary and the CLI/daemon clients.","a2's rejection rationale ('fixed-label forced recovery needs additional instance fencing') is consistent with its own stated con (a separate bounded watchdog still needs authority to stop the original instance), confirming it leaves the same native-capability/hung-process gap from the ISSUE's root cause unresolved — this reasoning chain is sound but could be made more explicit by naming the Mach task-right dependency directly, mirroring a1's pro ('Works without a Mach task right') for an easier side-by-side comparison."]},{"chunkId":"diagrams-and-citations","status":"incomplete","summary":"The sequenceDefinition's 4 participants and 10 messages are faithfully reproduced verbatim in the sequence-diagram.html companion's embedded Mermaid source, and the deadline-arm-before-shutdown-IPC ordering matches the contract/data-model prose in sections 2.2 and 3.2 with no contradiction. All 9 cited source-code files (c3–c11) and the native broker file c10 exist and are relevant (darwin.c's task_for_pid/Mach-port code matches the ISSUE's stated root cause). However, 10 of the 12 citations (c3–c12) are declared only in the References list and as plain call-site paths — they are never tied to a specific body claim via an inline [[cN]] marker, unlike c1/c2 which are properly inline-cited — a traceability gap (MED). I could not actually render the HTML's embedded Mermaid+JS in a browser (no such tool available to me), so visual-rendering fidelity beyond the embedded source text is unverified; per policy this is a limitation, not a finding, and forces status=incomplete regardless of the MED finding above.","findings":[{"id":"citation-traceability-gap","severity":"MED","location":"/citations/2 through /citations/10 (c3–c11); LLD.md References section lines 202-211 and call-site lists lines 80-102","message":"10 of the 12 declared citations (c3–c11, code files, plus c12 the external launchd doc) are listed in the References section and echoed as plain 'Call sites:' paths in the Data model changes section, but none is tied to a specific body claim via an inline [[cN]] marker anywhere in the prose (sections 1–8). Only c1 (implicitly, as the originating ISSUE) and c2 (explicitly, 3x in the Invariants list) are inline-cited. This leaves every contract, data-model and error-path claim that depends on c3–c11 (e.g. the stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog contracts, the supervisor/run-file-ownership invariant-change) without a traceable per-claim citation, weakening the citation-grounding requirement even though the cited files themselves are real and topically relevant.","evidenceReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:200-211","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:80-102","/citations/2","/citations/3","/citations/4","/citations/5","/citations/6","/citations/7","/citations/8","/citations/9","/citations/10"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/sequenceDefinition","/body/companions","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","document:line:104","/citations/0","/citations/1","/citations/2","/citations/3","/citations/4","/citations/5","/citations/6","/citations/7","/citations/8","/citations/9","/citations/10","/citations/11","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/cli/services/daemon.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/native/process-broker/darwin.c","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/scripts/daemon-ctl.sh"],"limitations":["Could not render the sequence-diagram.html companion's embedded Mermaid diagram + pan/zoom JavaScript in an actual browser or headless engine (no such rendering/screenshot tool is available to me in this session); I verified the embedded <pre class=\"mermaid\"> source text matches the sequenceDefinition's participants and all 10 messages verbatim, and confirmed the file wires mermaid.initialize and a pan/zoom controller, but the actual visual rendering output is unverified beyond this static source inspection.","Could not fetch the external c12 citation (https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPSystemStartup/Chapters/CreatingLaunchdJobs.html) to confirm it is live and its content matches the launchd-job claims it supports; I assessed only that it is a well-formed, plausible official Apple documentation URL."],"advice":["Consider adding inline [[cN]] markers next to the specific claims in sections 2 (Contract details) and 3 (Data model changes) that each code citation (c3–c11) actually supports, mirroring how [[c2]] is used in the Invariants list, so each contract/data-model assertion carries a traceable citation rather than relying on the separate References list and 'Call sites' paths."]},{"chunkId":"final-consistency","status":"incomplete","summary":"Cross-checked the whole LLD against the originating ISSUE's reproduction/root-cause/fix-intent and against the five prior chunks' completed feedback (scope-context, contracts, data-model, error-and-tests, migration-and-alternatives, diagrams-and-citations). Six of the seven ISSUE fix-intent elements trace cleanly to a concrete LLD section (same-user lifecycle without a debugger task right → §3.1/§3.2/chosen alternative a1; bounded recovery when hung → §5 error case + §3.2 \"forces at most once\"; verified exit before replacement → §2.1 postcondition + §5 invariants; guarded PID/socket ownership → §3.3; CLI/shell/daemon entry points + Linux preservation + IPC compatibility → §3.1/§3.2 call sites + §3.3 + §7 migration backward-compat; legacy/temporary-recovery migration → §7 + §8). The already-reported findings from scope-context (Summary's populated 'HLD base run/effective hash' directly contradicting §1's 'no parent HLD' statement), contracts (native broker-elimination not explicit; undiscriminated Errors), data-model (missing call sites; unaddressed broker disposition), and migration-and-alternatives (undocumented supervisor IPC protocol, no open question raised) are each real, non-duplicative, and do not contradict one another or the rest of the document. One new whole-document traceability gap was found during this check: the ISSUE's fix intent explicitly requires qualifying \"stale runtime files\" on real macOS alongside normal restart, blocked shutdown and replacement ownership, but §6's smoke-level subjects (the only level qualifying on real macOS/an installed build) list only normal/blocked shutdown, single-daemon restart and rollback — stale run files are exercised only at the unit level (mocked/fixture-based), so this ISSUE requirement is only partially addressed rather than silently dropped. This chunk's own status is forced to `incomplete` because the dependency chunk `error-and-tests` returned placeholder/test content (summary literally \"test\"; a toy finding with id \"f1\", location \"x\", message \"y\") and its own limitations confirm it never substantively inspected /body/errorPaths or /body/testStrategy — so those two sections of the LLD have received no genuine review coverage in this attempt, a gap this final-consistency chunk cannot retroactively fill since its assigned selectors are whole-document cross-checks rather than a section-by-section review. Separately, `diagrams-and-citations` could not render sequence-diagram.html's embedded Mermaid+JS in a browser/headless engine; it verified only that the embedded source text matches the sequenceDefinition's participants/messages verbatim, which is evidence of source fidelity but not of a working rendered visual.","findings":[{"id":"stale-files-smoke-qualification-gap","severity":"MED","location":"LLD.md section 6 Test strategy, smoke level (line ~154) vs ISSUE.md Fix intent (line ~15)","message":"The ISSUE's fix intent states: \"Qualify a normal restart, blocked shutdown, stale runtime files and replacement ownership on real macOS using isolated test instances.\" The LLD's smoke level — the only test level that qualifies on a real installed build on Apple Silicon — lists subjects \"Install or upgrade into supported launchd domain\", \"Normal and blocked shutdown complete with verified generation change\", \"Restart leaves one daemon and serves status and indexing\", and \"Stopped daemon remains stopped and rollback preserves state\", with no subject exercising stale runtime files on real macOS. Stale run files appear only under the unit level (\"Guarded stale run files and replacement ownership\"), which per the test framework description runs against isolated launchd fixtures, not a real installed daemon. This leaves one named element of the ISSUE's fix intent (real-macOS qualification of stale runtime files) only partially addressed rather than fully traceable to a concrete real-device test subject.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 6 (smoke subjects list)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 6 (unit subjects list: 'Guarded stale run files and replacement ownership')","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md Fix intent: 'Qualify a normal restart, blocked shutdown, stale runtime files and replacement ownership on real macOS using isolated test instances.'"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/tmp/design-review/macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld.json","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json"],"limitations":["The dependency chunk 'error-and-tests' returned placeholder/stub content (summary 'test'; a single toy finding id 'f1', location 'x', message 'y', evidenceReferences 'z') rather than a genuine review, and its own recorded limitations state 'Required coverage not reported' for /body/errorPaths, /body/testStrategy and the LLD.md path itself. As a result, Section 5 (Error paths) and Section 6 (Test strategy) of the LLD have had no substantive dedicated review in this attempt; this final-consistency chunk performed only a high-level cross-check of those sections against the ISSUE and the other chunks' findings, not the detailed error-case/edge-case/test-subject review that chunk was assigned to do.","The dependency chunk 'diagrams-and-citations' could not render docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html's embedded Mermaid diagram and pan/zoom JavaScript in an actual browser or headless engine (no such rendering/screenshot tool was available); it verified only that the embedded source text matches the sequenceDefinition verbatim. This final-consistency chunk likewise had no rendering tool available and could not independently verify the working visual beyond source-text fidelity."],"advice":["Before this LLD is finalized, re-run (or manually substitute) a genuine 'error-and-tests' review pass over Section 5 and Section 6 against the ISSUE's fix intent and reproduction — the placeholder submission in this attempt means that mandatory review coverage is still outstanding, independent of the three changes_requested chunks' findings.","Consolidate the four already-reported MED findings (HLD-metadata contradiction, broker-elimination not explicit in contracts, undiscriminated Errors, missing call sites, unaddressed native-broker disposition, undocumented supervisor IPC protocol) plus the new stale-files smoke-qualification gap into a single revision pass rather than patching them independently, since several touch the same root issue (the supervisor's new control-channel protocol is specified only in prose and is not yet reflected in Section 2's contracts, Section 3's call-site lists, or Section 6's real-macOS test subjects).","When the sequence-diagram.html rendering can be inspected (e.g. a headless-browser tool becomes available), confirm the Mermaid diagram actually renders without errors and matches the embedded source — a structured sequenceDefinition and matching embedded source text are not proof of a working visual."]}],"progress":{"status":"incomplete","phase":"complete","completedChunks":7,"totalChunks":7,"lastMeaningfulActivityAt":null,"activeToolCount":0,"requiresUserInput":false,"checkpointPath":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/tmp/design-review/macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld.json","sequence":0,"reviewElapsedMs":0,"unitElapsedMs":null,"observedAt":"2026-11-10T05:39:28.037Z","freshness":"stored"},"startedAt":"2026-11-10T04:56:33.963Z","reviewer":"daemon","model":"claude-sonnet-5","unit":{"unitId":"final-consistency","phase":"chunk_review","executionId":"156d6051-03ab-4942-bc27-8ae5ab311894","dispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[]},"actions":[{"actionId":"resume-planning-claude-sonnet-medium","signature":"4160e0b9e066d2a25a6a55e2c14573b5a0756769b46b3bd14e4087afbeda5dfe","operation":"resume","sourceAttempt":1,"resultAttempt":1,"userDecision":{"action":"retry_failed_unit","reason":"User returned to the macOS restart issue after switching the review role to Claude Code Sonnet medium and restarting the daemon to apply it.","recordedAt":"2026-11-10T05:17:44.991Z"},"acceptedAt":"2026-11-10T05:17:45.018Z","outcome":{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Unknown/duplicate selectors in contracts"}},{"actionId":"retry-sonnet-validated-selectors-02","signature":"1d723122e5af40bb7930b24855d9ce3fe6c29bb5bc3003dbd4c177e696054331","operation":"resume","sourceAttempt":1,"resultAttempt":1,"userDecision":{"action":"retry_failed_unit","reason":"User explicitly approved one more planning retry after the validator rejected unknown or duplicate selectors in the contracts chunk.","recordedAt":"2026-11-10T05:22:13.068Z"},"acceptedAt":"2026-11-10T05:22:13.082Z","outcome":{"code":"completed","summary":"Unit feedback accepted"}}],"plan":{"chunks":[{"id":"scope-context","title":"Scope framing and traceability to the standalone issue","selectors":["/body/summary","/body/hldContextSlice","document:line:25"],"concerns":["Does the summary and HLD-context framing correctly declare this as a standalone (no-HLD) LLD and match the ISSUE's fix intent (same-user lifecycle, bounded recovery, verified exit, guarded ownership, CLI/shell/daemon coverage, Linux preservation)?","Are non-goals/boundary correctly scoped (no shared contracts to honour) given the standalone nature, and is this consistent with the originating ISSUE's root cause (Mach task-right denial) rather than inventing unrelated scope?","Is the rollout phase and 'standalone' framing traceable to the ISSUE citations rather than asserted without grounding?"],"dependsOn":[],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"contracts","title":"API contract: stopDaemon / armShutdownWatchdog","selectors":["/body/contractDetails"],"concerns":["Do stopDaemon and armShutdownWatchdog's pre/postconditions and error conditions actually address the ISSUE's root cause (same native capability failing for both CLI stop and watchdog arm) — e.g. does armShutdownWatchdog's precondition ('inherited private parent channel') eliminate the Mach task-right dependency, and is that stated explicitly rather than implied?","Are the error conditions specific enough to be testable/distinguishable (ownership vs exact-exit failure for stopDaemon; deadline-arm failure for watchdog), matching the ISSUE's complaint that 'a permission denial is reported without distinguishing the failed native operation'?","Is the surface level (internal-shared) consistent with the call sites declared in section 3 (same functions reused across cli/services/lifecycle.ts, daemon.ts, etc.)?"],"dependsOn":[],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"data-model","title":"Data model: supervisor/child entities and run-file invariant","selectors":["/body/dataModelChanges","/body/interactionWithShared"],"concerns":["Is ownership/cardinality of the new entities precise and non-contradictory: exactly one supervisor per per-user job/state root, at most one unreaped child, exactly one live generation — and do the call sites listed for each entity plausibly correspond to where that entity's described behavior would be implemented (cross-check against the actual repo files, e.g. src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts, src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts, src/daemon/shutdown.ts, src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts, src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts, src/cli/services/daemon.ts, scripts/daemon-ctl.sh, src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts)?","Does the 'PID and socket run files' invariant-change entity correctly preserve the existing ownership guard described in the ISSUE/root-cause (run-file-ownership.ts) while adding the new 'only remove proven-dead generation's files' rule, and does it explicitly preserve Linux routing and daemon.status/daemon.shutdown IPC compatibility as the ISSUE's fix intent requires?","interactionWithShared is empty — is that correct for a standalone story with no HLD shared contracts, or does it silently omit a real cross-cutting dependency (e.g. the native darwin.c broker cited in references) that should have been described here?"],"dependsOn":[],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"error-and-tests","title":"Error paths and test strategy coverage","selectors":["/body/errorPaths","/body/testStrategy"],"concerns":["Does every error case/edge case map to a verifiable detection+response, and do the invariants-to-preserve citations ([[c2]]) actually trace to the analyze-bundle citation text in references, not a fabricated quote?","Does the test strategy's subjects actually exercise each declared error case and edge case (e.g. wrong peer/generation, hung-before-shutdown, hung-after-watchdog-readiness, supervisor loss, concurrent restart, stale files, legacy/recovery migration, Linux regression) — identify any error/edge case with no corresponding test subject, or any test subject with no acceptance mapping entry (acceptanceMapping is empty in the canonical artifact) despite the rendered doc implying coverage.","Is the smoke-test's reliance on 'Apple Silicon as a regular user' sufficient to qualify the fix for the exact denial reproduced in the ISSUE (same-user, Homebrew Node, Apple Silicon), or does it omit verifying the specific prior-failure scenario (temporary recovery LaunchAgent migration) as an integration/smoke subject?"],"dependsOn":[],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"migration-and-alternatives","title":"Migration steps, alternatives and chosen design","selectors":["/body/migration","/body/alternativesConsidered","/body/chosenAlternative","/body/alternativesConsidered/0","/body/alternativesConsidered/1","/body/alternativesConsidered/2","/body/openQuestions"],"concerns":["Are the migration steps feasible and properly ordered (validate build -> quiesce old instance, distinguishing temporary-recovery-job vs legacy-direct-process paths -> load managed job -> rollback), and do they correctly abort/require explicit operator recovery per the declared edge case for a stuck legacy instance?","Does the chosen alternative (a1) consistently match the rejection rationale for a2/a3, and does a1's rationale (works without a Mach task right, independent enforcement survives a blocked event loop) directly resolve the ISSUE's stated root cause, as opposed to a2/a3 which plausibly leave the same native-capability dependency or hung-process gap unresolved?","openQuestions is empty — confirm no outstanding design decision was left unresolved that should have blocked finalization (e.g. exact supervisor IPC protocol, launchd plist parameters, generation-ID scheme) given the depth of behavior described in section 3."],"dependsOn":["scope-context","contracts","data-model"],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"diagrams-and-citations","title":"Sequence diagram, companions and citation grounding","selectors":["/body/sequenceDefinition","/body/companions","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","document:line:104","/citations/0","/citations/1","/citations/2","/citations/3","/citations/4","/citations/5","/citations/6","/citations/7","/citations/8","/citations/9","/citations/10","/citations/11"],"concerns":["Inspect the actual rendered sequence-diagram.html companion (not just the structured definition) at docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html — does it render correctly and faithfully reflect the sequenceDefinition's participants (ctl, supervisor, daemon, guard) and message flow, particularly the deadline-arm-before-shutdown-IPC ordering central to the fix?","Do the sequence messages match the contract/data-model sections exactly (e.g. 'Bind generation and arm deadline' before 'Request graceful shutdown' consistent with armShutdownWatchdog's precondition; 'Reconcile owned run files' consistent with the run-file-ownership invariant-change), or is there a contradiction in ordering between section 2/3 and the diagram?","Are all 12 references ([[c1]]-[[c12]]) real and correctly typed against the actual repo/ISSUE — spot-check that c1 points to the real ISSUE.md, c3-c9 point to files that exist in the repo and are relevant to the described behavior (src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts, src/cli/services/daemon.ts, src/daemon/shutdown.ts, src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts, src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts, src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts, src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts), c10 (src/native/process-broker/darwin.c) is the actual native broker named in the ISSUE's root cause, and c12 is a legitimate external launchd doc; note any citation that is unused in the body or any body claim lacking a citation."],"dependsOn":[],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"final-consistency","title":"Whole-document consistency and upstream (ISSUE) alignment","selectors":[],"concerns":["Cross-check the whole LLD for internal contradictions across sections (contract preconditions vs data-model entity behavior vs sequence diagram vs error paths vs migration steps) and confirm every element of the ISSUE's fix intent (same-user lifecycle completing graceful stop/restart without a debugger task right; bounded recovery when hung; verified exit before replacement; guarded PID/socket ownership; CLI+shell+daemon entry points; Linux preservation; IPC compatibility; migration from the live legacy/temporary-recovery install; real-macOS qualification of normal/blocked/stale-files/ownership scenarios) is traceable to a concrete LLD section.","Confirm no requirement from the ISSUE's reproduction/root-cause/fix-intent was silently dropped or left only partially addressed, and that the alternatives/migration/test sections do not contradict the chosen contract/data-model design.","Aggregate limitations: note explicitly if the sequence-diagram.html rendering could not be inspected/verified in this review, since a structured sequenceDefinition alone is not proof of a working visual."],"dependsOn":["scope-context","contracts","data-model","error-and-tests","migration-and-alternatives","diagrams-and-citations"],"purpose":"consistency"}],"coverageExplanation":"Every inventory unit is assigned to exactly one substantive chunk (no unit repeated across chunks, no selector outside the supplied inventory list). Section-level units are grouped by document area: scope/framing (summary, hldContextSlice, section-1 heading) in scope-context; the two API operations in contracts; the three new/changed data-model entities plus the empty interactionWithShared in data-model; error/edge cases and test strategy together in error-and-tests (tests must be checked against the error cases they claim to cover); migration steps, alternatives (including each alternative sub-unit a1/a2/a3), chosenAlternative and openQuestions together in migration-and-alternatives (alternatives and chosen design are one coherent decision); and all visual/diagram units (sequenceDefinition, its four participants, companions, the Diagrams section heading) plus all twelve citations together in diagrams-and-citations, since citation grounding must be checked against what the diagram and contracts actually claim. The final-consistency chunk has empty selectors (whole-artifact check) and depends on all six substantive chunks, re-validating cross-chunk contradictions and alignment with the originating ISSUE.","finalConsistencyChunkId":"final-consistency","coverage":[{"unitId":"/body/summary","chunkIds":["scope-context"]},{"unitId":"/body/hldContextSlice","chunkIds":["scope-context"]},{"unitId":"document:line:25","chunkIds":["scope-context"]},{"unitId":"/body/contractDetails","chunkIds":["contracts"]},{"unitId":"/body/dataModelChanges","chunkIds":["data-model"]},{"unitId":"/body/interactionWithShared","chunkIds":["data-model"]},{"unitId":"/body/errorPaths","chunkIds":["error-and-tests"]},{"unitId":"/body/testStrategy","chunkIds":["error-and-tests"]},{"unitId":"/body/migration","chunkIds":["migration-and-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/alternativesConsidered","chunkIds":["migration-and-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/chosenAlternative","chunkIds":["migration-and-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/openQuestions","chunkIds":["migration-and-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/alternativesConsidered/0","chunkIds":["migration-and-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/alternativesConsidered/1","chunkIds":["migration-and-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/alternativesConsidered/2","chunkIds":["migration-and-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/body/companions","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"document:line:104","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/0","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/1","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/2","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/3","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/4","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/5","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/6","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/7","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/8","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/9","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/10","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/11","chunkIds":["diagrams-and-citations"]}]},"unitSummaries":[{"unitId":"planning","phase":"planning","automaticDispatches":4,"retriesUsed":3,"userDirectedDispatches":2,"diagnostics":[{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Review activity CLI failed; NATIVE_CLI_FAILURE /: Resolve the reported native CLI output failure."},{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Review activity CLI failed; NATIVE_CLI_FAILURE /: Resolve the reported native CLI output failure."},{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Review activity CLI failed; NATIVE_CLI_FAILURE /: Resolve the reported native CLI output failure."},{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Review activity CLI failed; NATIVE_CLI_FAILURE /: Resolve the reported native CLI output failure."}],"actionIds":["resume-planning-claude-sonnet-medium","retry-sonnet-validated-selectors-02"]},{"unitId":"scope-context","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"contracts","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"data-model","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"error-and-tests","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"migration-and-alternatives","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"diagrams-and-citations","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":2,"retriesUsed":1,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Status contradicts findings or mandatory verification limits: received changes_requested, expected incomplete; limitations=2, HIGH/MED=1"}],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"final-consistency","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]}],"report":{"reviewId":"macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld","attempt":1,"artifactId":"LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1","status":"incomplete","counts":{"HIGH":0,"MED":9,"LOW":3},"chunks":[{"chunkId":"scope-context","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The Summary and HLD-context section correctly declare this as a standalone, no-HLD LLD and the prose framing is traceable to the originating ISSUE's fix intent (same-user lifecycle, bounded recovery via an independent deadline, confirmed exit, guarded ownership, CLI/shell coverage). However, the Summary metadata block (lines 7-9) prints populated \"HLD base run\" and \"HLD effective hash\" values immediately above a Section 1 statement that explicitly says \"no parent HLD\" — a direct contradiction in exactly the material under review. Scope/non-goals are not invented beyond the ISSUE's Mach-task-right root cause; the chosen approach (per-user supervisor) is grounded in that root cause. The Summary omits an explicit mention of Linux preservation and the daemon-shutdown entry point (both present elsewhere in the body but outside this chunk's selectors), which is a minor completeness gap in the executive framing.","findings":[{"id":"hld-metadata-contradiction","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md lines 7-9 (Summary) vs lines 25-28 (Section 1 HLD context)","message":"The Summary block states 'HLD base run: wf-1794286431327-0tz91a' and 'HLD effective hash: 0868ff19170b...' with real populated values, immediately followed by Section 1's declaration 'Standalone feature — no parent HLD... There are no HLD shared contracts to honour.' Cross-checking the canonical artifact (LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json meta) shows hldBaseRunId/hldEffectiveHash are actually just this design.story run's own runId/contentHash, reused under HLD-labeled field names — not a reference to any real HLD document. Rendering these as 'HLD base run'/'HLD effective hash' in a standalone LLD misrepresents the artifact's provenance: a reader cannot tell from the document alone that no HLD exists, directly contradicting the very next section's explicit statement.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:7-9","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:25-28","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json (meta.hldBaseRunId, meta.hldEffectiveHash, meta.standalone)"]},{"id":"summary-omits-linux-and-daemon-entry","severity":"LOW","location":"S001/LLD.md lines 5-11 (Summary)","message":"The originating ISSUE's fix intent requires covering 'CLI, shell and daemon shutdown entry points while preserving Linux behavior'. The Summary names only 'shell and CLI actions' and does not mention the daemon's own shutdown-watchdog entry point or Linux preservation, even though both are addressed later in the body (Section 2.2, Section 3.3). This is a completeness gap in the executive framing rather than a missing requirement.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:5-11","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md:13-16 (Fix intent)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/summary","/body/hldContextSlice","document:line:25","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Consider suppressing or relabeling the 'HLD base run'/'HLD effective hash' fields in the rendered Summary for a standalone LLD (meta.standalone === true), since those values are just the design.story run's own identifiers and not a real HLD reference — this appears to be a template rendering gap rather than an authoring choice.","Optionally broaden the Summary's one sentence on lifecycle coverage to explicitly name the daemon's own shutdown-watchdog entry point and Linux preservation, matching the ISSUE's fix intent wording, purely for executive-summary completeness."]},{"chunkId":"contracts","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The contracts for stopDaemon and armShutdownWatchdog (section 2) are internally consistent with their call sites in section 3 and have reasonable pre/postconditions, but they do not make explicit — at the contract level a builder/tester implements against — how the chosen supervisor architecture actually eliminates the native Mach task_for_pid/process-broker dependency that the ISSUE names as root cause, and their Errors clauses collapse distinguishable failure modes into one untyped Error, which does not address the ISSUE's complaint that a denial is reported without distinguishing the failed native operation and is not testable as a discriminated condition.","findings":[{"id":"f1-broker-elimination-not-explicit","severity":"MED","location":"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 2.1 (stopDaemon Preconditions) and 2.2 (armShutdownWatchdog Preconditions)","message":"The root cause is that both stopDaemon's capture path and armShutdownWatchdog's own helper acquire a Mach task right via the native `insrc-process-broker` (task_for_pid), and that acquisition is denied in the field. The chosen alternative (a1: LaunchAgent with a parent supervisor) is supposed to fix this by making the supervisor the real OS parent of the daemon, so it can signal/reap without task_for_pid. But the contracts in section 2 never say this explicitly: stopDaemon's precondition is just 'Resolve the installed daemon and authenticate its active generation'; armShutdownWatchdog's precondition is just 'Managed Darwin children have an inherited private parent channel.' Neither states that exit/arm confirmation is now obtained from the supervisor's native parent-child relationship instead of a task_for_pid-based broker capture, and WatchdogOptions still carries a `brokerPath` field in the current interface with no contract-level statement that it is removed, replaced, or kept only as a fallback. A conforming implementation could satisfy this contract text while still calling the existing `captureStopTarget`/`insrc-process-broker` path underneath (as `src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts` does today), reproducing the exact denial the Story exists to fix.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:48-49 (stopDaemon Preconditions)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:68-69 (armShutdownWatchdog Preconditions)","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts:183-195 (captureStopTarget spawns the broker)","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c:55 (task_for_pid)","src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts:45 (watchdog helper still calls captureStopTarget on its own parent)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md root cause section"]},{"id":"f2-errors-not-discriminated","severity":"MED","location":"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 2.1 (stopDaemon Errors) and 2.2 (armShutdownWatchdog Errors)","message":"stopDaemon's Errors clause is a single undiscriminated `Error` for 'Ownership or exact exit could not be verified' — this merges two causally different conditions (a run-file ownership/identity conflict, vs. inability to confirm the process actually exited) that a caller or test would need to tell apart to react correctly, yet the contract names no error code/category/type to assert on. armShutdownWatchdog's Errors clause ('The independent deadline could not be armed on the verified generation') is likewise one opaque condition, even though the current helper already internally distinguishes readiness-timeout, spawn failure and invalid-reply causes (`src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts` fail() call sites). This directly reproduces, at the contract level, the ISSUE's own complaint that 'a permission denial is reported without distinguishing the failed native operation' — the new contract does not require the implementation to fix that diagnosability gap. Section 6's test strategy also names no subject that asserts a discriminated error for ownership-vs-exit-uncertainty, so this ambiguity would not be caught by the planned tests either.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:45-46 (stopDaemon Errors)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:65-66 (armShutdownWatchdog Errors)","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts:29-42 (fail() with distinct reasons that the contract does not require to surface)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md root cause: 'A permission denial is reported without distinguishing the failed native operation.'","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 6 Test strategy (no discriminated-error subject listed)"]},{"id":"f3-watchdog-helper-listed-as-call-site","severity":"LOW","location":"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 3.2 Call sites","message":"`src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts` is listed as a call site of the 'Supervisor lifecycle and stop receipt' entity that covers the armShutdownWatchdog contract, but in the current code this file is the watchdog's forked CHILD helper that armShutdownWatchdog spawns — it does not call armShutdownWatchdog, armShutdownWatchdog calls (forks) it. If the new design retains this parent/child relationship, labeling it a 'call site' alongside true callers (daemon.ts, shutdown.ts) is imprecise and could mislead an implementer about which module owns versus consumes the contracted API.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:89-93 (section 3.2 Call sites)","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts:21-27 (armShutdownWatchdog forks the helper, rather than being called by it)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/contractDetails","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","src/cli/services/daemon.ts","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/daemon/shutdown.ts","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c","src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Add a line to the stopDaemon and armShutdownWatchdog preconditions that explicitly names the mechanism replacing task_for_pid/process-broker capture for managed Darwin children (e.g. 'exit/arm confirmation is obtained via the supervisor's direct child relationship — waitpid/kill — never via captureStopTarget's native broker'), so the contract itself forecloses a conforming-but-unfixed implementation.","Split the Errors clauses into discriminated conditions (e.g. an ownership-conflict error distinct from an exit-unconfirmed error for stopDaemon; a readiness-timeout vs spawn-failure vs invalid-reply distinction for armShutdownWatchdog, or at least a documented error category/code) and add a test-strategy subject that asserts on the discriminator, so the ISSUE's 'denial without distinguishing the failed native operation' complaint is verifiably closed.","Clarify in section 3.2 whether insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts remains a spawned helper (not a caller) of armShutdownWatchdog, or correct the call-site framing if its role changes under the new supervisor design."]},{"chunkId":"data-model","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The three new/changed data-model entities are internally non-contradictory on cardinality (one supervisor per per-user job/state root, at most one unreaped child, exactly one live generation) and §3.3's ownership-guard rule plausibly extends the existing identity-check pattern in run-file-ownership.ts. However, cross-checking the listed call sites against the actual repo surfaces two real omissions that undercut the \"cover CLI, shell and daemon shutdown entry points\" fix intent, plus a silent gap on the native Darwin capability broker that is the literal root-cause component.","findings":[{"id":"callsite-missing-daemon-start","severity":"MED","location":"/body/dataModelChanges (entity 3.1 \"Managed macOS daemon instance\", callSites)","message":"Entity 3.1 describes the supervisor that spawns the daemon entry and binds a fresh generation before advertising ready — i.e. the SPAWN/start side of the lifecycle. Its listed call sites are lifecycle.ts, run-file-ownership.ts and scripts/daemon-ctl.sh, but the actual spawn logic for the CLI/TUI path lives in src/cli/services/daemon.ts#startDaemon (child_process.spawn of DAEMON_ENTRY, then waitForReady) — not in lifecycle.ts, which only contains the stop-side capture/wait helpers (captureDaemon, waitForStop, cleanupStoppedDaemon, isRunning). The LLD's own Summary requires 'the same installed lifecycle for shell and CLI actions', yet daemon.ts#startDaemon is never named anywhere as a call site for the new supervisor-spawn entity. As written, a builder following the call-site list would update the shell script's spawn_daemon() but have no pointer to the CLI/TUI start path that must equally become supervisor-mediated, risking exactly the CLI/shell inconsistency the fix intent warns against.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:76-83","src/cli/services/daemon.ts:62-80 (startDaemon)","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts:1-121 (no spawn logic; only capture/wait/cleanup)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md:15 (\"Cover CLI, shell and daemon shutdown entry points\")"]},{"id":"callsite-missing-control-bridge","severity":"MED","location":"/body/dataModelChanges (entities 3.1 and 3.2, callSites)","message":"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh's stop path does not call lifecycle.ts or daemon.ts directly: stop_managed_daemon() invokes the compiled helper src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts, which itself imports stopDaemon (daemon.ts) and captureDaemon/cleanupStoppedDaemon (lifecycle.ts) to capture the kernel handle in one Node invocation before the RPC. This file is the actual shell-to-lifecycle bridge for the 'shell' entry point the fix intent calls out, and it currently owns the capture-before-RPC contract the new generation/supervisor binding would also need to thread through. It appears in neither the dataModelChanges call sites for 3.1/3.2 nor the References/citations list (c1-c12), so the design gives no indication this file is in scope for the behavior it already implements and that the new entities change.","evidenceReferences":["src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts:1-21","scripts/daemon-ctl.sh:204-217 (stop_managed_daemon calls out/bin/insrc-daemon-control.js)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:198-211 (References c1-c12, no insrc-daemon-control.ts entry)"]},{"id":"native-broker-disposition-unaddressed","severity":"MED","location":"/body/interactionWithShared (empty) and /body/dataModelChanges","message":"interactionWithShared is empty, which is formally consistent with this being a standalone story with no HLD shared contracts, but it leaves a real cross-cutting dependency undescribed: the ISSUE's root cause is specifically that the native process-capability broker (src/native/process-broker/darwin.c, acquired via the Mach task right in src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts's armShutdownWatchdog, which forks insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts with a brokerPath pointing at out/bin/insrc-process-broker) is denied under the sandboxed/non-debugger entitlement. The LLD cites darwin.c as [[c10]] but never states, anywhere in the data model or error paths, whether the new supervisor-owned child path stops invoking this broker for macOS (plausible, since a direct parent-child relationship no longer needs task_for_pid), retains it for some other purpose, or whether Linux's unchanged retained-target routing still depends on it. Given this native component is the literal defect being fixed, its disposition should be stated explicitly rather than left to be inferred from a precondition sentence ('Managed Darwin children have an inherited private parent channel').","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:160,198-211 (interactionWithShared=[], c10 citation with no body discussion)","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts:21-27 (brokerPath -> out/bin/insrc-process-broker)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md:9-11 (root cause: native retained process capability denial)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/dataModelChanges","/body/interactionWithShared","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","scripts/daemon-ctl.sh","src/cli/services/daemon.ts","src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts","src/daemon/shutdown.ts","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/native/process-broker/ (file listing: darwin.c, linux.c, main.c, broker.h, README.md)"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Consider adding src/cli/services/daemon.ts#startDaemon (and the TUI restart action that calls it) explicitly to entity 3.1's call sites so the CLI start path is not left on the old direct-spawn model while the shell script is updated.","Consider adding src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts to either the call sites or the References list, since it is the concrete shell-to-lifecycle bridge that already implements the capture-before-RPC contract the new generation binding extends.","A one- or two-sentence statement on whether the Darwin native process-broker (darwin.c) is still invoked anywhere in the new macOS path, or is bypassed entirely in favor of the supervisor's direct child ownership, would close a natural reviewer question given it is the cited root-cause component."]},{"chunkId":"error-and-tests","status":"incomplete","summary":"test","findings":[{"id":"f1","severity":"LOW","location":"x","message":"y","evidenceReferences":["z"]}],"inspectedReferences":["a"],"limitations":["Required coverage not reported: /body/errorPaths","Required coverage not reported: /body/testStrategy","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md"],"advice":[]},{"chunkId":"migration-and-alternatives","status":"changes_requested","summary":"Migration steps are feasible, correctly ordered (validate → quiesce via the temp-recovery-job vs legacy-direct-process branches → load managed job → rollback), and the stuck-legacy-instance abort path is consistent with the error-path edge case. The chosen alternative a1 and the rejection rationale for a2/a3 both trace coherently back to the ISSUE's root cause (the shared native task-right dependency and the watchdog arming before drain). However, openQuestions is empty despite section 3's supervisor/control-channel behavior being specified only in prose, with no corresponding interface contract in section 2 — a real gap that should have been flagged as an open question or resolved with a concrete protocol contract before finalization.","findings":[{"id":"lld-migalt-001","severity":"MED","location":"/body/openQuestions; /body/dataModelChanges/1 (\"Supervisor lifecycle and stop receipt\"); LLD.md section 3.2","message":"openQuestions is empty, but the LLD never specifies the actual supervisor IPC protocol as a contract. Section 2 (Contract details) only documents `stopDaemon` and `armShutdownWatchdog` — both pre-existing CLI/daemon-side entry points — while section 3.2 describes an entirely new 'bounded private local control channel' with peer-UID verification, per-installation/per-generation binding, concurrent-request joining of a single deadline, and bounded exit-evidence retention, purely in prose and with no message schema, wire format, or API surface. Given the safety-critical nature of these invariants (only the unreaped child may be signalled; concurrent requests cannot extend the deadline; bounded exit evidence until consumed), the absence of any concrete protocol contract or open question flagging this as undecided is a real gap in finalization-readiness, not merely an implementation detail.","evidenceReferences":["LLD.md:85-93 (section 3.2)","LLD.md:30-72 (section 2, only stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog)","LLD.md:338 openQuestions: []"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/migration","/body/alternativesConsidered","/body/chosenAlternative","/body/alternativesConsidered/0","/body/alternativesConsidered/1","/body/alternativesConsidered/2","/body/openQuestions","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Migration step 2's wording ('Abort migration when that evidence is unavailable') for the legacy-direct-process branch should explicitly cross-reference the error-path edge case's 'Require explicit operator recovery if a legacy instance is stuck' so a reader does not need to infer that aborting the migration is what triggers the operator-recovery requirement.","Consider adding an explicit open question (or a short protocol-sketch subsection) for the new supervisor control channel's message format/versioning before build, since section 3.2's invariants (peer UID check, generation binding, idempotent repeat status/stop) are exactly the kind of detail a missing contract would let drift between the supervisor binary and the CLI/daemon clients.","a2's rejection rationale ('fixed-label forced recovery needs additional instance fencing') is consistent with its own stated con (a separate bounded watchdog still needs authority to stop the original instance), confirming it leaves the same native-capability/hung-process gap from the ISSUE's root cause unresolved — this reasoning chain is sound but could be made more explicit by naming the Mach task-right dependency directly, mirroring a1's pro ('Works without a Mach task right') for an easier side-by-side comparison."]},{"chunkId":"diagrams-and-citations","status":"incomplete","summary":"The sequenceDefinition's 4 participants and 10 messages are faithfully reproduced verbatim in the sequence-diagram.html companion's embedded Mermaid source, and the deadline-arm-before-shutdown-IPC ordering matches the contract/data-model prose in sections 2.2 and 3.2 with no contradiction. All 9 cited source-code files (c3–c11) and the native broker file c10 exist and are relevant (darwin.c's task_for_pid/Mach-port code matches the ISSUE's stated root cause). However, 10 of the 12 citations (c3–c12) are declared only in the References list and as plain call-site paths — they are never tied to a specific body claim via an inline [[cN]] marker, unlike c1/c2 which are properly inline-cited — a traceability gap (MED). I could not actually render the HTML's embedded Mermaid+JS in a browser (no such tool available to me), so visual-rendering fidelity beyond the embedded source text is unverified; per policy this is a limitation, not a finding, and forces status=incomplete regardless of the MED finding above.","findings":[{"id":"citation-traceability-gap","severity":"MED","location":"/citations/2 through /citations/10 (c3–c11); LLD.md References section lines 202-211 and call-site lists lines 80-102","message":"10 of the 12 declared citations (c3–c11, code files, plus c12 the external launchd doc) are listed in the References section and echoed as plain 'Call sites:' paths in the Data model changes section, but none is tied to a specific body claim via an inline [[cN]] marker anywhere in the prose (sections 1–8). Only c1 (implicitly, as the originating ISSUE) and c2 (explicitly, 3x in the Invariants list) are inline-cited. This leaves every contract, data-model and error-path claim that depends on c3–c11 (e.g. the stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog contracts, the supervisor/run-file-ownership invariant-change) without a traceable per-claim citation, weakening the citation-grounding requirement even though the cited files themselves are real and topically relevant.","evidenceReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:200-211","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:80-102","/citations/2","/citations/3","/citations/4","/citations/5","/citations/6","/citations/7","/citations/8","/citations/9","/citations/10"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/sequenceDefinition","/body/companions","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","document:line:104","/citations/0","/citations/1","/citations/2","/citations/3","/citations/4","/citations/5","/citations/6","/citations/7","/citations/8","/citations/9","/citations/10","/citations/11","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/cli/services/daemon.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/native/process-broker/darwin.c","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/scripts/daemon-ctl.sh"],"limitations":["Could not render the sequence-diagram.html companion's embedded Mermaid diagram + pan/zoom JavaScript in an actual browser or headless engine (no such rendering/screenshot tool is available to me in this session); I verified the embedded <pre class=\"mermaid\"> source text matches the sequenceDefinition's participants and all 10 messages verbatim, and confirmed the file wires mermaid.initialize and a pan/zoom controller, but the actual visual rendering output is unverified beyond this static source inspection.","Could not fetch the external c12 citation (https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPSystemStartup/Chapters/CreatingLaunchdJobs.html) to confirm it is live and its content matches the launchd-job claims it supports; I assessed only that it is a well-formed, plausible official Apple documentation URL."],"advice":["Consider adding inline [[cN]] markers next to the specific claims in sections 2 (Contract details) and 3 (Data model changes) that each code citation (c3–c11) actually supports, mirroring how [[c2]] is used in the Invariants list, so each contract/data-model assertion carries a traceable citation rather than relying on the separate References list and 'Call sites' paths."]},{"chunkId":"final-consistency","status":"incomplete","summary":"Cross-checked the whole LLD against the originating ISSUE's reproduction/root-cause/fix-intent and against the five prior chunks' completed feedback (scope-context, contracts, data-model, error-and-tests, migration-and-alternatives, diagrams-and-citations). Six of the seven ISSUE fix-intent elements trace cleanly to a concrete LLD section (same-user lifecycle without a debugger task right → §3.1/§3.2/chosen alternative a1; bounded recovery when hung → §5 error case + §3.2 \"forces at most once\"; verified exit before replacement → §2.1 postcondition + §5 invariants; guarded PID/socket ownership → §3.3; CLI/shell/daemon entry points + Linux preservation + IPC compatibility → §3.1/§3.2 call sites + §3.3 + §7 migration backward-compat; legacy/temporary-recovery migration → §7 + §8). The already-reported findings from scope-context (Summary's populated 'HLD base run/effective hash' directly contradicting §1's 'no parent HLD' statement), contracts (native broker-elimination not explicit; undiscriminated Errors), data-model (missing call sites; unaddressed broker disposition), and migration-and-alternatives (undocumented supervisor IPC protocol, no open question raised) are each real, non-duplicative, and do not contradict one another or the rest of the document. One new whole-document traceability gap was found during this check: the ISSUE's fix intent explicitly requires qualifying \"stale runtime files\" on real macOS alongside normal restart, blocked shutdown and replacement ownership, but §6's smoke-level subjects (the only level qualifying on real macOS/an installed build) list only normal/blocked shutdown, single-daemon restart and rollback — stale run files are exercised only at the unit level (mocked/fixture-based), so this ISSUE requirement is only partially addressed rather than silently dropped. This chunk's own status is forced to `incomplete` because the dependency chunk `error-and-tests` returned placeholder/test content (summary literally \"test\"; a toy finding with id \"f1\", location \"x\", message \"y\") and its own limitations confirm it never substantively inspected /body/errorPaths or /body/testStrategy — so those two sections of the LLD have received no genuine review coverage in this attempt, a gap this final-consistency chunk cannot retroactively fill since its assigned selectors are whole-document cross-checks rather than a section-by-section review. Separately, `diagrams-and-citations` could not render sequence-diagram.html's embedded Mermaid+JS in a browser/headless engine; it verified only that the embedded source text matches the sequenceDefinition's participants/messages verbatim, which is evidence of source fidelity but not of a working rendered visual.","findings":[{"id":"stale-files-smoke-qualification-gap","severity":"MED","location":"LLD.md section 6 Test strategy, smoke level (line ~154) vs ISSUE.md Fix intent (line ~15)","message":"The ISSUE's fix intent states: \"Qualify a normal restart, blocked shutdown, stale runtime files and replacement ownership on real macOS using isolated test instances.\" The LLD's smoke level — the only test level that qualifies on a real installed build on Apple Silicon — lists subjects \"Install or upgrade into supported launchd domain\", \"Normal and blocked shutdown complete with verified generation change\", \"Restart leaves one daemon and serves status and indexing\", and \"Stopped daemon remains stopped and rollback preserves state\", with no subject exercising stale runtime files on real macOS. Stale run files appear only under the unit level (\"Guarded stale run files and replacement ownership\"), which per the test framework description runs against isolated launchd fixtures, not a real installed daemon. This leaves one named element of the ISSUE's fix intent (real-macOS qualification of stale runtime files) only partially addressed rather than fully traceable to a concrete real-device test subject.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 6 (smoke subjects list)","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md section 6 (unit subjects list: 'Guarded stale run files and replacement ownership')","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md Fix intent: 'Qualify a normal restart, blocked shutdown, stale runtime files and replacement ownership on real macOS using isolated test instances.'"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/tmp/design-review/macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld.json","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json"],"limitations":["The dependency chunk 'error-and-tests' returned placeholder/stub content (summary 'test'; a single toy finding id 'f1', location 'x', message 'y', evidenceReferences 'z') rather than a genuine review, and its own recorded limitations state 'Required coverage not reported' for /body/errorPaths, /body/testStrategy and the LLD.md path itself. As a result, Section 5 (Error paths) and Section 6 (Test strategy) of the LLD have had no substantive dedicated review in this attempt; this final-consistency chunk performed only a high-level cross-check of those sections against the ISSUE and the other chunks' findings, not the detailed error-case/edge-case/test-subject review that chunk was assigned to do.","The dependency chunk 'diagrams-and-citations' could not render docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html's embedded Mermaid diagram and pan/zoom JavaScript in an actual browser or headless engine (no such rendering/screenshot tool was available); it verified only that the embedded source text matches the sequenceDefinition verbatim. This final-consistency chunk likewise had no rendering tool available and could not independently verify the working visual beyond source-text fidelity."],"advice":["Before this LLD is finalized, re-run (or manually substitute) a genuine 'error-and-tests' review pass over Section 5 and Section 6 against the ISSUE's fix intent and reproduction — the placeholder submission in this attempt means that mandatory review coverage is still outstanding, independent of the three changes_requested chunks' findings.","Consolidate the four already-reported MED findings (HLD-metadata contradiction, broker-elimination not explicit in contracts, undiscriminated Errors, missing call sites, unaddressed native-broker disposition, undocumented supervisor IPC protocol) plus the new stale-files smoke-qualification gap into a single revision pass rather than patching them independently, since several touch the same root issue (the supervisor's new control-channel protocol is specified only in prose and is not yet reflected in Section 2's contracts, Section 3's call-site lists, or Section 6's real-macOS test subjects).","When the sequence-diagram.html rendering can be inspected (e.g. a headless-browser tool becomes available), confirm the Mermaid diagram actually renders without errors and matches the embedded source — a structured sequenceDefinition and matching embedded source text are not proof of a working visual."]}],"limitations":["Required coverage not reported: /body/errorPaths","Required coverage not reported: /body/testStrategy","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","Could not render the sequence-diagram.html companion's embedded Mermaid diagram + pan/zoom JavaScript in an actual browser or headless engine (no such rendering/screenshot tool is available to me in this session); I verified the embedded <pre class=\"mermaid\"> source text matches the sequenceDefinition's participants and all 10 messages verbatim, and confirmed the file wires mermaid.initialize and a pan/zoom controller, but the actual visual rendering output is unverified beyond this static source inspection.","Could not fetch the external c12 citation (https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPSystemStartup/Chapters/CreatingLaunchdJobs.html) to confirm it is live and its content matches the launchd-job claims it supports; I assessed only that it is a well-formed, plausible official Apple documentation URL.","The dependency chunk 'error-and-tests' returned placeholder/stub content (summary 'test'; a single toy finding id 'f1', location 'x', message 'y', evidenceReferences 'z') rather than a genuine review, and its own recorded limitations state 'Required coverage not reported' for /body/errorPaths, /body/testStrategy and the LLD.md path itself. As a result, Section 5 (Error paths) and Section 6 (Test strategy) of the LLD have had no substantive dedicated review in this attempt; this final-consistency chunk performed only a high-level cross-check of those sections against the ISSUE and the other chunks' findings, not the detailed error-case/edge-case/test-subject review that chunk was assigned to do.","The dependency chunk 'diagrams-and-citations' could not render docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html's embedded Mermaid diagram and pan/zoom JavaScript in an actual browser or headless engine (no such rendering/screenshot tool was available); it verified only that the embedded source text matches the sequenceDefinition verbatim. This final-consistency chunk likewise had no rendering tool available and could not independently verify the working visual beyond source-text fidelity."],"reviewer":"daemon","reviewedContentIdentity":"f39e67e2f81e081fefe06b9044b63cf3ba0000adeb3ec2eb7e3a65c67f968f13"},"finishedAt":"2026-11-10T05:39:28.037Z"}]}}}}
