:"controller"},"preservationDigest":"d90b9e8f40a3b1b0098a34feeee46394a3eccd39bb807a7ec65628e053a5767a","schemaVersion":1,"submitted":{"artifactPath":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","attempt":1},"attempts":[{"number":1,"status":"incomplete","preparationKind":"planning","baseline":{"subjectFingerprints":{"canonical":"7d702a0c7f39c196b3605a3ba7f0c34338a4ebf3b3590856acd0946f85b42d9c","document":"2396e6f2cc655178d99729105f3405cdd715677affd079bc96e694a71d60281e"},"contentIdentity":"760e28455ee38634b1b39f6e1c6697b7ddd823336c802b8c0a49c1ffc12e98e3","reviewedContent":{"canonical":"{\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"workflow\": \"design.story\",\n    \"runId\": \"wf-1794289307330-lepla3\",\n    \"repoPath\": \"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc\",\n    \"createdAt\": \"2026-11-10T05:43:39.243Z\",\n    \"epicCreatedAt\": \"2026-11-10T04:52:20.369Z\",\n    \"attribution\": {\n      \"outputs\": [\n        {\n          \"role\": \"(client)\",\n          \"tier\": \"core\",\n          \"runner\": \"cli-codex\",\n          \"model\": \"unknown\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"elapsedMs\": 111578,\n    \"repoIndexedAt\": null,\n    \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n    \"epicHash\": \"3992d2e1e26124dc\",\n    \"epicSlug\": \"restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos\",\n    \"storyId\": \"s1\",\n    \"hldBaseRunId\": \"wf-1794289307330-lepla3\",\n    \"hldEffectiveHash\": \"83d3c68defdd5ab8d976b43e178aded1059b70f370663641f9128b764e5b58a1\",\n    \"hldAmendmentsApplied\": [],\n    \"storyTitle\": \"Restore reliable daemon restart on macOS\",\n    \"standalone\": true,\n    \"sizeClass\": \"bugfix\",\n    \"authoredBy\": \"controller\",\n    \"lifecycle\": {\n      \"status\": \"draft\",\n      \"revision\": {\n        \"number\": 2,\n        \"contentHash\": \"48ffe33c3fadb3cc8dedccd12996e15c65ebcaccb43f1b66509c9155ce203b0c\",\n        \"at\": \"2026-11-10T05:43:39.371Z\",\n        \"by\": {\n          \"id\": \"insrc:system\",\n          \"kind\": \"system\",\n          \"properties\": {}\n        }\n      },\n      \"signoffs\": []\n    },\n    \"envelope\": {\n      \"attachments\": []\n    },\n    \"revision\": 2\n  },\n  \"body\": {\n    \"summary\": {\n      \"audience\": \"technical\",\n      \"prose\": \"Run the installed macOS daemon as the child of a dedicated per-user LaunchAgent supervisor [[c12]]. The supervisor handles shell and CLI/TUI start and stop, and independently bounds the daemon’s own graceful shutdown. Linux keeps its existing pidfd lifecycle. This standalone design has no parent HLD.\"\n    },\n    \"hldContextSlice\": {\n      \"frameworkSummary\": \"Standalone feature — no parent HLD. Design directly against the repo, grounded on the s1 analyze passes. There are no HLD shared contracts to honour.\",\n      \"ownedContracts\": [],\n      \"consumedContracts\": [],\n      \"boundary\": {\n        \"storyId\": \"s1\",\n        \"owns\": [],\n        \"depends\": [],\n        \"internal\": \"Restore reliable daemon restart on macOS\"\n      },\n      \"adjacentBoundaries\": [],\n      \"rolloutPhase\": \"standalone\",\n      \"nonFunctional\": {}\n    },\n    \"contractDetails\": {\n      \"surfaceLevel\": \"internal-shared\",\n      \"api\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"stopDaemon\",\n          \"signature\": \"stopDaemon(over?: Partial<StopDeps>): Promise<StopOutcome>\",\n          \"parameters\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"over\",\n              \"type\": \"Partial<StopDeps>\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Existing lifecycle test seam.\",\n              \"optional\": true\n            }\n          ],\n          \"returns\": {\n            \"type\": \"Promise<StopOutcome>\",\n            \"meaning\": \"Exit of the exact original daemon, classified as graceful or forced.\"\n          },\n          \"errors\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"LifecycleError<'ownership-conflict'>\",\n              \"condition\": \"The supervisor peer, installation, expected generation or guarded run files do not match.\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"type\": \"LifecycleError<'exit-unconfirmed'>\",\n              \"condition\": \"The original generation has no confirmed reap within the forced-stop bound.\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"type\": \"LifecycleError<'supervisor-unavailable'>\",\n              \"condition\": \"No authenticated managed supervisor is reachable.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"preconditions\": [\n            \"Resolve the installed daemon and bind the authenticated supervisor to the original generation [[c3]] [[c4]]. Managed Darwin obtains exit evidence from the supervisor’s direct child relationship using waitpid and never calls captureStopTarget or the native process broker [[c9]] [[c10]].\"\n          ],\n          \"postconditions\": [\n            \"A stop receipt alone never authorizes cleanup or restart. Use confirmed child reap and guarded file reconciliation for the exact original generation [[c7]].\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"armShutdownWatchdog\",\n          \"signature\": \"armShutdownWatchdog(options: WatchdogOptions): Promise<ShutdownWatchdog>\",\n          \"parameters\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"options\",\n              \"type\": \"WatchdogOptions\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Existing deadline, diagnostics and readiness settings.\",\n              \"optional\": false\n            }\n          ],\n          \"returns\": {\n            \"type\": \"Promise<ShutdownWatchdog>\",\n            \"meaning\": \"Acknowledged independent watchdog bound to the actual daemon instance.\"\n          },\n          \"errors\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"LifecycleError<'watchdog-timeout'>\",\n              \"condition\": \"No valid arm acknowledgement arrives within the readiness bound.\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"type\": \"LifecycleError<'watchdog-channel'>\",\n              \"condition\": \"The inherited supervisor channel is missing or closes before acknowledgement.\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"type\": \"LifecycleError<'watchdog-protocol'>\",\n              \"condition\": \"The reply version, request correlation or child generation is invalid.\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"preconditions\": [\n            \"Managed Darwin arms the deadline through the authenticated inherited parent channel without launching the native process broker or the independent Linux watchdog helper. brokerPath is only for the existing broker-backed watchdog branch [[c6]] [[c8]] [[c10]].\"\n          ],\n          \"postconditions\": [\n            \"The daemon enters its existing drain only after receiving an acknowledgement for its own generation. The deadline remains armed through native exit hooks [[c5]].\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"dataModelChanges\": [\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"Managed macOS daemon instance\",\n        \"change\": \"new\",\n        \"details\": \"One per-user job per installed state root hosts one supervisor that owns at most one unreaped daemon child. The supervisor spawns the exact absolute built entry and approved Node runtime with explicit working directory and installation environment. The child authenticates over an inherited private channel. The supervisor binds the daemon identity, a fresh generation identifier and the owned PID/socket publication before advertising ready. A single parent event loop serializes signal and reap. Only the unreaped child may be signalled by the supervisor. Controller callers cannot select a PID or move a stop to a replacement generation. An exited child has no termination authority. The TUI startDaemon and restart orchestration use the same installed controller as the shell start and stop bridge [[c4]] [[c11]] [[c13]] [[c14]]. Controller commands resolve the configured installation before connecting. Development mode retains its separately identified route.\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/cli/services/daemon.ts:65\",\n          \"src/cli/services/maintenance.ts\",\n          \"src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts\",\n          \"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh\",\n          \"src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"Supervisor lifecycle and stop receipt\",\n        \"change\": \"new\",\n        \"details\": \"The private control socket is owner-only. Verify peer UID and the endpoint’s owned installation identity. Use an inherited per-child channel for daemon messages. The supervisor accepts child messages only on the channel it created for that child. For controller stop, atomically arm the generation deadline and issue a drain command on that inherited channel. The daemon routes the command into the same shutdown handler as the public RPC and signals [[c4]] [[c5]] [[c13]]. This prevents a delayed controller from sending shutdown to a replacement over the public socket. Direct RPC or signal shutdown arms via the inherited channel. Duplicate stop or arm requests join the first deadline. Deadline and waitpid handling are serialized by the parent. Escalation targets only the currently owned unreaped child. Reap records actual status and forced termination is reported only if escalation was sent and the observed exit matches it. Retain one last confirmed exit until the next generation is bound. Supervisor loss is an error and does not authorize cleanup or replacement without other trusted proof of the original exit.\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/cli/services/daemon.ts\",\n          \"src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/shutdown.ts\",\n          \"src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts\"\n        ],\n        \"schemaDiff\": \"Protocol v1 uses bounded UTF-8 JSON lines, one frame per message, 8 KiB maximum.\\nRequest { v: 1, id: string, op: Op, generation: string | null, deadlineMs?: number }\\nReply { v: 1, id: string, generation: string | null, ok: true, state: State, exit?: Exit }\\n    or { v: 1, id: string, generation: string | null, ok: false, error: LifecycleErrorCode }\\nOp = status | start | stop | ready | arm | drain\\nState = idle | starting | running | draining | exited\\nExit { kind: graceful | forced | failed, code: number | null, signal: number | null }\\nLifecycleErrorCode = ownership-conflict | exit-unconfirmed | supervisor-unavailable |\\n                     watchdog-timeout | watchdog-channel | watchdog-protocol | busy\\n\\nController channel allows status, start and stop. status with null discovers the active or last generation. stop requires the exact active generation. start requires the current exited generation, or null on a fresh empty install. Serialize start and reject stale preconditions. Only one child can be starting or running. Return starting until child readiness and owned run-file publication are verified. Repeated start for the same predecessor returns the already-started generation.\\nInherited channel allows child ready and arm, and supervisor drain. All carry the bound generation. ready supplies no caller-selected PID or paths. arm and stop accept an optional bounded relative deadline and latch the first admitted deadline for the generation. Arm acknowledgement reports draining and never means exited. Use the existing shutdown default when no override is supplied. No request can disarm or extend an admitted deadline.\\nRequests on the wrong channel, invalid fields, missing correlation or mismatched versions are protocol errors. Stale generations are ownership conflicts. Bound pending requests and reject excess as busy. Drop incomplete frames on close. Status reports exited only after waitpid and guarded reconciliation.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"entity\": \"PID and socket run files\",\n        \"change\": \"invariant-change\",\n        \"details\": \"The daemon still publishes the existing PID/socket under the existing ownership guard. The supervisor or controller may remove only the original proven-dead generation’s exact captured files while holding that guard, and refuses changed identities or uncertain ownership. Child supervision is not a replacement for the guard. A failed or missing supervisor produces an explicit degraded result and never authorizes a second daemon. Preserve existing Linux retained-target routing and compatibility with existing clients of daemon.status and daemon.shutdown. The managed Darwin controller and shutdown watchdog use the supervisor, with no call to the Darwin Mach broker and no automatic fallback to it. Linux keeps captureStopTarget, the native pidfd broker and the standalone watchdog helper [[c6]] [[c8]] [[c9]] [[c10]].\",\n        \"callSites\": [\n          \"src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts\",\n          \"src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts\",\n          \"src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"interactionWithShared\": [],\n    \"errorPaths\": {\n      \"errorCases\": [\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"The controller sees a foreign or changed generation.\",\n          \"detection\": \"Peer credentials, installation identity, child generation or guarded file identities do not match.\",\n          \"response\": \"Refuse control, cleanup and replacement. Preserve the foreign owner’s files. Return ownership-conflict.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Report which ownership check failed.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"The daemon is hung before it can accept shutdown or after watchdog readiness.\",\n          \"detection\": \"The supervisor reaches the original monotonic deadline without observing exit.\",\n          \"response\": \"Signal only the still-owned unreaped child. Reap and record actual exit before cleanup or replacement. If exit cannot be verified within the forced-stop bound, withhold restart. Return exit-unconfirmed if the bound expires without a confirmed reap.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Report a forced stop only after confirmed exit.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"The supervisor channel or service disappears.\",\n          \"detection\": \"Channel closes or the expected owner no longer answers its authenticated status request.\",\n          \"response\": \"Treat the outcome as unknown unless positive exit of the original is available. Do not grant a new job authority over an orphan. The daemon retains its existing emergency shutdown path when a verified watchdog cannot be armed. Classify a missing supervisor as supervisor-unavailable. Classify an arm failure as watchdog-channel, watchdog-timeout or watchdog-protocol according to the observed failure.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Report degraded lifecycle and give precise migration or recovery instructions.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"scenario\": \"There is no usable launchd GUI domain or daemon runtime.\",\n          \"detection\": \"The supported user-domain query, absolute runtime validation or registration fails.\",\n          \"response\": \"Report an actionable error before replacing a live install. Leave direct development mode available with existing explicit limitations.\",\n          \"userImpact\": \"Installed restart is withheld without losing the running daemon.\",\n          \"recoverable\": true\n        }\n      ],\n      \"edgeCases\": [\n        {\n          \"input\": \"Concurrent stop and restart, or repeat shutdown while drain is in flight.\",\n          \"expected\": \"Serialize replacement and join the original deadline. Duplicate requests cannot extend the deadline or act on a new generation.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"Graceful exit races the timeout.\",\n          \"expected\": \"The supervisor processes signal and reap in one order. Report forced only if escalation actually caused the recorded exit. A later process cannot receive the signal.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"A legacy or temporary recovery instance is live.\",\n          \"expected\": \"Migrate with cooperative shutdown and verified exit. If a legacy watchdog ends the process before drain, report that outcome accurately. Require explicit operator recovery if a legacy instance is stuck. Do not treat a job label or missing socket as proof of process exit.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"input\": \"A controller stalls after binding the old generation and a different controller replaces it.\",\n          \"expected\": \"The original stop remains scoped to its inherited child channel. The delayed caller receives ownership-conflict for stale generation control and cannot drain or force-stop the replacement.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"invariantsToPreserve\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Startup and cleanup preserve guarded ownership of the published PID and socket pair.\",\n          \"source\": \"c2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"The independent shutdown deadline remains in force through blocked native close or exit hooks.\",\n          \"source\": \"c2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"The shell and CLI confirm the old daemon exited before starting its replacement.\",\n          \"source\": \"c2\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"testStrategy\": {\n      \"testFramework\": \"Node.js node:test via tsx with isolated macOS launchd fixtures\",\n      \"testLevels\": [\n        {\n          \"level\": \"unit\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Verify lifecycle authorization and exact state transitions.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Wrong peer, root and generation\",\n            \"Monotonic deadline, repeated requests and grace versus forced outcome\",\n            \"Guarded stale run files and replacement ownership\",\n            \"Protocol version, frame bounds, channel role and request correlation\",\n            \"Discriminated ownership-conflict, exit-unconfirmed, supervisor-unavailable and watchdog errors\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"integration\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Exercise both entry paths against disposable real supervisor and daemon processes.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Normal CLI and shell restart\",\n            \"IPC and signal shutdown with no controller\",\n            \"Hung before shutdown reply and hung native close after watchdog readiness\",\n            \"Supervisor loss, concurrent restart and stale files\",\n            \"Recovery job and legacy migration\",\n            \"Linux existing pidfd and watchdog suites\",\n            \"Delayed controller cannot stop the replacement generation\",\n            \"Managed Darwin never invokes the Mach broker from either the controller or shutdown watchdog\"\n          ],\n          \"fixturesNeeded\": [\n            \"Unique label and temporary state root\",\n            \"Disposable daemon with delayed drain and independently blocked shutdown fixture\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"level\": \"smoke\",\n          \"purpose\": \"Qualify an installed build on Apple Silicon as a regular user.\",\n          \"subjects\": [\n            \"Install or upgrade into supported launchd domain\",\n            \"Normal and blocked shutdown complete with verified generation change\",\n            \"Restart leaves one daemon and serves status and indexing\",\n            \"Stopped daemon remains stopped and rollback preserves state\",\n            \"On the installed Apple Silicon build recover stale owned PID and socket files and refuse files belonging to a replacement\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"acceptanceMapping\": []\n    },\n    \"migration\": {\n      \"stateBefore\": \"The S1 analyzer bundle identifies the existing stop and watchdog paths that both use the retained broker. The approved ISSUE records the Darwin denial and temporary recovery launch job.\",\n      \"stateAfter\": \"The standard installed macOS daemon is the supervised child of one per-user launch job. The Linux stop and watchdog use their existing retained-target path.\",\n      \"migrationSteps\": [\n        {\n          \"order\": 1,\n          \"action\": \"Validate the new supervisor build and supported launchd domain before taking down the old service. Keep a compatible rollback build available.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 2,\n          \"action\": \"Quiesce the old instance. For the temporary recovery job, verify its service identity and process, stop its daemon via the existing socket, confirm exit and then unload only the exact known recovery job. For a legacy direct process, request cooperative shutdown and verify its exit and captured run-file ownership before admitting the managed job. Abort migration when that evidence is unavailable. A stuck or unverifiable legacy process requires operator recovery under the error-path policy.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 3,\n          \"action\": \"Load the managed job with explicit runtime, work directory and provider discovery environment. Start the supervisor-owned daemon, confirm one live generation, and verify the existing status and indexing paths.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"order\": 4,\n          \"action\": \"If rollout fails, stop the managed generation through its supervisor, verify exit, then unload the owned job and restore the previous installation. Preserve user config and index data. A rollback to the old build restores its known broker limitation.\",\n          \"rollbackable\": true\n        }\n      ],\n      \"backwardCompat\": \"The daemon status and shutdown RPC contracts and current CLI and script commands are retained. Existing state directories and data schemas require no rewrite.\",\n      \"zeroDowntime\": false,\n      \"dataRewriteRequired\": false\n    },\n    \"alternativesConsidered\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a1\",\n        \"name\": \"LaunchAgent with a parent supervisor\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Run the installed daemon as the direct child of a per-user supervisor.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Use launchd to host one installed supervisor and preserve graceful IPC shutdown. The supervisor owns exact child exit and an independent deadline, with control correlated to a specific supervised instance.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Works without a Mach task right.\",\n          \"Independent enforcement survives a blocked daemon event loop.\",\n          \"One owner serializes replacement and can provide exact exit evidence.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"Requires one new launch job and a migration path for existing standalone daemons.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"M\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a2\",\n        \"name\": \"LaunchAgent with direct daemon ownership\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Let launchd directly own the daemon and use launch service operations.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Model each installed daemon as the direct launchd service process and use service identity for recovery alongside existing graceful IPC.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Fewer processes and little packaging overhead.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"Fixed-label service operations must be proven generation-safe; a separate bounded watchdog still needs authority to stop the original instance when shutdown was initiated by an IDE or signal.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"M\",\n        \"reasonRejected\": \"Ordinary stop is plausible but fixed-label forced recovery needs additional instance fencing.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"a3\",\n        \"name\": \"Cooperative drain with exit observation\",\n        \"oneLineSummary\": \"Use the existing socket and observe Darwin process exit.\",\n        \"approach\": \"Use authenticated daemon shutdown IPC and a verified process exit observation, withholding restart if the process never confirms exit.\",\n        \"pros\": [\n          \"Smallest change to ordinary stop and restart.\",\n          \"Uses existing IPC and run-file ownership.\"\n        ],\n        \"cons\": [\n          \"An exit observer cannot enforce a hung shutdown deadline.\"\n        ],\n        \"costEstimate\": \"S\",\n        \"reasonRejected\": \"Preserves safe ordinary exit evidence but cannot recover a blocked process.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"chosenAlternative\": \"a1\",\n    \"openQuestions\": [],\n    \"sequenceDefinition\": {\n      \"id\": \"macos-daemon-restart\",\n      \"narrative\": \"A managed restart binds the current daemon generation before requesting a drain. The independent supervisor owns the deadline and the controller starts a replacement only after exact exit is confirmed.\",\n      \"participants\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ctl\",\n          \"label\": \"CLI or shell\",\n          \"description\": \"Shares the installed lifecycle controller.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Parent supervisor\",\n          \"description\": \"Runs under the per-user LaunchAgent and owns the exact daemon child.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Daemon\",\n          \"description\": \"Handles existing shutdown IPC and closes background work and stores.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"guard\",\n          \"label\": \"Run-file guard\",\n          \"description\": \"Serializes publication and cleanup of the daemon PID and socket.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"messages\": [\n        {\n          \"from\": \"ctl\",\n          \"to\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Bind generation and arm deadline\",\n          \"description\": \"Authenticate the installation and exact generation.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Request graceful shutdown\",\n          \"description\": \"Use the inherited channel bound to the original child. The daemon enters its existing shutdown handler.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"daemon\",\n          \"to\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Confirm watchdog readiness\",\n          \"description\": \"The same arm is idempotent. Direct RPC and signal shutdown can initiate this arm.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"daemon\",\n          \"to\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Drain and close stores\",\n          \"description\": \"Readiness precedes drain and the deadline stays armed through exit hooks.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Escalate if deadline expires\",\n          \"description\": \"Only if the exact child is still owned and unreaped.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Reap original child\",\n          \"description\": \"Record its actual exit.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"guard\",\n          \"label\": \"Reconcile owned run files\",\n          \"description\": \"Check ownership and delete only files proven to belong to the exited child.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"ctl\",\n          \"label\": \"Return confirmed exit\",\n          \"description\": \"A replacement may now be requested.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"ctl\",\n          \"to\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"label\": \"Start replacement generation\",\n          \"description\": \"Serialize launch and wait for daemon readiness.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"from\": \"supervisor\",\n          \"to\": \"daemon\",\n          \"label\": \"Spawn and bind new child\",\n          \"description\": \"Publish one new generation under the existing guard.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"companions\": [\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"diagram-mermaid\",\n        \"relPath\": \"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html\",\n        \"title\": \"Sequence diagram\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c1\",\n      \"kind\": \"prior-artifact\",\n      \"ref\": \"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c2\",\n      \"kind\": \"analyze-bundle\",\n      \"ref\": \"s1:context.assemble\",\n      \"quotedText\": \"The CLI lifecycle and independent watchdog share the retained capability. Run-file ownership guards publication and cleanup.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c3\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c4\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/cli/services/daemon.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c5\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/shutdown.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c6\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c7\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c8\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c9\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c10\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/native/process-broker/darwin.c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c11\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c12\",\n      \"kind\": \"doc\",\n      \"ref\": \"https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPSystemStartup/Chapters/CreatingLaunchdJobs.html\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c13\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"c14\",\n      \"kind\": \"code\",\n      \"ref\": \"src/cli/services/maintenance.ts\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","document":"<!-- insrc:artifact LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1 -->\n\n# LLD: STORY-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1 — Restore reliable daemon restart on macOS\n\n## Summary\n\n**Epic:** `restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos`\n**Design provenance:** Standalone issue design.\n\nRun the installed macOS daemon as the child of a dedicated per-user LaunchAgent supervisor [[c12]]. The supervisor handles shell and CLI/TUI start and stop, and independently bounds the daemon’s own graceful shutdown. Linux keeps its existing pidfd lifecycle. This standalone design has no parent HLD.\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [HLD context](#1-hld-context)\n2. [Contract details](#2-contract-details)\n3. [Data model changes](#3-data-model-changes)\n4. [Diagrams](#4-diagrams)\n5. [Error paths](#5-error-paths)\n6. [Test strategy](#6-test-strategy)\n7. [Migration](#7-migration)\n8. [Alternatives considered](#8-alternatives-considered)\n9. [References](#9-references)\n\n## 1. HLD context\n\n**Framework:** Standalone feature — no parent HLD. Design directly against the repo, grounded on the s1 analyze passes. There are no HLD shared contracts to honour.\n**Rollout phase:** standalone\n\n## 2. Contract details\n\n**Surface level:** internal-shared\n\n### 2.1 `stopDaemon`\n\n```typescript\nstopDaemon(over?: Partial<StopDeps>): Promise<StopOutcome>\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `over: Partial<StopDeps>` _(optional)_ — Existing lifecycle test seam.\n\n**Returns:** `Promise<StopOutcome>` — Exit of the exact original daemon, classified as graceful or forced.\n\n**Errors:**\n- `LifecycleError<'ownership-conflict'>` when The supervisor peer, installation, expected generation or guarded run files do not match.\n- `LifecycleError<'exit-unconfirmed'>` when The original generation has no confirmed reap within the forced-stop bound.\n- `LifecycleError<'supervisor-unavailable'>` when No authenticated managed supervisor is reachable.\n\n**Preconditions:**\n- Resolve the installed daemon and bind the authenticated supervisor to the original generation [[c3]] [[c4]]. Managed Darwin obtains exit evidence from the supervisor’s direct child relationship using waitpid and never calls captureStopTarget or the native process broker [[c9]] [[c10]].\n\n**Postconditions:**\n- A stop receipt alone never authorizes cleanup or restart. Use confirmed child reap and guarded file reconciliation for the exact original generation [[c7]].\n\n### 2.2 `armShutdownWatchdog`\n\n```typescript\narmShutdownWatchdog(options: WatchdogOptions): Promise<ShutdownWatchdog>\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `options: WatchdogOptions` — Existing deadline, diagnostics and readiness settings.\n\n**Returns:** `Promise<ShutdownWatchdog>` — Acknowledged independent watchdog bound to the actual daemon instance.\n\n**Errors:**\n- `LifecycleError<'watchdog-timeout'>` when No valid arm acknowledgement arrives within the readiness bound.\n- `LifecycleError<'watchdog-channel'>` when The inherited supervisor channel is missing or closes before acknowledgement.\n- `LifecycleError<'watchdog-protocol'>` when The reply version, request correlation or child generation is invalid.\n\n**Preconditions:**\n- Managed Darwin arms the deadline through the authenticated inherited parent channel without launching the native process broker or the independent Linux watchdog helper. brokerPath is only for the existing broker-backed watchdog branch [[c6]] [[c8]] [[c10]].\n\n**Postconditions:**\n- The daemon enters its existing drain only after receiving an acknowledgement for its own generation. The deadline remains armed through native exit hooks [[c5]].\n\n## 3. Data model changes\n\n### 3.1 `Managed macOS daemon instance` — new\n\nOne per-user job per installed state root hosts one supervisor that owns at most one unreaped daemon child. The supervisor spawns the exact absolute built entry and approved Node runtime with explicit working directory and installation environment. The child authenticates over an inherited private channel. The supervisor binds the daemon identity, a fresh generation identifier and the owned PID/socket publication before advertising ready. A single parent event loop serializes signal and reap. Only the unreaped child may be signalled by the supervisor. Controller callers cannot select a PID or move a stop to a replacement generation. An exited child has no termination authority. The TUI startDaemon and restart orchestration use the same installed controller as the shell start and stop bridge [[c4]] [[c11]] [[c13]] [[c14]]. Controller commands resolve the configured installation before connecting. Development mode retains its separately identified route.\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/cli/services/daemon.ts:65`\n- `src/cli/services/maintenance.ts`\n- `src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts`\n- `scripts/daemon-ctl.sh`\n- `src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts`\n\n### 3.2 `Supervisor lifecycle and stop receipt` — new\n\nThe private control socket is owner-only. Verify peer UID and the endpoint’s owned installation identity. Use an inherited per-child channel for daemon messages. The supervisor accepts child messages only on the channel it created for that child. For controller stop, atomically arm the generation deadline and issue a drain command on that inherited channel. The daemon routes the command into the same shutdown handler as the public RPC and signals [[c4]] [[c5]] [[c13]]. This prevents a delayed controller from sending shutdown to a replacement over the public socket. Direct RPC or signal shutdown arms via the inherited channel. Duplicate stop or arm requests join the first deadline. Deadline and waitpid handling are serialized by the parent. Escalation targets only the currently owned unreaped child. Reap records actual status and forced termination is reported only if escalation was sent and the observed exit matches it. Retain one last confirmed exit until the next generation is bound. Supervisor loss is an error and does not authorize cleanup or replacement without other trusted proof of the original exit.\n\n```\nProtocol v1 uses bounded UTF-8 JSON lines, one frame per message, 8 KiB maximum.\nRequest { v: 1, id: string, op: Op, generation: string | null, deadlineMs?: number }\nReply { v: 1, id: string, generation: string | null, ok: true, state: State, exit?: Exit }\n    or { v: 1, id: string, generation: string | null, ok: false, error: LifecycleErrorCode }\nOp = status | start | stop | ready | arm | drain\nState = idle | starting | running | draining | exited\nExit { kind: graceful | forced | failed, code: number | null, signal: number | null }\nLifecycleErrorCode = ownership-conflict | exit-unconfirmed | supervisor-unavailable |\n                     watchdog-timeout | watchdog-channel | watchdog-protocol | busy\n\nController channel allows status, start and stop. status with null discovers the active or last generation. stop requires the exact active generation. start requires the current exited generation, or null on a fresh empty install. Serialize start and reject stale preconditions. Only one child can be starting or running. Return starting until child readiness and owned run-file publication are verified. Repeated start for the same predecessor returns the already-started generation.\nInherited channel allows child ready and arm, and supervisor drain. All carry the bound generation. ready supplies no caller-selected PID or paths. arm and stop accept an optional bounded relative deadline and latch the first admitted deadline for the generation. Arm acknowledgement reports draining and never means exited. Use the existing shutdown default when no override is supplied. No request can disarm or extend an admitted deadline.\nRequests on the wrong channel, invalid fields, missing correlation or mismatched versions are protocol errors. Stale generations are ownership conflicts. Bound pending requests and reject excess as busy. Drop incomplete frames on close. Status reports exited only after waitpid and guarded reconciliation.\n```\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/cli/services/daemon.ts`\n- `src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts`\n- `src/daemon/shutdown.ts`\n- `src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts`\n\n### 3.3 `PID and socket run files` — invariant-change\n\nThe daemon still publishes the existing PID/socket under the existing ownership guard. The supervisor or controller may remove only the original proven-dead generation’s exact captured files while holding that guard, and refuses changed identities or uncertain ownership. Child supervision is not a replacement for the guard. A failed or missing supervisor produces an explicit degraded result and never authorizes a second daemon. Preserve existing Linux retained-target routing and compatibility with existing clients of daemon.status and daemon.shutdown. The managed Darwin controller and shutdown watchdog use the supervisor, with no call to the Darwin Mach broker and no automatic fallback to it. Linux keeps captureStopTarget, the native pidfd broker and the standalone watchdog helper [[c6]] [[c8]] [[c9]] [[c10]].\n\n**Call sites:**\n- `src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts`\n- `src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts`\n- `src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts`\n\n## 4. Diagrams\n\n- [Sequence diagram](docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html)\n\n## 5. Error paths\n\n**Error cases**\n\n- **The controller sees a foreign or changed generation.** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Peer credentials, installation identity, child generation or guarded file identities do not match.\n  - Response: Refuse control, cleanup and replacement. Preserve the foreign owner’s files. Return ownership-conflict.\n  - User impact: Report which ownership check failed.\n- **The daemon is hung before it can accept shutdown or after watchdog readiness.** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: The supervisor reaches the original monotonic deadline without observing exit.\n  - Response: Signal only the still-owned unreaped child. Reap and record actual exit before cleanup or replacement. If exit cannot be verified within the forced-stop bound, withhold restart. Return exit-unconfirmed if the bound expires without a confirmed reap.\n  - User impact: Report a forced stop only after confirmed exit.\n- **The supervisor channel or service disappears.** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: Channel closes or the expected owner no longer answers its authenticated status request.\n  - Response: Treat the outcome as unknown unless positive exit of the original is available. Do not grant a new job authority over an orphan. The daemon retains its existing emergency shutdown path when a verified watchdog cannot be armed. Classify a missing supervisor as supervisor-unavailable. Classify an arm failure as watchdog-channel, watchdog-timeout or watchdog-protocol according to the observed failure.\n  - User impact: Report degraded lifecycle and give precise migration or recovery instructions.\n- **There is no usable launchd GUI domain or daemon runtime.** (recoverable)\n  - Detection: The supported user-domain query, absolute runtime validation or registration fails.\n  - Response: Report an actionable error before replacing a live install. Leave direct development mode available with existing explicit limitations.\n  - User impact: Installed restart is withheld without losing the running daemon.\n\n**Edge cases**\n\n| Input | Expected |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Concurrent stop and restart, or repeat shutdown while drain is in flight. | Serialize replacement and join the original deadline. Duplicate requests cannot extend the deadline or act on a new generation. |\n| Graceful exit races the timeout. | The supervisor processes signal and reap in one order. Report forced only if escalation actually caused the recorded exit. A later process cannot receive the signal. |\n| A legacy or temporary recovery instance is live. | Migrate with cooperative shutdown and verified exit. If a legacy watchdog ends the process before drain, report that outcome accurately. Require explicit operator recovery if a legacy instance is stuck. Do not treat a job label or missing socket as proof of process exit. |\n| A controller stalls after binding the old generation and a different controller replaces it. | The original stop remains scoped to its inherited child channel. The delayed caller receives ownership-conflict for stale generation control and cannot drain or force-stop the replacement. |\n\n**Invariants to preserve**\n\n- Startup and cleanup preserve guarded ownership of the published PID and socket pair. [[c2]]\n- The independent shutdown deadline remains in force through blocked native close or exit hooks. [[c2]]\n- The shell and CLI confirm the old daemon exited before starting its replacement. [[c2]]\n\n## 6. Test strategy\n\n**Test framework:** `Node.js node:test via tsx with isolated macOS launchd fixtures`\n\n**Test levels**\n\n- **unit** — Verify lifecycle authorization and exact state transitions.\n  - Subjects: `Wrong peer, root and generation`, `Monotonic deadline, repeated requests and grace versus forced outcome`, `Guarded stale run files and replacement ownership`, `Protocol version, frame bounds, channel role and request correlation`, `Discriminated ownership-conflict, exit-unconfirmed, supervisor-unavailable and watchdog errors`\n- **integration** — Exercise both entry paths against disposable real supervisor and daemon processes.\n  - Subjects: `Normal CLI and shell restart`, `IPC and signal shutdown with no controller`, `Hung before shutdown reply and hung native close after watchdog readiness`, `Supervisor loss, concurrent restart and stale files`, `Recovery job and legacy migration`, `Linux existing pidfd and watchdog suites`, `Delayed controller cannot stop the replacement generation`, `Managed Darwin never invokes the Mach broker from either the controller or shutdown watchdog`\n  - Fixtures: `Unique label and temporary state root`, `Disposable daemon with delayed drain and independently blocked shutdown fixture`\n- **smoke** — Qualify an installed build on Apple Silicon as a regular user.\n  - Subjects: `Install or upgrade into supported launchd domain`, `Normal and blocked shutdown complete with verified generation change`, `Restart leaves one daemon and serves status and indexing`, `Stopped daemon remains stopped and rollback preserves state`, `On the installed Apple Silicon build recover stale owned PID and socket files and refuse files belonging to a replacement`\n\n## 7. Migration\n\n**State before:** The S1 analyzer bundle identifies the existing stop and watchdog paths that both use the retained broker. The approved ISSUE records the Darwin denial and temporary recovery launch job.\n\n**State after:** The standard installed macOS daemon is the supervised child of one per-user launch job. The Linux stop and watchdog use their existing retained-target path.\n\n**Zero downtime:** no — **Data rewrite:** no\n\n**Steps**\n\n1. Validate the new supervisor build and supported launchd domain before taking down the old service. Keep a compatible rollback build available. — ↩ rollbackable\n2. Quiesce the old instance. For the temporary recovery job, verify its service identity and process, stop its daemon via the existing socket, confirm exit and then unload only the exact known recovery job. For a legacy direct process, request cooperative shutdown and verify its exit and captured run-file ownership before admitting the managed job. Abort migration when that evidence is unavailable. A stuck or unverifiable legacy process requires operator recovery under the error-path policy. — ↩ rollbackable\n3. Load the managed job with explicit runtime, work directory and provider discovery environment. Start the supervisor-owned daemon, confirm one live generation, and verify the existing status and indexing paths. — ↩ rollbackable\n4. If rollout fails, stop the managed generation through its supervisor, verify exit, then unload the owned job and restore the previous installation. Preserve user config and index data. A rollback to the old build restores its known broker limitation. — ↩ rollbackable\n\n**Backward compat:** The daemon status and shutdown RPC contracts and current CLI and script commands are retained. Existing state directories and data schemas require no rewrite.\n\n## 8. Alternatives considered\n\n### 8.1 a1: LaunchAgent with a parent supervisor — **CHOSEN**\n\nRun the installed daemon as the direct child of a per-user supervisor.\n\nUse launchd to host one installed supervisor and preserve graceful IPC shutdown. The supervisor owns exact child exit and an independent deadline, with control correlated to a specific supervised instance.\n\n### 8.2 a2: LaunchAgent with direct daemon ownership\n\nLet launchd directly own the daemon and use launch service operations.\n\nModel each installed daemon as the direct launchd service process and use service identity for recovery alongside existing graceful IPC.\n\n**Rejected because:** Ordinary stop is plausible but fixed-label forced recovery needs additional instance fencing.\n\n### 8.3 a3: Cooperative drain with exit observation\n\nUse the existing socket and observe Darwin process exit.\n\nUse authenticated daemon shutdown IPC and a verified process exit observation, withholding restart if the process never confirms exit.\n\n**Rejected because:** Preserves safe ordinary exit evidence but cannot recover a blocked process.\n\n## 9. References\n\n- **[[c1]]** `prior-artifact` `docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md`\n- **[[c2]]** `analyze-bundle` `s1:context.assemble` — \"The CLI lifecycle and independent watchdog share the retained capability. Run-file ownership guards publication and cleanup.\"\n- **[[c3]]** `code` `src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts`\n- **[[c4]]** `code` `src/cli/services/daemon.ts`\n- **[[c5]]** `code` `src/daemon/shutdown.ts`\n- **[[c6]]** `code` `src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts`\n- **[[c7]]** `code` `src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts`\n- **[[c8]]** `code` `src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts`\n- **[[c9]]** `code` `src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts`\n- **[[c10]]** `code` `src/native/process-broker/darwin.c`\n- **[[c11]]** `code` `scripts/daemon-ctl.sh`\n- **[[c12]]** `doc` `https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/MacOSX/Conceptual/BPSystemStartup/Chapters/CreatingLaunchdJobs.html`\n- **[[c13]]** `code` `src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts`\n- **[[c14]]** `code` `src/cli/services/maintenance.ts`\n"},"capturedAt":"2026-11-10T09:32:46.507Z"},"inventory":{"units":[{"id":"/body/summary","selector":"/body/summary","title":"summary","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/hldContextSlice","selector":"/body/hldContextSlice","title":"hldContextSlice","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/contractDetails","selector":"/body/contractDetails","title":"contractDetails","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/dataModelChanges","selector":"/body/dataModelChanges","title":"dataModelChanges","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/interactionWithShared","selector":"/body/interactionWithShared","title":"interactionWithShared","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/errorPaths","selector":"/body/errorPaths","title":"errorPaths","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/testStrategy","selector":"/body/testStrategy","title":"testStrategy","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/migration","selector":"/body/migration","title":"migration","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered","title":"alternativesConsidered","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/chosenAlternative","selector":"/body/chosenAlternative","title":"chosenAlternative","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/openQuestions","selector":"/body/openQuestions","title":"openQuestions","kind":"section"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition","title":"sequenceDefinition","kind":"visual"},{"id":"/body/companions","selector":"/body/companions","title":"companions","kind":"visual"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered/0","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered/0","title":"a1","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered/1","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered/1","title":"a2","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/alternativesConsidered/2","selector":"/body/alternativesConsidered/2","title":"a3","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","title":"ctl","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","title":"supervisor","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","title":"daemon","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","selector":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","title":"guard","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"/citations/0","selector":"/citations/0","title":"Citation 1","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/1","selector":"/citations/1","title":"Citation 2","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/2","selector":"/citations/2","title":"Citation 3","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/3","selector":"/citations/3","title":"Citation 4","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/4","selector":"/citations/4","title":"Citation 5","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/5","selector":"/citations/5","title":"Citation 6","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/6","selector":"/citations/6","title":"Citation 7","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/7","selector":"/citations/7","title":"Citation 8","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/8","selector":"/citations/8","title":"Citation 9","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/9","selector":"/citations/9","title":"Citation 10","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/10","selector":"/citations/10","title":"Citation 11","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/11","selector":"/citations/11","title":"Citation 12","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/12","selector":"/citations/12","title":"Citation 13","kind":"citation"},{"id":"/citations/13","selector":"/citations/13","title":"Citation 14","kind":"citation"},{"id":"document:line:24","selector":"document:line:24","title":"1. HLD context","kind":"section"},{"id":"document:line:125","selector":"document:line:125","title":"4. Diagrams","kind":"section"}]},"completedChunks":[{"chunkId":"c1-summary-context","status":"passed","summary":"The Summary and §1 HLD context framing accurately reflect a standalone, no-parent-HLD design and correctly bound the fix to macOS lifecycle while preserving Linux behavior. The ISSUE's fix intent (same-user lifecycle avoiding the native task-right broker, bounded recovery, verified exit, guarded ownership, CLI/shell/daemon coverage, Linux preservation, migration evaluation) is traceable to this document, though most of the detail lives in later sections (contracts, invariants, migration) rather than being restated in the two-paragraph Summary — which is normal for an LLD summary and not a defect. No fabricated HLD contracts are present; §1 explicitly disclaims any parent HLD/shared contracts.","findings":[],"inspectedReferences":["/body/summary","/body/hldContextSlice","document:line:24","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md"],"limitations":[],"advice":["The Summary's `**Epic:** restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos` field label sits immediately above `**Design provenance:** Standalone issue design.` and `This standalone design has no parent HLD.` The field is evidently reusing the epic-slug/identifier namespace rather than asserting a real Epic/HLD exists (consistent with the sibling EPIC-3992d2e1e26124dc artifact used purely as an identifier wrapper for the standalone issue), but a reader skimming only the Summary could momentarily read 'Epic:' as implying Epic-level ancestry before reaching the very next line that disclaims it. Consider relabeling that field (e.g. 'Scope id:' or 'Standalone id:') to remove the ambiguity, though this is cosmetic and not a traceability or contract defect.","The Summary does not explicitly restate the ISSUE's specific trigger ('without requiring a debugger task right' / Mach task-right denial) — it is only made concrete later in §2.1's precondition ('never calls captureStopTarget or the native process broker') and §9's citations. Readers relying on the Summary alone would not see the causal link to the original denial; an optional one-clause addition to the Summary (e.g. 'avoiding the native task-right broker that macOS denies') would tighten the traceability without changing scope."]},{"chunkId":"c2-contracts","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog contracts in §2 are internally plausible and the preconditions correctly state that managed Darwin never calls captureStopTarget or the native process broker, matching the ISSUE's root-cause (Mach task-right denial via src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts and src/native/process-broker/darwin.c). Citations [[c3]][[c4]][[c6]][[c7]][[c8]][[c9]][[c10]] point to plausible call sites given repo conventions (src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts and daemon.ts host the current stopDaemon/capture logic; src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts and src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts are the watchdog paths being replaced; darwin.c is the native broker). However, the three-variant LifecycleError sets for each function do not fully cover the failure modes the document itself describes: a documented error case (no usable launchd domain/runtime) and a documented wire-level error code (`busy`) have no corresponding contract-level discriminant, and the `LifecycleError<Code>` type's own shape (fields beyond the discriminant) is never specified.","findings":[{"id":"c2-f1","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §2.1 stopDaemon Errors / §5 Error paths case 2","message":"stopDaemon's three LifecycleError variants (ownership-conflict, exit-unconfirmed, supervisor-unavailable) do not account for the case where there is no active generation to stop at all (nothing running). The current real implementation (src/cli/services/daemon.ts:159, stopDaemonWith) throws a distinct 'daemon is not running' error for exactly this case, and §3.2's own protocol says 'stop requires the exact active generation' without stating what happens when none exists. It is unspecified whether that maps to ownership-conflict, a new code, or something else, leaving the contract incomplete for a real, currently-handled failure mode.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:44-47","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:105","src/cli/services/daemon.ts:156-159"]},{"id":"c2-f2","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §5 Error paths case 4 vs §2 contractDetails and §3.2 LifecycleErrorCode enum","message":"Error-paths case 4 ('There is no usable launchd GUI domain or daemon runtime') is a documented recoverable failure with its own detection/response/user-impact, but it has no corresponding LifecycleErrorCode in the §3.2 protocol enum and no function contract anywhere in §2 that could surface it (it belongs to neither stopDaemon nor armShutdownWatchdog). The error-handling surface for this case is therefore entirely unspecified at the contract level despite being called out as a first-class error path.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:145-148","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:102-103"]},{"id":"c2-f3","severity":"LOW","location":"S001/LLD.md §3.2 protocol LifecycleErrorCode enum vs §2.1/§2.2 Errors lists","message":"The wire protocol's LifecycleErrorCode union includes `busy` ('Bound pending requests and reject excess as busy'), stated generically for both the controller and inherited channels, but neither stopDaemon's nor armShutdownWatchdog's documented Errors list includes a busy variant, and the text never clarifies whether a controller `stop` call (which stopDaemon issues) can ever observe `busy`.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:102-107","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:44-47","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:66-69"]},{"id":"c2-f4","severity":"LOW","location":"S001/LLD.md §2.1-2.2 Errors (LifecycleError<Code> usage)","message":"Both functions' Errors sections use the generic form `LifecycleError<'code'>` as the thrown/rejected type, but the LLD never defines the LifecycleError type's own shape (e.g. whether it carries the discriminant, a message, the generation id, or diagnostic detail) anywhere in the document, leaving the error contract's concrete structure unspecified for implementation.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:44-47","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:66-69"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/contractDetails","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","src/cli/services/daemon.ts","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","src/daemon/shutdown.ts","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c (existence confirmed via glob)"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Consider explicitly stating in §2.1 what stopDaemon does when no generation is currently active (idle/exited state) — either a fourth discriminant or an explicit note that this is a no-op success rather than an error.","Consider adding a LifecycleErrorCode (or explicitly stating out-of-scope) for the launchd-domain-unavailable case in §5 so every documented error path has a contract-level home.","A short inline type sketch for LifecycleError<Code> (e.g. { code: Code; message: string; generation?: string }) would remove ambiguity for the builder."]},{"chunkId":"c3-data-model","status":"incomplete","summary":"test","findings":[{"id":"test1","severity":"LOW","location":"x","message":"y","evidenceReferences":["z"]}],"inspectedReferences":["a"],"limitations":["Required coverage not reported: /body/dataModelChanges","Required coverage not reported: /body/interactionWithShared","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md"],"advice":[]},{"chunkId":"c4-diagrams","status":"incomplete","summary":"The sequenceDefinition's 10 messages and 4 participants are reproduced byte-for-byte in the generated companion sequence-diagram.html (a valid mermaid sequenceDiagram block), and the Diagrams section at LLD.md:127 links to the exact companions[0].relPath with the matching title. The shutdown/teardown half of the flow (bind generation, drain, escalate, reap, reconcile run files, return exit) is fully consistent with the stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog contracts and the 3.1-3.3 data model. Two gaps were found in the startup half of the flow and in the watchdog-arm message's directionality. I could not execute or render the HTML in a browser/headless renderer, so visual correctness beyond the embedded source text is not confirmed — this is reported as a limitation rather than a pass.","findings":[{"id":"f1-startup-ready-guard-gap","severity":"MED","location":"/body/sequenceDefinition/messages/8-9 (\"Start replacement generation\", \"Spawn and bind new child\"); LLD.md ## 3.1 (lines 79-89) and ## 3.2 protocol block (lines 94-108)","message":"The new-generation startup portion of the sequence diagram is compressed to two messages (ctl->supervisor 'Start replacement generation', supervisor->daemon 'Spawn and bind new child') and stops there. It omits the child's 'ready' handshake back to the supervisor and the run-file guard's publication of the new PID/socket pair, even though data model 3.1 states the supervisor 'binds the daemon identity, a fresh generation identifier and the owned PID/socket publication before advertising ready', and 3.2's protocol explicitly defines 'ready' as an inherited-channel op and says start 'Return[s] starting until child readiness and owned run-file publication are verified.' The teardown half of the same diagram shows the equivalent guard interaction explicitly (message 7, 'Reconcile owned run files', supervisor->guard), so the two halves of the lifecycle are documented at different levels of detail and the diagram does not fully trace the startup contract onto the 'guard' participant it declares.","evidenceReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition.messages[8]","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition.messages[9]","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.dataModelChanges[0].details","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:81","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:105-106","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html:110-111"]},{"id":"f2-watchdog-arm-direction-ambiguous","severity":"LOW","location":"/body/sequenceDefinition/messages/2 (\"Confirm watchdog readiness\", daemon->supervisor); LLD.md ## 2.2 postcondition (line 75) and ## 3.2 protocol block (lines 105-106)","message":"The diagram's third message is daemon->supervisor labelled 'Confirm watchdog readiness', immediately followed by daemon->daemon 'Drain and close stores' with no intervening supervisor->daemon message. But armShutdownWatchdog's postcondition (2.2, line 75) says 'The daemon enters its existing drain only after receiving an acknowledgement for its own generation', and the protocol (3.2) models this as the child sending 'arm' and the supervisor replying with an admitted-deadline acknowledgement on the same inherited channel. As drawn, the single daemon->supervisor arrow reads as the daemon confirming readiness to the supervisor rather than the daemon requesting an arm and waiting on the supervisor's ack before draining, so the diagram's directionality does not visibly carry the 'receiving an acknowledgement' precondition for drain.","evidenceReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition.messages[2]","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition.messages[3]","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:75","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:106","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html:103-105"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/sequenceDefinition","/body/companions","document:line:125","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.companions","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.dataModelChanges","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html:96-111","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md"],"limitations":["Could not render docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html in a browser or headless renderer to confirm the mermaid diagram actually renders without error or truncation; no browser/screenshot tool was available in this review session. Verification was limited to confirming the embedded mermaid 'sequenceDiagram' source text (sequence-diagram.html:96-111) is byte-for-byte consistent with body.sequenceDefinition's participants and messages in the canonical artifact — this is source-level, not rendered-output, verification."],"advice":["Add the child's 'ready' handshake and the run-file guard's publication of the new PID/socket pair to the sequenceDefinition's startup messages, so the new-generation half of the flow carries the same level of guard/readiness detail as the teardown half and visibly satisfies data model 3.1/3.2's 'owned PID/socket publication before advertising ready' requirement.","Consider adding an explicit supervisor->daemon acknowledgement message after 'Confirm watchdog readiness' (or renaming/re-pointing that message to read as the daemon's arm request), so the diagram visibly carries the 'daemon enters drain only after receiving an acknowledgement' precondition from contract 2.2 rather than relying on an implicit synchronous-call convention.","When a rendering/screenshot capability is available, capture an actual render of sequence-diagram.html to close the visual-verification gap noted in limitations."]},{"chunkId":"c5-error-test","status":"incomplete","summary":"test","findings":[{"id":"f1","severity":"MED","location":"a","message":"b","evidenceReferences":["c"]}],"inspectedReferences":["a","b"],"limitations":["Required coverage not reported: /body/errorPaths","Required coverage not reported: /body/testStrategy","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md"],"advice":[]},{"chunkId":"c6-migration","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The migration plan correctly sequences quiescing the recovery LaunchAgent / legacy process before admitting the managed job, and the backward-compat claim for daemon.status/daemon.shutdown RPC contracts and existing CLI/script commands is supported by the current code (rpc('daemon.status')/rpc('daemon.shutdown') call sites in src/cli/services/daemon.ts, and the already-existing src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts + scripts/daemon-ctl.sh entry points). However, Step 2 is marked rollbackable but no rollback procedure is actually given for it: the only rollback described (Step 4) reverses Step 3 (stop the managed generation, unload the job, restore the previous installation) and never addresses resurrecting or re-verifying the quiesced legacy/recovery instance. Issuing cooperative shutdown to the legacy instance in Step 2 is also not cleanly reversible once sent, which conflicts with marking the step \"↩ rollbackable\" the same way as Steps 1 and 3 (which take no irreversible action against a live process). Additionally, the migration's \"verify its exit\" step for a legacy direct process does not state which exit-evidence mechanism it uses; the ISSUE's root cause is specifically that the native process-capability broker (captureStopTarget / src/native/process-broker/darwin.c) is denied on this exact scenario, and the LLD elsewhere (§3.3) explicitly bars the new managed Darwin path from falling back to that broker, but never extends that same no-broker guarantee to the migration's legacy-process verification step, leaving the mechanism for this specific verification ambiguous at the one point where the original bug would otherwise reproduce.","findings":[{"id":"f1-migration-rollback-gap","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §7 Migration, Steps 2 and 4","message":"Step 2 (quiesce the old instance / recovery LaunchAgent or legacy direct process) is marked '↩ rollbackable', but Step 4 — the document's only described rollback procedure — only reverses Step 3 (stop the managed generation, unload the owned job, restore the previous installation). No step describes how to resurrect or recover the quiesced legacy/recovery instance if migration is aborted after Step 2 but before Step 3 succeeds, or what state the operator is left in. The error-path table's own guidance ('a stuck or unverifiable legacy process requires operator recovery') suggests this is not actually a clean rollback, but the Step-2 label does not say so or point to that fallback.","evidenceReferences":["S001/LLD.md:189-193 (Migration Steps 1-4)","S001/LLD.md:156 (edge-case row: 'A legacy or temporary recovery instance is live... Require explicit operator recovery if a legacy instance is stuck.')"]},{"id":"f2-migration-broker-ambiguity","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §7 Migration, Step 2 vs §3.3","message":"Step 2's verification of a legacy direct process's exit ('request cooperative shutdown and verify its exit... before admitting the managed job') does not name the exit-evidence mechanism. The ISSUE's root cause is exactly that the native process-capability broker (src/native/process-broker/darwin.c, invoked via captureStopTarget in src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts) is denied for this same-user Darwin scenario. Section 3.3 explicitly states the new managed Darwin controller/watchdog 'never call[s] the Darwin Mach broker and no automatic fallback to it', but that guarantee is stated only for the already-managed path, not for the migration's one-time legacy-process exit check — the one place where reusing the broken broker would silently reproduce the bug being fixed.","evidenceReferences":["S001/LLD.md:190 (Step 2)","S001/LLD.md:118 (§3.3 'no call to the Darwin Mach broker and no automatic fallback to it')","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts:32,61-80 (BROKER constant, native broker spawn)","docs/standalone/.../ISSUE.md:9-11 (root cause: Darwin Mach task-right denial)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/migration","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/cli/services/daemon.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/shared/daemon-process.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/scripts/daemon-ctl.sh"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Name the specific exit-verification mechanism used in Migration Step 2 for a legacy (non-supervisor-child) process — e.g. PID-liveness polling via readProcessIdentity/ps (src/shared/daemon-process.ts), which already avoids the native broker — so the migration step's feasibility is traceable rather than implied.","Either give Step 2 an explicit rollback/recovery procedure (even if it is simply 'treat as the stuck-legacy-process error path and require operator recovery') or drop the '↩ rollbackable' marker from Step 2 to avoid implying symmetry with Steps 1 and 3, which take no irreversible action."]},{"chunkId":"c7-alternatives","status":"changes_requested","summary":"chosenAlternative (a1) is correctly cross-referenced to section 8.1's CHOSEN marker and substantively satisfies the ISSUE's fix intent (no Mach task right, bounded recovery via independent supervisor deadline, verified exit before replacement, guarded PID/socket ownership, migration from the legacy/temporary-recovery instance). a3's rejection is well-grounded in the fix intent's \"bounded recovery when hung\" requirement. a2's rejection is defensible but its stated rationale (instance fencing) understates that the same root-cause native-broker denial likely recurs for IDE/signal-initiated (watchdog) stops under a2, which is a stronger reason than fencing alone. The empty openQuestions list is not fully credible given unresolved operational gaps this design itself surfaces: an \"abort migration... requires operator recovery\" step with no defined recovery procedure, and a data model that scopes one supervisor \"per installed state root\" without addressing concurrent multi-install-root behavior.","findings":[{"id":"c7alt-a2root","severity":"LOW","location":"/body/alternativesConsidered/1 (a2 reasonRejected); LLD.md section 8.2","message":"a2's reasonRejected ('Ordinary stop is plausible but fixed-label forced recovery needs additional instance fencing') frames the rejection as a fencing/safety gap rather than tying it to the ISSUE's actual root cause. The listed con itself says 'a separate bounded watchdog still needs authority to stop the original instance when shutdown was initiated by an IDE or signal' — i.e. under a2 the watchdog-initiated (forced) stop path would still depend on the same native capability whose denial is the ISSUE's root cause (Mach task right acquisition failing with errno 1). The rationale should say explicitly that a2 only moves the problem for ordinary stop and leaves the root-cause broker dependency unresolved for forced/watchdog-initiated stops, not merely that it 'needs fencing'.","evidenceReferences":["/body/alternativesConsidered/1","ISSUE.md Root cause: 'Both the CLI stop path and the daemon's independent shutdown watchdog require the same native retained process capability... A permission denial is reported... The daemon itself also arms the watchdog before stopping background work, so capture failure triggers emergency exit before the graceful drain.'","LLD.md section 8.2"]},{"id":"c7alt-openq","severity":"MED","location":"/body/openQuestions; LLD.md section 7 (Migration) step 2 and section 3.1","message":"openQuestions is empty, but the design itself surfaces at least two unresolved operational questions that this bugfix-sized LLD does not answer: (1) Migration step 2 states 'Abort migration when that evidence is unavailable... A stuck or unverifiable legacy process requires operator recovery under the error-path policy' but no error-path case or section defines what that operator recovery procedure actually is (manual kill? reinstall? documented runbook?) — this is left entirely unspecified. (2) Section 3.1 models 'One per-user job per installed state root' implying more than one installed state root can exist on a machine (e.g. dev vs. production install, or multiple Node/installation roots), yet neither the contract details, data model, nor test strategy address what happens when two installed state roots' supervisors or run-file guards interact (collision, isolation guarantees, or explicit non-goal). Given the protocol and migration complexity already present in this LLD, suppressing both as non-issues via an empty openQuestions list is not credible; at least one should be recorded as open (or explicitly scoped out as a non-goal) rather than silently assumed.","evidenceReferences":["/body/openQuestions","LLD.md section 7 step 2: 'A stuck or unverifiable legacy process requires operator recovery under the error-path policy.'","LLD.md section 3.1: 'One per-user job per installed state root hosts one supervisor...'","LLD.md section 5 error paths (no case matches 'operator recovery' procedure)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/alternativesConsidered","/body/alternativesConsidered/0","/body/alternativesConsidered/1","/body/alternativesConsidered/2","/body/chosenAlternative","/body/openQuestions","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.alternativesConsidered","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.chosenAlternative","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.openQuestions","LLD.md section 2 (Contract details, 2.1/2.2) for cross-check against a1's claimed pros","LLD.md section 3 (Data model changes) for cross-check against a1's claimed pros and a2/a3's cons","LLD.md section 7 (Migration) for cross-check of a1's migration-path con and the fix intent's migration-evaluation requirement","LLD.md section 5 (Error paths) for cross-check of bounded-recovery claims used to reject a3"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Consider recording at least one explicit open question (e.g. the operator-recovery procedure for a stuck legacy instance during migration, or multi-install-root isolation) rather than leaving openQuestions empty — even if the answer is 'out of scope for this bugfix', stating that explicitly is more defensible than silence.","Strengthen a2's reasonRejected to name the recurring native-broker/Mach-task-right dependency for watchdog-initiated stops directly, so the rejection traces unambiguously back to the ISSUE's root cause rather than reading as a generic fencing concern."]},{"chunkId":"c9-citations","status":"incomplete","summary":"Eleven of fourteen citations resolve to real files whose cited roles match actual responsibilities, and [[c1]]/[[c12]] are appropriately used. However, §3.3 mis-cites [[c10]] (darwin.c, the Darwin Mach-port broker) in support of the \"native pidfd broker\" claim, when the actual Linux pidfd implementation lives in a separate, uncited file (src/native/process-broker/linux.c). Two citations ([[c2]]'s analyze-bundle quote and [[c12]]'s external URL) could not be fully verified against their original sources, so the result is incomplete per the precedence rule even though it also carries a MED finding.","findings":[{"id":"c9-cite-linux-broker-mismatch","severity":"MED","location":"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:118 (§3.3, 'PID and socket run files')","message":"Section 3.3 states 'Linux keeps captureStopTarget, the native pidfd broker and the standalone watchdog helper [[c6]] [[c8]] [[c9]] [[c10]]' and cites [[c10]] as support for the Linux pidfd broker. But [[c10]] is defined in References as src/native/process-broker/darwin.c, which is exclusively the Darwin Mach-task broker (uses mach_port_t, task_for_pid, bsm/libbsm.h; no pidfd logic). The actual Linux pidfd implementation is a separate file, src/native/process-broker/linux.c (struct linux_capability { int fd; }, uses poll()/syscall pidfd calls), which exists in the repo but is never cited anywhere in the LLD's References. The same [[c10]] citation is used correctly elsewhere (§2.1, §2.2) to describe the Darwin-specific broker being avoided by the managed path, so the §3.3 reuse for the Linux broker is an internal inconsistency, not a one-off typo across the whole doc.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:118","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:231","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c:1-20","src/native/process-broker/linux.c:1-30"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/citations/0","/citations/1","/citations/2","/citations/3","/citations/4","/citations/5","/citations/6","/citations/7","/citations/8","/citations/9","/citations/10","/citations/11","/citations/12","/citations/13","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","src/cli/services/daemon.ts","src/daemon/shutdown.ts","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c","src/native/process-broker/linux.c","scripts/daemon-ctl.sh","src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts","src/cli/services/maintenance.ts"],"limitations":["Could not retrieve or cross-check the original s1:context.assemble analyze-bundle artifact itself to confirm [[c2]]'s quoted text ('The CLI lifecycle and independent watchdog share the retained capability. Run-file ownership guards publication and cleanup.') is a verbatim excerpt of that bundle; only assessed that the quote's substance matches current code behavior (captureStopTarget shared by lifecycle.ts and the watchdog helper; run-file-ownership.ts guarding publication/cleanup).","Did not fetch the live external URL for [[c12]] (https://developer.apple.com/.../CreatingLaunchdJobs.html) to confirm it currently resolves and covers per-user LaunchAgent job creation; assessed only topical appropriateness of an Apple launchd document as authority for the LaunchAgent approach."],"advice":["Add a dedicated citation for src/native/process-broker/linux.c and use it (instead of [[c10]]) wherever the LLD specifically describes the Linux pidfd broker, reserving [[c10]] for Darwin-Mach-broker-specific claims.","Consider qualifying [[c8]] (src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts)'s description as 'the Linux watchdog helper' / 'the standalone watchdog helper' with a note that the file itself is currently platform-agnostic (no Darwin/Linux branch) and only becomes Linux-exclusive once the managed-Darwin path in this design stops invoking it — the file's present form doesn't yet encode that restriction."]},{"chunkId":"c10-final-consistency","status":"incomplete","summary":"The LLD, taken as a whole, substantively resolves the ISSUE's root cause and fix intent: it replaces the native Mach-task-right/broker dependency with a per-user LaunchAgent supervisor that obtains exit evidence via waitpid on its direct child, preserves bounded recovery (deadline/escalation), requires verified exit before replacement, keeps the existing PID/socket ownership guard, covers CLI/shell/TUI/daemon-direct entry points, explicitly preserves Linux's pidfd path, and includes a migration plan from the temporary recovery LaunchAgent plus macOS smoke qualification. One new cross-section contradiction was found: §2.1's stopDaemon Returns describes outcome classification as only \"graceful or forced,\" but §3.2's own Exit type defines a third kind, `failed`, with no indication of how stopDaemon surfaces (or excludes) that outcome. Beyond this, I rely on the prior substantive chunks' findings (c2 contracts, c4 diagrams, c6 migration, c7 alternatives, c9 citations) for the error-code/enum gaps, sequence-diagram message gaps, migration rollback asymmetry, and the c10 mis-citation already on record — I did not re-raise those under new ids. However, the checkpoint shows the upstream c3 (data-model) and c5 (error-paths/test-strategy) chunks themselves ended with status \"incomplete\" and self-reported missing required coverage (e.g. /body/dataModelChanges, /body/interactionWithShared, /body/errorPaths, /body/testStrategy not reported), so this final pass cannot certify that those two sections were fully reconciled against the ISSUE and the rest of the document from upstream review alone; I inspected those sections directly in the live LLD myself but could not substitute for the missing upstream substantive coverage.","findings":[{"id":"c10f1","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §2.1 stopDaemon Returns vs §3.2 protocol Exit type","message":"stopDaemon's Returns description classifies the outcome as only 'graceful or forced' ('Exit of the exact original daemon, classified as graceful or forced'), but the data model's own wire-protocol Exit type (§3.2: 'Exit { kind: graceful | forced | failed, code: number | null, signal: number | null }') defines a third kind, `failed`. The LLD never states whether a `failed` exit (e.g. the child exits with a crash/error status that is neither a clean graceful exit nor an escalation-caused forced exit) is folded into stopDaemon's 'forced' classification, surfaced as some fourth StopOutcome variant, or instead routed to one of the three documented LifecycleError rejections. This is a direct contradiction between the function contract (§2) and the data model it is built on (§3.2), left unresolved for implementation.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:42 (§2.1 Returns: 'classified as graceful or forced')","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:101 (§3.2 protocol: 'Exit { kind: graceful | forced | failed, ... }')"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/tmp/design-review/macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld-fresh.json","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","LLD.md §1 HLD context","LLD.md §2 Contract details (2.1 stopDaemon, 2.2 armShutdownWatchdog)","LLD.md §3 Data model changes (3.1-3.3)","LLD.md §4 Diagrams","LLD.md §5 Error paths (error cases, edge cases, invariants)","LLD.md §6 Test strategy","LLD.md §7 Migration","LLD.md §8 Alternatives considered","LLD.md §9 References","checkpoint completedChunks c1-summary-context (status passed)","checkpoint completedChunks c2-contracts (status changes_requested, 4 findings)","checkpoint completedChunks c3-data-model (status incomplete)","checkpoint completedChunks c4-diagrams (status incomplete, 2 findings re sequence-diagram messages)","checkpoint completedChunks c5-error-test (status incomplete)","checkpoint completedChunks c6-migration (status changes_requested, 2 findings)","checkpoint completedChunks c7-alternatives (status changes_requested, 2 findings)","checkpoint completedChunks c9-citations (status incomplete, 1 finding re [[c10]] mis-citation)"],"limitations":["The checkpoint file (/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/tmp/design-review/macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld-fresh.json) is a single ~100KB minified JSON line; line-based read tools cannot paginate within it, so some nested finding text in earlier chunks could only be recovered via targeted pattern extraction and may be incomplete for chunks whose content did not surface in that extraction.","Upstream chunk c3-data-model ended with status 'incomplete' and self-reported missing required coverage for /body/dataModelChanges and /body/interactionWithShared, so I cannot confirm from upstream review that the data-model section (§3) was fully substantively checked for internal consistency and ISSUE alignment; I inspected §3 directly myself but could not substitute full upstream reconciliation.","Upstream chunk c5-error-test ended with status 'incomplete' and self-reported missing required coverage for /body/errorPaths and /body/testStrategy, so the same gap applies to confirmation that §5 and §6 were fully substantively reviewed upstream; I inspected both sections directly myself but could not substitute full upstream reconciliation.","I did not independently render the sequence-diagram.html companion artifact to visually verify it against the textual sequenceDefinition; I relied on the LLD's §4 reference and on chunk c4's (itself 'incomplete') textual findings about missing/out-of-order messages."],"advice":["The still-open findings from prior chunks — c2 (missing LifecycleErrorCode mapping for 'no active generation' and for error case 4; undocumented `busy` variant in stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog Errors lists; LifecycleError type shape never defined), c4 (sequence diagram omits the ready/publication handshake around messages 8-9, and the ordering around message 2-3 doesn't match armShutdownWatchdog's postcondition), c6 (migration step 2's exit-evidence mechanism for a legacy direct process is unnamed, and step 4's rollback only reverses step 3, leaving step 1/2 with no described rollback), c7 (a2's reasonRejected doesn't tie back to the ISSUE's Mach-task-right root cause; openQuestions is empty despite at least two unresolved operational questions in §7/§3.1), and c9 ([[c10]] darwin.c is mis-cited in §3.3 as support for the Linux pidfd broker) — remain unresolved in the current document and should be addressed together with the StopOutcome/Exit.kind gap raised here before re-review.","When resolving the StopOutcome vs Exit.kind gap, consider whether a `failed` exit should map to one of the existing LifecycleError codes (e.g. exit-unconfirmed) or whether StopOutcome needs an explicit third classification; either choice should be stated once and kept consistent between §2.1 and §3.2."]}],"progress":{"status":"incomplete","phase":"complete","completedChunks":9,"totalChunks":9,"lastMeaningfulActivityAt":null,"activeToolCount":0,"requiresUserInput":false,"checkpointPath":"/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/tmp/design-review/macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld-fresh.json","sequence":0,"reviewElapsedMs":0,"unitElapsedMs":null,"observedAt":"2026-11-10T10:01:10.826Z","freshness":"stored"},"startedAt":"2026-11-10T09:32:46.509Z","reviewer":"daemon","model":"claude-sonnet-5","unit":{"unitId":"c10-final-consistency","phase":"chunk_review","executionId":"3c85c68d-b24b-4661-b644-4d3bb43dd4ed","dispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[]},"plan":{"chunks":[{"id":"c1-summary-context","title":"Summary and HLD/standalone framing vs ISSUE scope","selectors":["/body/summary","/body/hldContextSlice","document:line:24"],"concerns":["Does the summary and framing state the fix intent from the originating ISSUE (supported same-user macOS lifecycle, no debugger task right, bounded recovery, verified exit, guarded ownership)?","Is the standalone/no-parent-HLD framing accurate and consistent with the rest of the document (no fabricated HLD contracts)?","Scope and non-goals: does the framing correctly bound the design to macOS lifecycle while explicitly preserving Linux behavior?"],"dependsOn":[],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"c2-contracts","title":"stopDaemon and armShutdownWatchdog contract details","selectors":["/body/contractDetails"],"concerns":["Are parameters, return types, error discriminants and pre/postconditions internally consistent and fully specified for stopDaemon and armShutdownWatchdog?","Do the preconditions correctly state that managed Darwin never calls captureStopTarget or the native process broker, matching the ISSUE's root cause (Mach task right denial)?","Error handling completeness: do the three LifecycleError variants per function cover the failure modes described in error paths, and are they traceable to citations [[c3]] [[c4]] [[c6]] [[c7]] [[c8]] [[c9]] [[c10]]?","Are the cited call sites plausible for the described responsibilities given repository conventions (src/cli/services, src/daemon)?"],"dependsOn":["c1-summary-context"],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"c3-data-model","title":"Data model changes: supervisor, protocol, run-file ownership invariant","selectors":["/body/dataModelChanges","/body/interactionWithShared"],"concerns":["Ownership/cardinality: is 'one supervisor owns at most one unreaped child, one generation' consistently enforced across the entity description, protocol schema and call sites?","Protocol contract: do Op/State/Exit/LifecycleErrorCode enumerations in the schema diff match the error codes used in contractDetails and errorPaths (no drift)?","Is the invariant-change to PID/socket run files (guard still in force, only proven-dead generation's files removable) consistent with the preserved compatibility claim for daemon.status/daemon.shutdown clients?","interactionWithShared is empty — is that correct for a standalone design with no HLD shared contracts, or does it hide an undocumented cross-cutting dependency?","Are call sites (src/cli/services/daemon.ts, maintenance.ts, insrc-daemon-control.ts, daemon-ctl.sh, run-file-ownership.ts, shutdown.ts, shutdown-watchdog.ts, lifecycle.ts, daemon-process-client.ts) consistent with the repo's actual module responsibilities and naming conventions?"],"dependsOn":["c2-contracts"],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"c4-diagrams","title":"Sequence diagram definition, participants, and companion artifact","selectors":["/body/sequenceDefinition","/body/companions","document:line:125","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3"],"concerns":["Does the sequenceDefinition's message flow (bind generation, request shutdown, arm/drain, escalate, reap, reconcile run files, start replacement) match the stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog contracts and the data-model entities exactly, with no contradictions?","Is the generated companion (sequence-diagram.html) actually present, linked correctly from the Diagrams section, and does its rendering reflect the sequenceDefinition — or is this unverifiable from static inspection alone?","Are all four participants (ctl, supervisor, daemon, guard) necessary and sufficiently described, and does the diagram materially aid assessment of this interaction/dependency boundary per repo policy?"],"dependsOn":["c3-data-model"],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"c5-error-test","title":"Error paths, edge cases, invariants, and test strategy coverage","selectors":["/body/errorPaths","/body/testStrategy"],"concerns":["Do the four error cases and their detection/response/user-impact map 1:1 onto the LifecycleError codes defined in contractDetails and the protocol's LifecycleErrorCode enum, with no orphaned or missing codes?","Do the edge cases (concurrent stop/restart, timeout races, legacy migration, delayed-controller ownership-conflict) correspond to concrete test subjects in testStrategy's unit/integration/smoke levels, or are any left unaddressed?","Is test coverage sufficient for the ISSUE's reproduction scenario (broker denial on stop and on watchdog arm) and for the chosen design's key safety claims (never invokes Mach broker, confirmed reap before cleanup/restart, ownership-conflict on stale generation)?","Are the three 'invariants to preserve' ([[c2]]) actually upheld by the described mechanisms elsewhere in the document?"],"dependsOn":["c3-data-model"],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"c6-migration","title":"Migration plan from legacy/recovery instance to managed supervisor","selectors":["/body/migration"],"concerns":["Does the migration correctly sequence quiescing the ISSUE's stated temporary recovery LaunchAgent and any legacy direct process before admitting the new managed job, with verified exit evidence at each step?","Is each step's rollback claim feasible given the described supervisor/guard mechanisms (e.g., can step 2's abort-on-unverifiable-exit actually be realized with the tools described)?","Backward-compat claim: are daemon.status/daemon.shutdown RPC contracts and existing CLI/script commands genuinely unchanged per the contracts and data-model sections, or does the migration imply an undocumented behavior change?"],"dependsOn":["c5-error-test"],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"c7-alternatives","title":"Alternatives considered, chosen alternative, and open questions","selectors":["/body/alternativesConsidered","/body/alternativesConsidered/0","/body/alternativesConsidered/1","/body/alternativesConsidered/2","/body/chosenAlternative","/body/openQuestions"],"concerns":["Is the chosenAlternative (a1) correctly cross-referenced and are a2/a3's rejection rationales substantively correct (a2's fixed-label fencing gap, a3's inability to recover a blocked process) given the ISSUE's root cause?","Does a1 fully address the ISSUE's fix intent (graceful stop/restart without debugger task right, bounded recovery, verified exit, guarded ownership) better than the rejected alternatives, as claimed?","openQuestions is empty — for a bugfix-sized standalone LLD with this much protocol/migration complexity, is that credible, or does it suppress a real open design question (e.g., migration-abort operator recovery, multi-install-root behavior)?"],"dependsOn":["c1-summary-context"],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"c9-citations","title":"Citation accuracy and traceability to ISSUE and codebase","selectors":["/citations/0","/citations/1","/citations/2","/citations/3","/citations/4","/citations/5","/citations/6","/citations/7","/citations/8","/citations/9","/citations/10","/citations/11","/citations/12","/citations/13"],"concerns":["Does [[c1]] correctly point to the originating ISSUE.md as prior-artifact, and does [[c2]]'s quoted analyze-bundle text accurately reflect existing code behavior it is cited for (shared retained capability, run-file ownership)?","Do the code citations [[c3]]-[[c11]],[[c13]],[[c14]] resolve to real files at the stated paths in this repository, and do their cited roles match actual file responsibilities (e.g., [[c10]] darwin.c as the native Mach broker, [[c8]] as the Linux watchdog helper)?","Is [[c12]] (external Apple launchd doc) an appropriate and sufficient authority for the LaunchAgent approach, and are all citation ids used in the body ([[c1]] through [[c14]]) actually defined here with no gaps or unused entries?"],"dependsOn":["c2-contracts","c3-data-model","c4-diagrams","c5-error-test","c6-migration","c7-alternatives"],"purpose":"substantive"},{"id":"c10-final-consistency","title":"Final consistency: cross-chunk contradictions and ISSUE alignment","selectors":[],"concerns":["Does the LLD as a whole fully resolve the ISSUE's root cause and fix intent (same-user lifecycle without debugger task right; bounded recovery; verified exit; guarded PID/socket ownership; CLI+shell+daemon entry points; Linux preserved; migration from legacy/recovery job; qualification on real macOS) with no gaps?","Are there any contradictions between sections (e.g., error codes, protocol ops, participant roles, call sites) surfaced by the substantive chunks?","Is dependency/order feasibility sound across migration, data-model and contract sections taken together?"],"dependsOn":["c1-summary-context","c2-contracts","c3-data-model","c4-diagrams","c5-error-test","c6-migration","c7-alternatives","c9-citations"],"purpose":"consistency"}],"coverageExplanation":"Every inventory unit from the checkpoint is assigned to exactly the chunk(s) whose concern it substantively belongs to: document-level summary/framing units to c1; the two API methods to c2; the three data-model entities plus the empty interactionWithShared array to c3; the sequence diagram, its four participants, the companions array and the Diagrams heading anchor to c4; errorPaths and testStrategy to c5; migration to c6; alternativesConsidered (array and its three items), chosenAlternative and openQuestions to c7; all fourteen citation entries to c9. The final chunk is a whole-artifact consistency pass with no selectors, depending on every substantive chunk, satisfying the final-consistency requirement without duplicating unit coverage.","finalConsistencyChunkId":"c10-final-consistency","coverage":[{"unitId":"/body/summary","chunkIds":["c1-summary-context"]},{"unitId":"/body/hldContextSlice","chunkIds":["c1-summary-context"]},{"unitId":"document:line:24","chunkIds":["c1-summary-context"]},{"unitId":"/body/contractDetails","chunkIds":["c2-contracts"]},{"unitId":"/body/dataModelChanges","chunkIds":["c3-data-model"]},{"unitId":"/body/interactionWithShared","chunkIds":["c3-data-model"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition","chunkIds":["c4-diagrams"]},{"unitId":"/body/companions","chunkIds":["c4-diagrams"]},{"unitId":"document:line:125","chunkIds":["c4-diagrams"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","chunkIds":["c4-diagrams"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","chunkIds":["c4-diagrams"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","chunkIds":["c4-diagrams"]},{"unitId":"/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","chunkIds":["c4-diagrams"]},{"unitId":"/body/errorPaths","chunkIds":["c5-error-test"]},{"unitId":"/body/testStrategy","chunkIds":["c5-error-test"]},{"unitId":"/body/migration","chunkIds":["c6-migration"]},{"unitId":"/body/alternativesConsidered","chunkIds":["c7-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/alternativesConsidered/0","chunkIds":["c7-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/alternativesConsidered/1","chunkIds":["c7-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/alternativesConsidered/2","chunkIds":["c7-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/chosenAlternative","chunkIds":["c7-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/body/openQuestions","chunkIds":["c7-alternatives"]},{"unitId":"/citations/0","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/1","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/2","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/3","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/4","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/5","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/6","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/7","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/8","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/9","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/10","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/11","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/12","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]},{"unitId":"/citations/13","chunkIds":["c9-citations"]}]},"unitSummaries":[{"unitId":"planning","phase":"planning","automaticDispatches":3,"retriesUsed":2,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Unknown/duplicate selectors in c-summary-context"},{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Unknown/duplicate selectors in c1-issue-trace"}],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c1-summary-context","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c2-contracts","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c3-data-model","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":2,"retriesUsed":1,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Status contradicts findings or mandatory verification limits: received passed, expected incomplete; limitations=1, HIGH/MED=0"}],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c4-diagrams","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":2,"retriesUsed":1,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Status contradicts findings or mandatory verification limits: received passed, expected incomplete; limitations=1, HIGH/MED=0"}],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c5-error-test","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c6-migration","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c7-alternatives","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":2,"retriesUsed":1,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Finding id f1 is already used"}],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c9-citations","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":2,"retriesUsed":1,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Status contradicts findings or mandatory verification limits: received changes_requested, expected incomplete; limitations=1, HIGH/MED=1"}],"actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"c10-final-consistency","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":1,"retriesUsed":0,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[],"actionIds":[]}],"report":{"reviewId":"macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld-fresh","attempt":1,"artifactId":"LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1","status":"incomplete","counts":{"HIGH":0,"MED":9,"LOW":5},"chunks":[{"chunkId":"c1-summary-context","status":"passed","summary":"The Summary and §1 HLD context framing accurately reflect a standalone, no-parent-HLD design and correctly bound the fix to macOS lifecycle while preserving Linux behavior. The ISSUE's fix intent (same-user lifecycle avoiding the native task-right broker, bounded recovery, verified exit, guarded ownership, CLI/shell/daemon coverage, Linux preservation, migration evaluation) is traceable to this document, though most of the detail lives in later sections (contracts, invariants, migration) rather than being restated in the two-paragraph Summary — which is normal for an LLD summary and not a defect. No fabricated HLD contracts are present; §1 explicitly disclaims any parent HLD/shared contracts.","findings":[],"inspectedReferences":["/body/summary","/body/hldContextSlice","document:line:24","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md"],"limitations":[],"advice":["The Summary's `**Epic:** restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos` field label sits immediately above `**Design provenance:** Standalone issue design.` and `This standalone design has no parent HLD.` The field is evidently reusing the epic-slug/identifier namespace rather than asserting a real Epic/HLD exists (consistent with the sibling EPIC-3992d2e1e26124dc artifact used purely as an identifier wrapper for the standalone issue), but a reader skimming only the Summary could momentarily read 'Epic:' as implying Epic-level ancestry before reaching the very next line that disclaims it. Consider relabeling that field (e.g. 'Scope id:' or 'Standalone id:') to remove the ambiguity, though this is cosmetic and not a traceability or contract defect.","The Summary does not explicitly restate the ISSUE's specific trigger ('without requiring a debugger task right' / Mach task-right denial) — it is only made concrete later in §2.1's precondition ('never calls captureStopTarget or the native process broker') and §9's citations. Readers relying on the Summary alone would not see the causal link to the original denial; an optional one-clause addition to the Summary (e.g. 'avoiding the native task-right broker that macOS denies') would tighten the traceability without changing scope."]},{"chunkId":"c2-contracts","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog contracts in §2 are internally plausible and the preconditions correctly state that managed Darwin never calls captureStopTarget or the native process broker, matching the ISSUE's root-cause (Mach task-right denial via src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts and src/native/process-broker/darwin.c). Citations [[c3]][[c4]][[c6]][[c7]][[c8]][[c9]][[c10]] point to plausible call sites given repo conventions (src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts and daemon.ts host the current stopDaemon/capture logic; src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts and src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts are the watchdog paths being replaced; darwin.c is the native broker). However, the three-variant LifecycleError sets for each function do not fully cover the failure modes the document itself describes: a documented error case (no usable launchd domain/runtime) and a documented wire-level error code (`busy`) have no corresponding contract-level discriminant, and the `LifecycleError<Code>` type's own shape (fields beyond the discriminant) is never specified.","findings":[{"id":"c2-f1","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §2.1 stopDaemon Errors / §5 Error paths case 2","message":"stopDaemon's three LifecycleError variants (ownership-conflict, exit-unconfirmed, supervisor-unavailable) do not account for the case where there is no active generation to stop at all (nothing running). The current real implementation (src/cli/services/daemon.ts:159, stopDaemonWith) throws a distinct 'daemon is not running' error for exactly this case, and §3.2's own protocol says 'stop requires the exact active generation' without stating what happens when none exists. It is unspecified whether that maps to ownership-conflict, a new code, or something else, leaving the contract incomplete for a real, currently-handled failure mode.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:44-47","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:105","src/cli/services/daemon.ts:156-159"]},{"id":"c2-f2","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §5 Error paths case 4 vs §2 contractDetails and §3.2 LifecycleErrorCode enum","message":"Error-paths case 4 ('There is no usable launchd GUI domain or daemon runtime') is a documented recoverable failure with its own detection/response/user-impact, but it has no corresponding LifecycleErrorCode in the §3.2 protocol enum and no function contract anywhere in §2 that could surface it (it belongs to neither stopDaemon nor armShutdownWatchdog). The error-handling surface for this case is therefore entirely unspecified at the contract level despite being called out as a first-class error path.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:145-148","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:102-103"]},{"id":"c2-f3","severity":"LOW","location":"S001/LLD.md §3.2 protocol LifecycleErrorCode enum vs §2.1/§2.2 Errors lists","message":"The wire protocol's LifecycleErrorCode union includes `busy` ('Bound pending requests and reject excess as busy'), stated generically for both the controller and inherited channels, but neither stopDaemon's nor armShutdownWatchdog's documented Errors list includes a busy variant, and the text never clarifies whether a controller `stop` call (which stopDaemon issues) can ever observe `busy`.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:102-107","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:44-47","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:66-69"]},{"id":"c2-f4","severity":"LOW","location":"S001/LLD.md §2.1-2.2 Errors (LifecycleError<Code> usage)","message":"Both functions' Errors sections use the generic form `LifecycleError<'code'>` as the thrown/rejected type, but the LLD never defines the LifecycleError type's own shape (e.g. whether it carries the discriminant, a message, the generation id, or diagnostic detail) anywhere in the document, leaving the error contract's concrete structure unspecified for implementation.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:44-47","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:66-69"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/contractDetails","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","src/cli/services/daemon.ts","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","src/daemon/shutdown.ts","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c (existence confirmed via glob)"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Consider explicitly stating in §2.1 what stopDaemon does when no generation is currently active (idle/exited state) — either a fourth discriminant or an explicit note that this is a no-op success rather than an error.","Consider adding a LifecycleErrorCode (or explicitly stating out-of-scope) for the launchd-domain-unavailable case in §5 so every documented error path has a contract-level home.","A short inline type sketch for LifecycleError<Code> (e.g. { code: Code; message: string; generation?: string }) would remove ambiguity for the builder."]},{"chunkId":"c3-data-model","status":"incomplete","summary":"test","findings":[{"id":"test1","severity":"LOW","location":"x","message":"y","evidenceReferences":["z"]}],"inspectedReferences":["a"],"limitations":["Required coverage not reported: /body/dataModelChanges","Required coverage not reported: /body/interactionWithShared","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md"],"advice":[]},{"chunkId":"c4-diagrams","status":"incomplete","summary":"The sequenceDefinition's 10 messages and 4 participants are reproduced byte-for-byte in the generated companion sequence-diagram.html (a valid mermaid sequenceDiagram block), and the Diagrams section at LLD.md:127 links to the exact companions[0].relPath with the matching title. The shutdown/teardown half of the flow (bind generation, drain, escalate, reap, reconcile run files, return exit) is fully consistent with the stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog contracts and the 3.1-3.3 data model. Two gaps were found in the startup half of the flow and in the watchdog-arm message's directionality. I could not execute or render the HTML in a browser/headless renderer, so visual correctness beyond the embedded source text is not confirmed — this is reported as a limitation rather than a pass.","findings":[{"id":"f1-startup-ready-guard-gap","severity":"MED","location":"/body/sequenceDefinition/messages/8-9 (\"Start replacement generation\", \"Spawn and bind new child\"); LLD.md ## 3.1 (lines 79-89) and ## 3.2 protocol block (lines 94-108)","message":"The new-generation startup portion of the sequence diagram is compressed to two messages (ctl->supervisor 'Start replacement generation', supervisor->daemon 'Spawn and bind new child') and stops there. It omits the child's 'ready' handshake back to the supervisor and the run-file guard's publication of the new PID/socket pair, even though data model 3.1 states the supervisor 'binds the daemon identity, a fresh generation identifier and the owned PID/socket publication before advertising ready', and 3.2's protocol explicitly defines 'ready' as an inherited-channel op and says start 'Return[s] starting until child readiness and owned run-file publication are verified.' The teardown half of the same diagram shows the equivalent guard interaction explicitly (message 7, 'Reconcile owned run files', supervisor->guard), so the two halves of the lifecycle are documented at different levels of detail and the diagram does not fully trace the startup contract onto the 'guard' participant it declares.","evidenceReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition.messages[8]","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition.messages[9]","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.dataModelChanges[0].details","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:81","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:105-106","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html:110-111"]},{"id":"f2-watchdog-arm-direction-ambiguous","severity":"LOW","location":"/body/sequenceDefinition/messages/2 (\"Confirm watchdog readiness\", daemon->supervisor); LLD.md ## 2.2 postcondition (line 75) and ## 3.2 protocol block (lines 105-106)","message":"The diagram's third message is daemon->supervisor labelled 'Confirm watchdog readiness', immediately followed by daemon->daemon 'Drain and close stores' with no intervening supervisor->daemon message. But armShutdownWatchdog's postcondition (2.2, line 75) says 'The daemon enters its existing drain only after receiving an acknowledgement for its own generation', and the protocol (3.2) models this as the child sending 'arm' and the supervisor replying with an admitted-deadline acknowledgement on the same inherited channel. As drawn, the single daemon->supervisor arrow reads as the daemon confirming readiness to the supervisor rather than the daemon requesting an arm and waiting on the supervisor's ack before draining, so the diagram's directionality does not visibly carry the 'receiving an acknowledgement' precondition for drain.","evidenceReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition.messages[2]","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition.messages[3]","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:75","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:106","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html:103-105"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/sequenceDefinition","/body/companions","document:line:125","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/0","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/1","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/2","/body/sequenceDefinition/participants/3","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.sequenceDefinition","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.companions","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.dataModelChanges","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html:96-111","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md"],"limitations":["Could not render docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html in a browser or headless renderer to confirm the mermaid diagram actually renders without error or truncation; no browser/screenshot tool was available in this review session. Verification was limited to confirming the embedded mermaid 'sequenceDiagram' source text (sequence-diagram.html:96-111) is byte-for-byte consistent with body.sequenceDefinition's participants and messages in the canonical artifact — this is source-level, not rendered-output, verification."],"advice":["Add the child's 'ready' handshake and the run-file guard's publication of the new PID/socket pair to the sequenceDefinition's startup messages, so the new-generation half of the flow carries the same level of guard/readiness detail as the teardown half and visibly satisfies data model 3.1/3.2's 'owned PID/socket publication before advertising ready' requirement.","Consider adding an explicit supervisor->daemon acknowledgement message after 'Confirm watchdog readiness' (or renaming/re-pointing that message to read as the daemon's arm request), so the diagram visibly carries the 'daemon enters drain only after receiving an acknowledgement' precondition from contract 2.2 rather than relying on an implicit synchronous-call convention.","When a rendering/screenshot capability is available, capture an actual render of sequence-diagram.html to close the visual-verification gap noted in limitations."]},{"chunkId":"c5-error-test","status":"incomplete","summary":"test","findings":[{"id":"f1","severity":"MED","location":"a","message":"b","evidenceReferences":["c"]}],"inspectedReferences":["a","b"],"limitations":["Required coverage not reported: /body/errorPaths","Required coverage not reported: /body/testStrategy","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md"],"advice":[]},{"chunkId":"c6-migration","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The migration plan correctly sequences quiescing the recovery LaunchAgent / legacy process before admitting the managed job, and the backward-compat claim for daemon.status/daemon.shutdown RPC contracts and existing CLI/script commands is supported by the current code (rpc('daemon.status')/rpc('daemon.shutdown') call sites in src/cli/services/daemon.ts, and the already-existing src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts + scripts/daemon-ctl.sh entry points). However, Step 2 is marked rollbackable but no rollback procedure is actually given for it: the only rollback described (Step 4) reverses Step 3 (stop the managed generation, unload the job, restore the previous installation) and never addresses resurrecting or re-verifying the quiesced legacy/recovery instance. Issuing cooperative shutdown to the legacy instance in Step 2 is also not cleanly reversible once sent, which conflicts with marking the step \"↩ rollbackable\" the same way as Steps 1 and 3 (which take no irreversible action against a live process). Additionally, the migration's \"verify its exit\" step for a legacy direct process does not state which exit-evidence mechanism it uses; the ISSUE's root cause is specifically that the native process-capability broker (captureStopTarget / src/native/process-broker/darwin.c) is denied on this exact scenario, and the LLD elsewhere (§3.3) explicitly bars the new managed Darwin path from falling back to that broker, but never extends that same no-broker guarantee to the migration's legacy-process verification step, leaving the mechanism for this specific verification ambiguous at the one point where the original bug would otherwise reproduce.","findings":[{"id":"f1-migration-rollback-gap","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §7 Migration, Steps 2 and 4","message":"Step 2 (quiesce the old instance / recovery LaunchAgent or legacy direct process) is marked '↩ rollbackable', but Step 4 — the document's only described rollback procedure — only reverses Step 3 (stop the managed generation, unload the owned job, restore the previous installation). No step describes how to resurrect or recover the quiesced legacy/recovery instance if migration is aborted after Step 2 but before Step 3 succeeds, or what state the operator is left in. The error-path table's own guidance ('a stuck or unverifiable legacy process requires operator recovery') suggests this is not actually a clean rollback, but the Step-2 label does not say so or point to that fallback.","evidenceReferences":["S001/LLD.md:189-193 (Migration Steps 1-4)","S001/LLD.md:156 (edge-case row: 'A legacy or temporary recovery instance is live... Require explicit operator recovery if a legacy instance is stuck.')"]},{"id":"f2-migration-broker-ambiguity","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §7 Migration, Step 2 vs §3.3","message":"Step 2's verification of a legacy direct process's exit ('request cooperative shutdown and verify its exit... before admitting the managed job') does not name the exit-evidence mechanism. The ISSUE's root cause is exactly that the native process-capability broker (src/native/process-broker/darwin.c, invoked via captureStopTarget in src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts) is denied for this same-user Darwin scenario. Section 3.3 explicitly states the new managed Darwin controller/watchdog 'never call[s] the Darwin Mach broker and no automatic fallback to it', but that guarantee is stated only for the already-managed path, not for the migration's one-time legacy-process exit check — the one place where reusing the broken broker would silently reproduce the bug being fixed.","evidenceReferences":["S001/LLD.md:190 (Step 2)","S001/LLD.md:118 (§3.3 'no call to the Darwin Mach broker and no automatic fallback to it')","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts:32,61-80 (BROKER constant, native broker spawn)","docs/standalone/.../ISSUE.md:9-11 (root cause: Darwin Mach task-right denial)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/migration","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/cli/services/daemon.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/src/shared/daemon-process.ts","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/scripts/daemon-ctl.sh"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Name the specific exit-verification mechanism used in Migration Step 2 for a legacy (non-supervisor-child) process — e.g. PID-liveness polling via readProcessIdentity/ps (src/shared/daemon-process.ts), which already avoids the native broker — so the migration step's feasibility is traceable rather than implied.","Either give Step 2 an explicit rollback/recovery procedure (even if it is simply 'treat as the stuck-legacy-process error path and require operator recovery') or drop the '↩ rollbackable' marker from Step 2 to avoid implying symmetry with Steps 1 and 3, which take no irreversible action."]},{"chunkId":"c7-alternatives","status":"changes_requested","summary":"chosenAlternative (a1) is correctly cross-referenced to section 8.1's CHOSEN marker and substantively satisfies the ISSUE's fix intent (no Mach task right, bounded recovery via independent supervisor deadline, verified exit before replacement, guarded PID/socket ownership, migration from the legacy/temporary-recovery instance). a3's rejection is well-grounded in the fix intent's \"bounded recovery when hung\" requirement. a2's rejection is defensible but its stated rationale (instance fencing) understates that the same root-cause native-broker denial likely recurs for IDE/signal-initiated (watchdog) stops under a2, which is a stronger reason than fencing alone. The empty openQuestions list is not fully credible given unresolved operational gaps this design itself surfaces: an \"abort migration... requires operator recovery\" step with no defined recovery procedure, and a data model that scopes one supervisor \"per installed state root\" without addressing concurrent multi-install-root behavior.","findings":[{"id":"c7alt-a2root","severity":"LOW","location":"/body/alternativesConsidered/1 (a2 reasonRejected); LLD.md section 8.2","message":"a2's reasonRejected ('Ordinary stop is plausible but fixed-label forced recovery needs additional instance fencing') frames the rejection as a fencing/safety gap rather than tying it to the ISSUE's actual root cause. The listed con itself says 'a separate bounded watchdog still needs authority to stop the original instance when shutdown was initiated by an IDE or signal' — i.e. under a2 the watchdog-initiated (forced) stop path would still depend on the same native capability whose denial is the ISSUE's root cause (Mach task right acquisition failing with errno 1). The rationale should say explicitly that a2 only moves the problem for ordinary stop and leaves the root-cause broker dependency unresolved for forced/watchdog-initiated stops, not merely that it 'needs fencing'.","evidenceReferences":["/body/alternativesConsidered/1","ISSUE.md Root cause: 'Both the CLI stop path and the daemon's independent shutdown watchdog require the same native retained process capability... A permission denial is reported... The daemon itself also arms the watchdog before stopping background work, so capture failure triggers emergency exit before the graceful drain.'","LLD.md section 8.2"]},{"id":"c7alt-openq","severity":"MED","location":"/body/openQuestions; LLD.md section 7 (Migration) step 2 and section 3.1","message":"openQuestions is empty, but the design itself surfaces at least two unresolved operational questions that this bugfix-sized LLD does not answer: (1) Migration step 2 states 'Abort migration when that evidence is unavailable... A stuck or unverifiable legacy process requires operator recovery under the error-path policy' but no error-path case or section defines what that operator recovery procedure actually is (manual kill? reinstall? documented runbook?) — this is left entirely unspecified. (2) Section 3.1 models 'One per-user job per installed state root' implying more than one installed state root can exist on a machine (e.g. dev vs. production install, or multiple Node/installation roots), yet neither the contract details, data model, nor test strategy address what happens when two installed state roots' supervisors or run-file guards interact (collision, isolation guarantees, or explicit non-goal). Given the protocol and migration complexity already present in this LLD, suppressing both as non-issues via an empty openQuestions list is not credible; at least one should be recorded as open (or explicitly scoped out as a non-goal) rather than silently assumed.","evidenceReferences":["/body/openQuestions","LLD.md section 7 step 2: 'A stuck or unverifiable legacy process requires operator recovery under the error-path policy.'","LLD.md section 3.1: 'One per-user job per installed state root hosts one supervisor...'","LLD.md section 5 error paths (no case matches 'operator recovery' procedure)"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/body/alternativesConsidered","/body/alternativesConsidered/0","/body/alternativesConsidered/1","/body/alternativesConsidered/2","/body/chosenAlternative","/body/openQuestions","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.alternativesConsidered","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.chosenAlternative","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/artifacts/LLD-3992d2e1e26124dc-s1.json#body.openQuestions","LLD.md section 2 (Contract details, 2.1/2.2) for cross-check against a1's claimed pros","LLD.md section 3 (Data model changes) for cross-check against a1's claimed pros and a2/a3's cons","LLD.md section 7 (Migration) for cross-check of a1's migration-path con and the fix intent's migration-evaluation requirement","LLD.md section 5 (Error paths) for cross-check of bounded-recovery claims used to reject a3"],"limitations":[],"advice":["Consider recording at least one explicit open question (e.g. the operator-recovery procedure for a stuck legacy instance during migration, or multi-install-root isolation) rather than leaving openQuestions empty — even if the answer is 'out of scope for this bugfix', stating that explicitly is more defensible than silence.","Strengthen a2's reasonRejected to name the recurring native-broker/Mach-task-right dependency for watchdog-initiated stops directly, so the rejection traces unambiguously back to the ISSUE's root cause rather than reading as a generic fencing concern."]},{"chunkId":"c9-citations","status":"incomplete","summary":"Eleven of fourteen citations resolve to real files whose cited roles match actual responsibilities, and [[c1]]/[[c12]] are appropriately used. However, §3.3 mis-cites [[c10]] (darwin.c, the Darwin Mach-port broker) in support of the \"native pidfd broker\" claim, when the actual Linux pidfd implementation lives in a separate, uncited file (src/native/process-broker/linux.c). Two citations ([[c2]]'s analyze-bundle quote and [[c12]]'s external URL) could not be fully verified against their original sources, so the result is incomplete per the precedence rule even though it also carries a MED finding.","findings":[{"id":"c9-cite-linux-broker-mismatch","severity":"MED","location":"docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:118 (§3.3, 'PID and socket run files')","message":"Section 3.3 states 'Linux keeps captureStopTarget, the native pidfd broker and the standalone watchdog helper [[c6]] [[c8]] [[c9]] [[c10]]' and cites [[c10]] as support for the Linux pidfd broker. But [[c10]] is defined in References as src/native/process-broker/darwin.c, which is exclusively the Darwin Mach-task broker (uses mach_port_t, task_for_pid, bsm/libbsm.h; no pidfd logic). The actual Linux pidfd implementation is a separate file, src/native/process-broker/linux.c (struct linux_capability { int fd; }, uses poll()/syscall pidfd calls), which exists in the repo but is never cited anywhere in the LLD's References. The same [[c10]] citation is used correctly elsewhere (§2.1, §2.2) to describe the Darwin-specific broker being avoided by the managed path, so the §3.3 reuse for the Linux broker is an internal inconsistency, not a one-off typo across the whole doc.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:118","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:231","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c:1-20","src/native/process-broker/linux.c:1-30"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/citations/0","/citations/1","/citations/2","/citations/3","/citations/4","/citations/5","/citations/6","/citations/7","/citations/8","/citations/9","/citations/10","/citations/11","/citations/12","/citations/13","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts","src/cli/services/daemon.ts","src/daemon/shutdown.ts","src/daemon/shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/daemon/run-file-ownership.ts","src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts","src/shared/daemon-process-client.ts","src/native/process-broker/darwin.c","src/native/process-broker/linux.c","scripts/daemon-ctl.sh","src/bin/insrc-daemon-control.ts","src/cli/services/maintenance.ts"],"limitations":["Could not retrieve or cross-check the original s1:context.assemble analyze-bundle artifact itself to confirm [[c2]]'s quoted text ('The CLI lifecycle and independent watchdog share the retained capability. Run-file ownership guards publication and cleanup.') is a verbatim excerpt of that bundle; only assessed that the quote's substance matches current code behavior (captureStopTarget shared by lifecycle.ts and the watchdog helper; run-file-ownership.ts guarding publication/cleanup).","Did not fetch the live external URL for [[c12]] (https://developer.apple.com/.../CreatingLaunchdJobs.html) to confirm it currently resolves and covers per-user LaunchAgent job creation; assessed only topical appropriateness of an Apple launchd document as authority for the LaunchAgent approach."],"advice":["Add a dedicated citation for src/native/process-broker/linux.c and use it (instead of [[c10]]) wherever the LLD specifically describes the Linux pidfd broker, reserving [[c10]] for Darwin-Mach-broker-specific claims.","Consider qualifying [[c8]] (src/bin/insrc-shutdown-watchdog.ts)'s description as 'the Linux watchdog helper' / 'the standalone watchdog helper' with a note that the file itself is currently platform-agnostic (no Darwin/Linux branch) and only becomes Linux-exclusive once the managed-Darwin path in this design stops invoking it — the file's present form doesn't yet encode that restriction."]},{"chunkId":"c10-final-consistency","status":"incomplete","summary":"The LLD, taken as a whole, substantively resolves the ISSUE's root cause and fix intent: it replaces the native Mach-task-right/broker dependency with a per-user LaunchAgent supervisor that obtains exit evidence via waitpid on its direct child, preserves bounded recovery (deadline/escalation), requires verified exit before replacement, keeps the existing PID/socket ownership guard, covers CLI/shell/TUI/daemon-direct entry points, explicitly preserves Linux's pidfd path, and includes a migration plan from the temporary recovery LaunchAgent plus macOS smoke qualification. One new cross-section contradiction was found: §2.1's stopDaemon Returns describes outcome classification as only \"graceful or forced,\" but §3.2's own Exit type defines a third kind, `failed`, with no indication of how stopDaemon surfaces (or excludes) that outcome. Beyond this, I rely on the prior substantive chunks' findings (c2 contracts, c4 diagrams, c6 migration, c7 alternatives, c9 citations) for the error-code/enum gaps, sequence-diagram message gaps, migration rollback asymmetry, and the c10 mis-citation already on record — I did not re-raise those under new ids. However, the checkpoint shows the upstream c3 (data-model) and c5 (error-paths/test-strategy) chunks themselves ended with status \"incomplete\" and self-reported missing required coverage (e.g. /body/dataModelChanges, /body/interactionWithShared, /body/errorPaths, /body/testStrategy not reported), so this final pass cannot certify that those two sections were fully reconciled against the ISSUE and the rest of the document from upstream review alone; I inspected those sections directly in the live LLD myself but could not substitute for the missing upstream substantive coverage.","findings":[{"id":"c10f1","severity":"MED","location":"S001/LLD.md §2.1 stopDaemon Returns vs §3.2 protocol Exit type","message":"stopDaemon's Returns description classifies the outcome as only 'graceful or forced' ('Exit of the exact original daemon, classified as graceful or forced'), but the data model's own wire-protocol Exit type (§3.2: 'Exit { kind: graceful | forced | failed, code: number | null, signal: number | null }') defines a third kind, `failed`. The LLD never states whether a `failed` exit (e.g. the child exits with a crash/error status that is neither a clean graceful exit nor an escalation-caused forced exit) is folded into stopDaemon's 'forced' classification, surfaced as some fourth StopOutcome variant, or instead routed to one of the three documented LifecycleError rejections. This is a direct contradiction between the function contract (§2) and the data model it is built on (§3.2), left unresolved for implementation.","evidenceReferences":["docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:42 (§2.1 Returns: 'classified as graceful or forced')","docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md:101 (§3.2 protocol: 'Exit { kind: graceful | forced | failed, ... }')"]}],"inspectedReferences":["/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/tmp/design-review/macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld-fresh.json","/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/ISSUE.md","LLD.md §1 HLD context","LLD.md §2 Contract details (2.1 stopDaemon, 2.2 armShutdownWatchdog)","LLD.md §3 Data model changes (3.1-3.3)","LLD.md §4 Diagrams","LLD.md §5 Error paths (error cases, edge cases, invariants)","LLD.md §6 Test strategy","LLD.md §7 Migration","LLD.md §8 Alternatives considered","LLD.md §9 References","checkpoint completedChunks c1-summary-context (status passed)","checkpoint completedChunks c2-contracts (status changes_requested, 4 findings)","checkpoint completedChunks c3-data-model (status incomplete)","checkpoint completedChunks c4-diagrams (status incomplete, 2 findings re sequence-diagram messages)","checkpoint completedChunks c5-error-test (status incomplete)","checkpoint completedChunks c6-migration (status changes_requested, 2 findings)","checkpoint completedChunks c7-alternatives (status changes_requested, 2 findings)","checkpoint completedChunks c9-citations (status incomplete, 1 finding re [[c10]] mis-citation)"],"limitations":["The checkpoint file (/Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/.insrc/tmp/design-review/macos-daemon-restart-3992d2e1-lld-fresh.json) is a single ~100KB minified JSON line; line-based read tools cannot paginate within it, so some nested finding text in earlier chunks could only be recovered via targeted pattern extraction and may be incomplete for chunks whose content did not surface in that extraction.","Upstream chunk c3-data-model ended with status 'incomplete' and self-reported missing required coverage for /body/dataModelChanges and /body/interactionWithShared, so I cannot confirm from upstream review that the data-model section (§3) was fully substantively checked for internal consistency and ISSUE alignment; I inspected §3 directly myself but could not substitute full upstream reconciliation.","Upstream chunk c5-error-test ended with status 'incomplete' and self-reported missing required coverage for /body/errorPaths and /body/testStrategy, so the same gap applies to confirmation that §5 and §6 were fully substantively reviewed upstream; I inspected both sections directly myself but could not substitute full upstream reconciliation.","I did not independently render the sequence-diagram.html companion artifact to visually verify it against the textual sequenceDefinition; I relied on the LLD's §4 reference and on chunk c4's (itself 'incomplete') textual findings about missing/out-of-order messages."],"advice":["The still-open findings from prior chunks — c2 (missing LifecycleErrorCode mapping for 'no active generation' and for error case 4; undocumented `busy` variant in stopDaemon/armShutdownWatchdog Errors lists; LifecycleError type shape never defined), c4 (sequence diagram omits the ready/publication handshake around messages 8-9, and the ordering around message 2-3 doesn't match armShutdownWatchdog's postcondition), c6 (migration step 2's exit-evidence mechanism for a legacy direct process is unnamed, and step 4's rollback only reverses step 3, leaving step 1/2 with no described rollback), c7 (a2's reasonRejected doesn't tie back to the ISSUE's Mach-task-right root cause; openQuestions is empty despite at least two unresolved operational questions in §7/§3.1), and c9 ([[c10]] darwin.c is mis-cited in §3.3 as support for the Linux pidfd broker) — remain unresolved in the current document and should be addressed together with the StopOutcome/Exit.kind gap raised here before re-review.","When resolving the StopOutcome vs Exit.kind gap, consider whether a `failed` exit should map to one of the existing LifecycleError codes (e.g. exit-unconfirmed) or whether StopOutcome needs an explicit third classification; either choice should be stated once and kept consistent between §2.1 and §3.2."]}],"limitations":["Required coverage not reported: /body/dataModelChanges","Required coverage not reported: /body/interactionWithShared","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","Could not render docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/sequence-diagram.html in a browser or headless renderer to confirm the mermaid diagram actually renders without error or truncation; no browser/screenshot tool was available in this review session. Verification was limited to confirming the embedded mermaid 'sequenceDiagram' source text (sequence-diagram.html:96-111) is byte-for-byte consistent with body.sequenceDefinition's participants and messages in the canonical artifact — this is source-level, not rendered-output, verification.","Required coverage not reported: /body/errorPaths","Required coverage not reported: /body/testStrategy","Required coverage not reported: /Users/subhagho/work/projects/insors/insrc/docs/standalone/restore-reliable-daemon-restart-macos-E202611103992d2e1/S001/LLD.md","Could not retrieve or cross-check the original s1:context.assemble analyze-bundle artifact itself to confirm [[c2]]'s quoted text ('The CLI lifecycle and independent watchdog share the retained capability. Run-file ownership guards pu